{
  "last_updated": "2026-08-23",
  "preamble": "A register of questions where the documented record and a publicly-circulating counter-reading both remain in play, and open-source evidence cannot presently adjudicate between them. Entries here are not endorsements. The purpose is the opposite: to name unresolvable questions honestly instead of silently treating the absence of documentation as proof of the negative — the failure mode this project calls the October Surprise trap. An entry is added only when three conditions are met: (1) the counter-reading draws on facts the project already treats as documented; (2) non-fringe actors are asserting it publicly; (3) resolution would require evidence that is not currently in the open record. Entries are retired when new evidence moves the question into confirmed or disconfirmed territory.",
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "mecca-pact-substitute-or-supplement-august-2026",
      "title": "The Mecca Joint Defence Agreement of August 7 binds Saudi Arabia, Türkiye and Pakistan to treat an attack on one as an attack on all — is it a hedge against an American security guarantee the signatories no longer trust, or a burden-sharing supplement that Washington welcomes and that leaves US basing, overflight and command arrangements untouched?",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "FIRST LISTED — August 23: On AUGUST 7, 2026 Saudi Crown Prince MOHAMMED BIN SALMAN, Turkish President RECEP TAYYIP ERDOGAN and Pakistani Prime Minister SHEHBAZ SHARIF signed the MECCA JOINT DEFENCE AGREEMENT, declaring in a joint statement that AN ARMED ATTACK ON ANY ONE OF THE THREE WOULD BE REGARDED AS AN ATTACK ON ALL. Turkish Foreign Minister HAKAN FIDAN said the pact would function similarly to NATO, with a MINISTERS' COMMITTEE, POLITICAL AND MILITARY COMMITTEES AND A PERMANENT SECRETARIAT overseeing implementation. A Turkish official told Reuters it is 'DEFENSIVE IN NATURE AND NOT DIRECTED AT ANY SPECIFIC ACTOR' and open to other regional countries; Pakistan's foreign ministry framed it in terms of 'longstanding historical ties' and 'Islamic solidarity.' President Trump welcomed it on AUGUST 16-17 as a 'BIG, BOLD, AND IMPORTANT FIRST STEP,' saying he was 'very happy to see' it. THE READINGS SPLIT IMMEDIATELY AND HAVE NOT CONVERGED: the COUNCIL ON FOREIGN RELATIONS published 'The U.S.-Led Middle East Order Is Over. The Mecca Agreement Proves It.'; the MIDDLE EAST COUNCIL ON GLOBAL AFFAIRS asked 'What Is the Mecca Joint Defense Pact — and What It Is Not?'; the ARAB CENTER called it 'DIVERSIFICATION WITHOUT SUBSTITUTION'; OSW called it 'A LOOSE ALLIANCE.' On AUGUST 22 Iran's SNSC secretary MOHSEN REZAEI said the agreement signalled that 'THE US WAS LEAVING THE REGION.' The three signatories are the states whose territory, airspace, basing and overflight a US ground campaign against Iran would most require, which is why this question is tracked here rather than treated as regional colour.",
      "synopsis": "This matters for scoring because a substantial body of corpus predictions turns on the proposition that a long, costly American war would visibly fracture the US alliance system and accelerate a multipolar realignment. The Mecca pact is the single strongest candidate event for that prediction in the entire 178-day record: three American security partners, two of them hosting or adjacent to US forces and one a NATO member, formalising mutual defence among themselves during a war Washington is prosecuting in their neighbourhood. But the same event is equally consistent with the burden-sharing outcome successive US administrations have demanded for two decades, and the President publicly welcomed it. Scoring the alliance-fracture predictions requires knowing which it is, and the open record cannot yet say.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "August 6-7, 2026: Pakistani Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif arrived in Jeddah on August 6 and performed the Umrah pilgrimage in Mecca the following day while Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan met Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman. The Mecca Joint Defence Agreement was signed into force on August 7, 2026.",
        "The joint statement declares that an armed attack on one of the three signatories would be regarded as an attack on all three.",
        "Turkish Foreign Minister Hakan Fidan said the pact would function similarly to NATO, with a ministers' committee, political and military committees, and a permanent secretariat to oversee implementation of decisions.",
        "A Turkish official told Reuters that the pact is 'defensive in nature and not directed at any specific actor' and that it remains open to other regional countries.",
        "Pakistan's foreign ministry characterised the agreement as guided by 'longstanding historical ties' and 'Islamic solidarity,' and Pakistan publicly described it as 'purely defensive' and open to others.",
        "The signatories' complementary assets are widely described as Turkey's NATO-standard military and growing domestic defence industry, Saudi Arabia's financial resources and regional influence, and Pakistan's status as the Muslim world's only nuclear-armed state.",
        "August 16-17, 2026: President Trump welcomed the agreement, saying 'Very happy to see that Saudi Arabia, Turkey, and Pakistan have recently, and finally, signed the Mecca Joint Defence Agreement,' and calling it a 'big, bold, and important first step.' He had earlier welcomed regional partners being 'finally able to defend themselves in a more meaningful way.'",
        "Context: Saudi Arabia has absorbed repeated Iranian and Houthi missile and drone strikes since the war began on February 28, 2026, and pressed Washington for restraint on August 1 when Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman spoke with Trump about the reported energy-infrastructure strike plans.",
        "August 17, 2026: Trump threatened to 'bomb the s*** out of' Oman — a US strategic partner and the war's principal mediator — if it 'got in the way,' adding that Oman had not 'behaved very well.'",
        "August 22, 2026: Mohsen Rezaei, secretary of Iran's Supreme National Security Council, said the Mecca Defence Agreement signalled that the United States was leaving the region.",
        "Analysts describe the pact variously as proof the US-led Middle East order is over (Council on Foreign Relations), as diversification without substitution (Arab Center), as something other than a NATO-style mutual defence organisation (Middle East Council on Global Affairs), and as 'a loose alliance' (OSW Centre for Eastern Studies).",
        "Not documented: any change to US basing agreements, overflight rights, status-of-forces arrangements or command relationships with any of the three signatories.",
        "Not documented: any text of the pact addressing third-party access, any operative annexes, or any instance of the agreement being invoked.",
        "Not documented: whether the signatories would grant or withhold basing, overflight and logistics support for a US ground operation against Iran."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "The hedge reading holds that three of Washington's closest regional partners, having watched an American war bring missiles and drones onto their own territory for six months without a US guarantee that stopped them, have constructed their own mutual-defence architecture — complete with committees and a secretariat — because they no longer trust the American umbrella; on this reading Trump's welcome is either cover or misreading, and Rezaei's 'the US is leaving the region' is the accurate description. The supplement reading holds that this is precisely the burden-sharing successive US administrations have demanded, that the signatories have said in terms that it is defensive and directed at no one, that nothing in US basing or command arrangements has changed, that Pakistan and Turkey have had bilateral defence ties for years and this formalises them, and that a pact welcomed by the President of the United States is a poor candidate for evidence of American decline. A third reading, asserted by neither government but present in the analysis, is that the pact is deliberately ambiguous — sufficiently real to give the signatories leverage in Washington and sufficiently loose to avoid committing any of them to fight for the others.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "The Council on Foreign Relations, in 'The U.S.-Led Middle East Order Is Over. The Mecca Agreement Proves It.'",
        "The signatory governments and a Turkish official quoted by Reuters, presenting the pact as defensive, complementary and open to other regional states",
        "Turkish Foreign Minister Hakan Fidan, describing a NATO-like structure of committees and a permanent secretariat",
        "President Trump, welcoming it as a 'big, bold, and important first step' toward regional self-defence",
        "The Middle East Council on Global Affairs and the Arab Center for Arab Studies in Washington, arguing it is diversification without substitution and less than a NATO-style organisation",
        "OSW Centre for Eastern Studies, characterising it as 'a loose alliance between Saudi Arabia, Pakistan and Turkey'",
        "Mohsen Rezaei, secretary of Iran's Supreme National Security Council, reading it as evidence of American withdrawal",
        "Commentators framing it as a 'Muslim NATO,' a description the signatories have not adopted"
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "An actual invocation of the mutual-defence clause, or a conspicuous non-invocation after an attack on a signatory",
        "A documented grant or refusal of basing, overflight or logistics support to the United States for an operation against Iran by any of the three signatories",
        "Publication of the pact's operative annexes or any text addressing third-party access and command relationships",
        "Any change to US basing agreements, status-of-forces arrangements or force levels in Saudi Arabia, Türkiye or Pakistan attributable to the pact",
        "Accession by additional regional states, which would indicate a bloc rather than a trilateral arrangement",
        "An on-record US assessment of whether the pact constrains or enables American regional operations"
      ],
      "current_assessment": "Unresolvable from open sources as of August 23, 2026. A hedge and a supplement produce the same treaty text, the same reassuring statements and the same presidential welcome until the pact is tested, and no party has an incentive to clarify in advance. The project records the pact as the strongest candidate event yet for corpus predictions of American alliance erosion, while declining to score those predictions as confirmed on it: nothing in the open record shows a single US basing, overflight or command arrangement changing, and the President of the United States publicly endorsed the agreement. The relevance to the ground-invasion tracker is recorded separately: whatever the pact means, it adds three governments that must be consulted before any American lodgement in Iran, and it was signed while Washington was pressing those same governments over the war.",
      "first_listed": "2026-08-23"
    },
    {
      "id": "hormuz-control-attribution-august-2026",
      "title": "Whose action is actually keeping the Strait of Hormuz closed — the US naval blockade or Iranian deterrence? Both governments claim control, and the same traffic data supports either reading.",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "UPDATED — August 23: THE TWO CLAIMS WERE MADE ON THE SAME DAY AND THE BEST NEW DATASET FAVOURS TEHRAN. On AUGUST 22 President Trump said 'I VIEW THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ AS AN AMERICAN TERRITORY RIGHT NOW' — telling supporters in South Carolina 'we don't even know if we won, because I view the Strait of Hormuz as an American territory right now' — and re-posted the Truth Social image labelling the waterway 'NEW U.S. TERRITORY' he first published on August 18. Hours apart, IRNA announced that IRAN HAD GRANTED A NUMBER OF IRAQI OIL TANKERS PERMISSION TO TRANSIT after repeated Baghdad requests through several channels, including during parliament speaker Ghalibaf's visit to Iraq — the FIRST NAMED STATE-LEVEL TRANSIT EXEMPTION OF THE WAR, and an exercise of allocation rather than closure. AND THE FIRST ROUTE-SHARE DATASET OF THE WAR WAS PUBLISHED: Al Jazeera's August 20 analysis of AUGUST 1-19 counted 236 VESSELS OF ALL CARGO TYPES crossing, of which 83 OPENLY USED IRAN'S PRESCRIBED NORTHERN ROUTE, THREE OPENLY USED THE US-BACKED OMANI ROUTE, TWO USED THE PRE-WAR CENTRAL ROUTE AND 148 (62%) WENT DARK; of 112 oil and gas vessels, 21 took the Iranian route, two the Omani route and 89 (more than 80%) went dark. Al Jazeera's stated conclusion is that 'THE FEAR OF OFFENDING TEHRAN APPEARS TO OUTWEIGH TRUMP'S THREATS' and that Iran 'still retains significant capacity' to influence traffic. Iran International reported separately on August 22 that nearly 200 ships transited last week against about 150 the week before, with over 80% using Omani routes or going dark. SNSC secretary MOHSEN REZAEI said on August 22 that 'THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ IS CLOSED AND WILL NOT REOPEN' unless the United States changes its behaviour. CENTCOM's tally reached 68 vessels redirected, 3 disabled and 2 boarded as of August 21, with more than 20 warships enforcing. THE QUESTION IS NOT RESOLVED, BUT ITS BALANCE HAS SHIFTED: the route-share data is the first evidence that discriminates between the two readings rather than being equally consistent with both, and it discriminates in Iran's favour on the narrow point of whose prohibitions shippers obey. It does not establish who is responsible for the traffic that is not moving at all, which remains the larger part of the question. || FIRST LISTED — August 22: On August 17 President Trump said 'we have total control over the strait,' described the US blockade as a 'wall of steel' and said he liked 'the idea of declaring it a territory.' In the same period Iran's parliament speaker stated the strait 'will not reopen until US sanctions are lifted and frozen assets released,' and on August 6-7 Iran announced an Oman-brokered transit arrangement barring American and Israeli vessels outright, fining violators up to 20% of cargo value, denying passage to states deemed to have damaged Iran until compensation is paid, and assigning Iran navigation guidance, traffic monitoring, maritime security and environmental protection — which Washington rejected. Traffic itself is running at one to thirteen transits a day against a pre-crisis baseline of 73, with 386 hulls holding position away from berth on August 21 and war-risk cover near 10% of hull value. CENTCOM has redirected 67 commercial vessels, disabled 3 and boarded 2, and on August 11 disabled the container ship Vela Nova with two Hellfire missiles. CNN's August 21 analysis holds that the reality is 'more nuanced than either side is willing to admit' and that restricted traffic underscores Tehran's continuing leverage. No public dataset attributes non-transit to fear of Iranian attack versus fear of US interdiction.",
      "synopsis": "This question matters for scoring because several corpus predictions turn on WHO can reopen the Strait and therefore on who must be bargained with. If the closure is principally an American blockade effect, reopening is a US decision and Iranian consent is close to irrelevant; if it is principally an Iranian deterrent effect, then no amount of American naval presence reopens the waterway without Tehran, and the coercion runs the other way. The two readings imply opposite negotiating leverage from an identical set of observables. The audit records the dispute rather than adjudicating it, because near-zero traffic is exactly what a successful blockade and a successful deterrent would each produce.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "August 22, 2026: President Trump said 'we don't even know if we won, because I view the Strait of Hormuz as an American territory right now,' speaking to supporters in South Carolina, and re-posted on Truth Social the image labelling the waterway 'NEW U.S. TERRITORY' that he first published on August 18.",
        "August 22, 2026: Iran's state news agency IRNA announced that Tehran had granted a number of Iraqi oil tankers permission to transit the Strait of Hormuz, following repeated requests from Baghdad through several channels including during the visit to Iraq by parliament speaker and lead negotiator Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf. Iraq produced roughly four million barrels a day before the war, has no national tanker fleet, and is developing alternative export routes via Ceyhan, Baniyas and Aqaba.",
        "August 22, 2026: Iraqi President Nizar Amidi said 'everyone is keen to prevent Iraq from being dragged into the ongoing war in the region' and that Iraqi territory 'will not be used to launch attacks against any other country.'",
        "August 20, 2026: Al Jazeera published the first route-share analysis of the war, covering August 1-19. Of 236 vessels of all cargo types crossing the Strait, 83 openly used Iran's prescribed northern route, three openly used the US-backed Omani route, two used the pre-war central route and 148 (62%) went dark or unclassified. Of 112 oil and gas vessels, 21 openly used the Iranian route, two the Omani route and 89 (more than 80%) went dark. The article's conclusion is that 'the fear of offending Tehran appears to outweigh Trump's threats' and that Iran 'still retains significant capacity' to influence maritime traffic.",
        "August 22, 2026: Iran International reported that nearly 200 ships transited the Strait in the previous week, up from about 150 the week before, with over 80% using Omani routes or going dark.",
        "August 22, 2026: Mohsen Rezaei, secretary of Iran's Supreme National Security Council, said 'the Strait of Hormuz is closed and will not reopen' unless the United States changes its behaviour, and warned that any country joining US sanctions would be 'considered an enemy' whose interests, including Gulf oil shipments, Iran could disrupt.",
        "August 21, 2026: CENTCOM said US forces had redirected 68 commercial vessels, disabled three and boarded two, publishing imagery of an MH-60R landing aboard USS Ross (DDG-71), described as one of more than 20 warships supporting the blockade.",
        "August 22, 2026: The straits.live board recorded one transit on August 16 as PortWatch's most recent published day against a 73-per-day pre-crisis baseline, 331 hulls holding position away from berth on a seven-day range of 201-530, war-risk cover at 40 times pre-crisis levels or roughly $10.0 million to insure a single VLCC, and market-implied odds of a reopening by September 1 below 1%.",
        "UKMTO data cited by CBS News recorded 21 inbound and 18 outbound transits in the week ending August 7, roughly 4% of pre-conflict levels, with the risk to mariners still assessed as severe.",
        "August 17, 2026: Trump said 'we have total control over the strait,' described the US blockade as a 'wall of steel' and said he liked 'the idea of declaring it a territory.'",
        "August 6-7, 2026: Iran announced an Oman-brokered transit arrangement barring American and Israeli vessels outright, fining violators up to 20% of cargo value, denying passage to states deemed to have damaged Iran until compensation is paid, and assigning Iran navigation guidance, traffic monitoring, maritime security and environmental protection. Washington rejected it.",
        "Iran's parliament speaker has stated that the Strait 'will not reopen until US sanctions are lifted and frozen assets released.'",
        "May 5, 2026: Iran established a Persian Gulf Strait Authority to authorise and regulate maritime transit, asserting that no vessel may pass without a valid PGSA permit. Enforcement remains contested.",
        "August 11, 2026: A US Navy helicopter disabled the container ship Vela Nova with two Hellfire missiles in the Gulf of Oman after the crew ignored repeated warnings while attempting to reach an Iranian port.",
        "CNN's August 21 analysis holds that the reality is 'more nuanced than either side is willing to admit' and that restricted traffic underscores Tehran's continuing leverage.",
        "No public dataset attributes non-transit to fear of Iranian attack versus fear of US interdiction, and no independent adjudication exists of which party's action causes which portion of the traffic collapse.",
        "Not documented: whether any Iraqi tanker has completed a transit under the August 22 grant, whether US forces would interdict one, or what fraction of the 148 dark hulls moved with Iranian permission, without it, or in defiance of both parties."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "The American reading is that the United States controls the Strait by blockade — Trump's 'wall of steel,' 'total control,' and now the assertion that he views the waterway as 'an American territory right now' — and that near-zero traffic is the measure of a successful interdiction campaign. The Iranian reading is that Tehran controls transit by permission: the Persian Gulf Strait Authority, the Oman-brokered arrangement barring US and Israeli vessels, Rezaei's 'closed and will not reopen,' and now a named exemption granted to Iraq demonstrate that passage is allocated from Tehran rather than from Washington. Both governments assert their reading in flatly incompatible terms and both point at the same traffic collapse as proof. The August 20 route-share analysis is the first evidence that discriminates rather than accommodating both: among hulls that DO cross and DO declare a route, Iran's prescribed route outnumbers the US-backed Omani route by roughly twenty-eight to one, which is a measure of whose prohibition shippers actually obey. It does not, however, speak to the much larger population of hulls that are not sailing at all, or to the 62% that go dark and therefore declare nothing — and the not-sailing population is where the substantive question of control lives. A third reading, neither asserted by a government nor excluded by the data, is that neither party controls the Strait and that the binding constraint is the war-risk underwriting market, which prices a single VLCC passage at roughly $10 million and is indifferent to whose legal regime is nominally in force.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "President Trump, asserting on August 22 'I view the Strait of Hormuz as an American territory right now' and re-posting a map labelling it 'NEW U.S. TERRITORY'; earlier, 'we have total control over the strait' and a 'wall of steel'",
        "CENTCOM, publishing a running interdiction tally of 68 vessels redirected, 3 disabled and 2 boarded and citing more than 20 enforcing warships",
        "Iran's IRNA and parliament speaker Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf, presenting the Iraqi tanker grant as an exercise of Iranian authority over transit",
        "Mohsen Rezaei, secretary of Iran's Supreme National Security Council, stating on August 22 that 'the Strait of Hormuz is closed and will not reopen' absent a change in US behaviour",
        "Iran's parliament speaker, foreign ministry and the Iran-Oman announcement, asserting Iranian sovereignty over transit and a permit regime under the Persian Gulf Strait Authority",
        "Al Jazeera's August 20 data analysis, concluding that 'the fear of offending Tehran appears to outweigh Trump's threats' and that Iran 'still retains significant capacity' to influence traffic",
        "CNN's August 21 analysis, that the reality 'is more nuanced than either side is willing to admit' and that restricted traffic underscores Tehran's continuing leverage"
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "A documented US interdiction — or documented non-interdiction — of an Iraqi cargo sailing under the August 22 Iranian grant, which would show whose permission is operative",
        "A reopening of the Strait under one side's declared terms, or a public reopening announcement that traffic actually follows",
        "Disclosure by war-risk underwriters of their own attribution of non-transit — fear of Iranian attack versus fear of US interdiction versus mine risk",
        "AIS or PortWatch data disaggregating the dark 62% by whether they carried PGSA permits",
        "Any US statement specifying what the 'American territory' claim entails operationally, or any formal legal instrument giving it effect",
        "A sustained divergence in route shares — a collapse in Iranian-route usage or a rise in Omani-route usage — that would indicate a shift in which prohibition shippers obey"
      ],
      "current_assessment": "Unresolved as of August 23, 2026, but no longer symmetric. The August 20 route-share data is the first evidence in this file that discriminates between the two readings rather than being equally consistent with both, and on the narrow question of whose prohibitions declaring shippers obey it favours Tehran by roughly twenty-eight to one. The August 22 grant of transit to Iraqi tankers is a second, weaker discriminator in the same direction: an exemption is a form of control that a blockading power's adversary should not be able to issue. Against this, the United States is demonstrably interdicting — 68 vessels redirected, three disabled — and the traffic that is not moving, which is the overwhelming majority, remains unattributed. The project therefore records the question as unresolved with a documented tilt, and continues to decline to score any corpus prediction that depends on assuming a single controlling party. For prediction purposes the operative fact is unchanged: whoever controls the Strait, no one has landed on anything to do it. || Unresolvable from open sources at present. The audit records both claims and neither. The operative fact for scoring is that transits are running between one and thirteen a day against a 73-per-day baseline, that neither government has demonstrated the ability to change that number by decision, and that the 60-day negotiating window expired on August 17 without an agreement on the waterway.",
      "first_listed": "2026-08-22"
    },
    {
      "id": "iran-strike-pause-choice-or-constraint-august-2026",
      "title": "Is the twenty-night halt in US strikes on Iranian territory a deliberate pivot to economic coercion, or a materiel constraint presented as a strategy?",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "FIRST LISTED — August 22: Late on August 1 Trump announced he was cancelling the planned multi-day campaign against Iran's energy and civil infrastructure, citing progress toward a deal and requests from Iran and 'other Middle Eastern countries.' No US or Israeli strike inside Iranian territory was confirmed by CENTCOM through the 0500 ET cutoff on August 19, a twentieth consecutive night. On August 20 Vice President JD Vance said the war had entered a 'new phase' of economic pressure that is 'the best way to ultimately accomplish the final objective here,' and Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent promised 'the greatest coordinated economic isolation in the history of the world' while saying the US likely would not restart large-scale combat. Against this: CSIS assesses more than 60% of US Patriot and THAAD interceptor stocks expended with three-plus years to rebuild; roughly a third of the Tomahawk inventory is depleted after more than 1,000 expended, answered on August 17 by a $22.9 billion seven-year Raytheon contract to lift output above 1,000 missiles a year; Gen. Dan Caine was reported in late July to have warned privately about shortages, cited as a restraint on broader strikes; and Al Jazeera's August 2 analysis listed Patriot depletion among the practical influences on the cancellation. No US statement has connected the pause to inventory, and no formal stand-down order has entered the open record.",
      "synopsis": "The distinction is decisive for every corpus prediction that models escalation as a ratchet. A pause chosen for leverage leaves the kinetic option intact and implies resumption if sanctions fail; a pause forced by inventory implies a ceiling on American action that will persist for as long as the rebuild takes — CSIS says three or more years for interceptors, and the Tomahawk contract runs seven. The public signature is identical either way: no strikes, an economic announcement, and a munitions contract that both readings can claim as evidence.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "August 1, 2026: Trump announced he was cancelling the planned attack on Iran, citing progress toward a deal and requests from Iran and other regional states.",
        "August 1-19, 2026: no US or Israeli strike inside Iranian territory confirmed by CENTCOM, a twentieth consecutive night, with no new Iranian civilian casualties from strikes reported across the span.",
        "August 20, 2026: Vance said the war had entered a 'new phase' of economic pressure and that this is 'the best way to ultimately accomplish the final objective here'; Bessent said the US likely would not restart large-scale combat operations.",
        "CSIS assesses fewer than 827 Patriot interceptors remaining against roughly 2,200 pre-war and fewer than 278 THAAD against 452 — more than 60% of both expended — with three or more years to rebuild; three sources told CNN the estimates track internal government figures.",
        "Roughly one-third of the US Tomahawk stockpile is depleted after more than 1,000 expended since Operation Epic Fury began, over 850 in the first four weeks; on August 17 the Navy awarded Raytheon a seven-year, $22.9 billion contract to raise annual production above 1,000 missiles.",
        "Gen. Dan Caine was reported in late July to have warned privately about interceptor and munitions shortages, cited in reporting as a factor in restraint on broader strikes.",
        "Al Jazeera's August 2 analysis listed the fall in Patriot stocks from roughly 2,300 to fewer than 827 among the practical influences on the cancellation."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "That the 'new phase' framing is a post-hoc rationalisation of a halt the United States could not avoid — that with interceptors, Tomahawks and the target set all depleted, and with the CENTCOM commander reported in July to have told the White House the target set was 'mostly exhausted,' the administration converted an enforced stop into an announced strategy. On this reading the sanctions campaign is what remains available rather than what was chosen, and the $22.9 billion Tomahawk contract is the admission.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "The White House and Treasury, presenting the pivot to economic isolation as a strategic choice made on its merits (Vance, Bessent, August 20-21)",
        "Al Jazeera, listing interceptor depletion among the practical influences on the August 1 cancellation",
        "CNN and Military Times, reporting stockpile depletion as a constraint on the administration's options",
        "Axios reporting on Gen. Caine's private warnings as a restraint on broader strikes",
        "Analyst Bryan Clark, that US military options are limited and air power alone cannot decide the conflict"
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "A resumption of large-scale strikes on Iranian territory before inventories are rebuilt, which would falsify the constraint reading",
        "An on-record account from the President, Secretary of War or CENTCOM connecting or disconnecting the pause from inventory",
        "Publication of a readiness or munitions assessment covering the period",
        "Congressional testimony or an inspector-general review addressing the decision",
        "A sanctions campaign that visibly fails without any resumption of strikes, which would support the constraint reading"
      ],
      "current_assessment": "Unresolvable from open sources at present. The audit records the pause as fact and declines to infer its cause. For scoring purposes the operative point is the same under either reading: the kinetic escalation the corpus predicted did not occur, and the administration's stated forward path is financial.",
      "first_listed": "2026-08-22"
    },
    {
      "id": "iran-energy-strike-campaign-order-status-july-2026",
      "title": "Has Trump ORDERED the multi-day campaign against Iran's energy infrastructure, or is he still weighing it? The Wall Street Journal reports the campaign as ordered with the aim of forcing surrender; CBS News reports no final authorisation; CNN reports it could be called off; an Israeli official says Israel is unaware of a decision.",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "UPDATED AUGUST 22 — PARTIALLY RESOLVED, AND THE RESIDUE IS NARROW. Late on August 1 the President publicly announced that he was CANCELLING the planned attack on Iran, saying Iran and 'other Middle Eastern countries' had asked him to hold off and conditioning the cancellation on 'being able to rapidly make a DEAL' covering the 'Immediate, Complete, and Total OPENING OF THE HORMUZ STRAIT, and an end to Iran's nuclear threat'; Saudi Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman had pressed him that Saturday, with Qatar, the UAE, Turkey and Pakistan also urging de-escalation. Tehran denied requesting anything, calling the claim 'nothing but a new lie,' and on August 2-3 Trump called Iranian leadership 'unbelievably duplicitous.' The companion question — why the reported weekend campaign never began — is RETIRED as resolved by this statement, and is recorded as a confirmed event. What remains unresolved here is narrower and still undocumented: whether a formal execute order was ever issued. 'Cancelling the attack' is equally consistent with cancelling a plan that had been ordered and with cancelling one that had only been prepared, and no War Powers notification, cancellation order or decision-chain record has entered the open source. No US or Israeli strike inside Iranian territory was confirmed by CENTCOM through August 19, a twentieth consecutive night. || UPDATED — August 2: The weekend in which the campaign was reported able to begin passed without it. Through the end of August 1, 2026 no US strike wave against Iranian territory was reported, and Al Jazeera recorded that Trump 'had not yet authorized the potential two-week operation targeting Tehran's electricity grid' — consistent with CBS News's July 31 reporting that final authorisation had not been given and inconsistent with the Wall Street Journal's framing that the order had been issued. ABC News added that CENTCOM had prepared a two-week air campaign while some administration officials favoured 'a shorter, more severe salvo' to advance negotiations. Non-occurrence within the reported window strengthens the 'not authorised' side without settling the question: an order held in reserve produces the same public signature. The successor operational question — WHY the campaign did not begin — is listed separately as iran-weekend-campaign-non-start-august-2026. || FIRST LISTED — August 1: On July 31, 2026 four outlets reported the same coercive package on the same day and split on whether it had been ordered. The Wall Street Journal, citing US officials, reported that President Trump had ordered another wave of strikes against Iran 'with the aim of forcing the Islamic Republic's surrender,' expected to last several days and able to begin as soon as the weekend. CBS News, citing multiple US sources, reported that the president had NOT given final authorisation and that the plan remained pending his decision. CNN, citing two US officials, reported planning for fresh strikes as soon as this weekend while stating the precise scope and targets were unclear and that the strikes could be called off. Axios reported that Trump was CONSIDERING strikes on Iranian energy targets within days. An Israeli official said: 'Israel is unaware of a decision to restart full military operations, nor has been requested of Israel to join any military actions against Iran' — against multiple US sources telling CBS that Israel had been notified and was coordinating.",
      "synopsis": "The distinction is not editorial. An ordered multi-day campaign against a state's power plants, refineries and capital-city electricity grid, declared to be aimed at surrender, is a different act in law, in escalation management and in prediction-scoring terms than a plan drafted and held. It bears on the War Powers question, on whether allies were consulted or merely informed, and on whether the reported target classes represent a decision about instruments — airpower rather than the Kharg Island seizure option prepared since March — or a menu still open. The project records the reporting on both sides and scores the ground-invasion predictions on what is executed, not on what is reported as planned.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "July 31, 2026: The Wall Street Journal, citing US officials, reported that Trump has ordered another wave of strikes against Iran aimed at forcing the Islamic Republic's surrender, expected to last several days and able to start as soon as this weekend (relayed by Bloomberg and The Times of Israel).",
        "July 31, 2026: CBS News, citing multiple US sources, reported that the US and Israel are preparing to bombard energy-related targets in Iran, and that President Trump has NOT yet given final authorisation; the plan remains pending his decision.",
        "July 31, 2026: CBS reported the target classes as power plants and refineries, with electricity infrastructure across Tehran under high-level discussion but no decision as of Friday, and bridges of dual military-civilian purpose also under consideration.",
        "July 31, 2026: CBS reported strikes were possible throughout the weekend, that an end point was not locked in, and that there was discussion of concluding before financial markets opened Monday.",
        "July 31, 2026: The plan was raised at Trump's cabinet meeting at Camp David, where he said: 'We'll be hitting them very hard. At some point, they're going to say, We just can't take it anymore.'",
        "July 31, 2026: One source told CBS that White House aides focused on politics 'were strongly opposed.'",
        "July 31, 2026: CNN, citing two US officials, reported fresh strikes as soon as this weekend, noting the precise scope and potential targets were not clear and that both officials cautioned the strikes could be called off.",
        "July 31, 2026: Axios reported Trump was considering strikes on Iranian energy targets within days.",
        "July 31, 2026: An Israeli official said 'Israel is unaware of a decision to restart full military operations, nor has been requested of Israel to join any military actions against Iran,' while multiple US sources told CBS that Israel had been notified and was coordinating with the American military.",
        "July 31, 2026: Trump said publicly 'I'm losing faith in them because they do lie' while not ruling out a deal, and that 'Iran is doing very poorly.'",
        "Not documented as of the cycle close: any execute order, air tasking order, deployment order, War Powers notification, or on-record confirmation or denial of an order by Trump, Secretary Hegseth, the Pentagon or CENTCOM.",
        "Not documented: any ground component in any version of the reporting — no landing force, embarkation, MEU tasking, airborne insertion or Kharg Island operation was named by any of the four outlets.",
        "August 1, 2026: Al Jazeera reported that Trump 'had not yet authorized the potential two-week operation targeting Tehran's electricity grid.'",
        "July 31, 2026 (7:34 PM EDT): ABC News reported that CENTCOM had prepared a two-week air campaign, and that some administration officials favoured 'a shorter, more severe salvo' intended to advance negotiations rather than force capitulation.",
        "Through the end of August 1, 2026: the campaign did not begin. No US strike wave against Iranian territory was reported within the window described by the July 31 sourcing.",
        "As of August 2, 2026 no execute order, tasking message, air tasking order, War Powers notification or on-record confirmation or denial has emerged on either side of the dispute."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "That no order exists and the WSJ account reflects planning traffic and presidential intent read as decision — a pattern this war has produced repeatedly, most notably in the May 'two or three days' strike windows that were reset rather than executed. On this reading the reporting is itself an instrument of coercion: publicising a prepared campaign against Iran's refineries and power grid applies pressure at the negotiating table at no military cost, and is consistent with CBS's account of a plan awaiting a decision the president has not made.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "The Wall Street Journal, reporting the campaign as ordered, relayed by Bloomberg and The Times of Israel",
        "CBS News, reporting that final authorisation has not been given and the plan is pending the president's decision",
        "CNN, citing two US officials, reporting planning without a decision and noting the strikes could be called off",
        "Axios, reporting the strikes as under consideration within days",
        "An unnamed Israeli official, stating Israel is unaware of a decision to restart full military operations",
        "President Trump, describing intent at Camp David without confirming an order"
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "Execution of the campaign, or its visible non-execution over a defined period",
        "A War Powers Resolution notification to Congress specifying the authorised operation",
        "An on-record statement by the President, the Secretary of Defense or CENTCOM confirming or denying that an order has been issued",
        "A CENTCOM public release announcing the operation by name, as with Operation Epic Fury",
        "An Israeli government statement confirming or denying a request to participate",
        "Continued non-occurrence over an extended period, which progressively favours the 'not authorised' reading without confirming it",
        "Any reporting that identifies a cancelled or rescinded order rather than the absence of one"
      ],
      "current_assessment": "Unresolvable from open sources as of August 2, 2026, and now bearing a first piece of behavioural evidence: the campaign did not begin within the window its own reporting described. That favours the CBS/CNN/Al Jazeera 'no final authorisation' account over the Wall Street Journal's 'ordered' account, but does not settle it, because an order withheld and an order never given are indistinguishable from outside. The project continues to score ground-invasion predictions on what is executed, not on what is reported as planned or ordered — and notes that no version of this reporting, on either side of the dispute, has ever contained a ground element. || Unresolvable from open sources as of August 1, 2026. 'Ordered' and 'planned but not authorised' generate an identical public signature in the interval before execution: the same planning traffic, the same allied notification, the same presidential rhetoric, the same anonymous sourcing from officials with access to different points in a decision chain. The project records the reporting as documented and consequential, does not treat it as establishing that an order exists, and continues to score the ground-invasion predictions on execution. Note independently of the dispute: every version of this reporting describes an AIR campaign, and none contains a ground component.",
      "first_listed": "2026-08-01"
    },
    {
      "id": "iraq-prior-knowledge-us-saudi-strikes-july-2026",
      "title": "Was Baghdad consulted before the July 28 US-Saudi strikes on PMF positions? Trump said the operation was coordinated with Baghdad; the Iraqi government says it had no prior knowledge and calls the strikes a flagrant violation of its sovereignty.",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "FIRST LISTED — August 1: On July 28, 2026 CENTCOM and the Saudi Arabian Armed Forces conducted joint precision strikes in Iraq against Iran-aligned militia logistics hubs and weapons storage sites, after what CENTCOM described as 30+ IRGC-directed drone attacks on US forces and Saudi energy infrastructure in 72 hours. The Popular Mobilisation Forces reported 20 killed and 32 wounded across Baghdad, Wasit, Nineveh, Basra, Kirkuk, Karbala and Diyala. On July 29 Iraq's National Security Council, chaired by Prime Minister Ali al-Zaidi, called the attacks on PMF bases in seven provinces 'a flagrant violation of Iraq's sovereignty and the sanctity of its lands' and 'an aggression' contrary to international law and the UN Charter, and Baghdad summoned both the US charge d'affaires and the Iranian ambassador. On July 31 the Iraqi government stated it had had NO PRIOR KNOWLEDGE of the strikes, directly contradicting President Trump's statement that the operation had been coordinated with Baghdad. Iraq suspended al-Zaidi's planned visit to Saudi Arabia.",
      "synopsis": "The dispute determines whether the July 28 strikes were a coalition operation conducted with host-state consent or an armed action on the territory of a partner government without it. That in turn bears on the status of the September 30 US withdrawal timetable, on al-Zaidi's parallel commitment to disarm the Iran-aligned militias by the same date, and on whether Iraq is a participant in the war or a theatre of it. Both governments have made flat, incompatible public statements and neither has published a record.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "July 28, 2026: CENTCOM stated that it 'and the Saudi Arabian Armed Forces conducted precision strikes in Iraq, July 28, against Iran-aligned terrorists that the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) directed to attack U.S. forces and Saudi energy infrastructure.'",
        "The strikes targeted militia logistics hubs and weapons storage sites and followed a reported 30+ IRGC-directed drone attacks in 72 hours; they were Riyadh's first claimed strikes inside Iraq of the war, framed under Article 51.",
        "The Popular Mobilisation Forces reported 20 killed and 32 wounded across Baghdad, Wasit, Nineveh, Basra, Kirkuk, Karbala and Diyala.",
        "July 29, 2026: Iraq's National Security Council, chaired by PM Ali al-Zaidi, called the attacks on PMF bases in seven provinces 'a flagrant violation of Iraq's sovereignty and the sanctity of its lands' and 'an aggression' in contravention of international law and the UN Charter; the Iraqi presidency 'categorically rejected any attacks in Iraqi territory.'",
        "Baghdad summoned both the US charge d'affaires and the Iranian ambassador.",
        "July 31, 2026: The Iraqi government said it had no prior knowledge of the US and Saudi strikes, contradicting President Trump's statement that the operation had been coordinated with Baghdad.",
        "Iraq suspended Prime Minister al-Zaidi's planned visit to Saudi Arabia following the strikes.",
        "Al-Zaidi has separately committed to disarming the armed groups and bringing weapons under state control by September 30, 2026, the date on which US forces are scheduled to withdraw from Iraq.",
        "Iran-aligned Iraqi factions publicly condemned the strikes as a 'dangerous escalation.'",
        "Not documented: any notification record, readout, call transcript, timestamp or third-party account establishing whether, when and at what level Iraq was informed."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "That both statements are literally defensible and describe the same event at different levels: a short-notice warning passed to a single Iraqi official or channel shortly before execution can be characterised in Washington as coordination and in Baghdad as no prior knowledge of the operation. On this reading neither government is lying and the dispute is about what threshold of notice constitutes consultation — with a government under acute domestic pressure from the targeted militias having every incentive to describe the notice it received, if any, as none.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "President Trump, stating that the operation was coordinated with Baghdad",
        "The Government of Iraq, stating it had no prior knowledge of the strikes",
        "Iraq's National Security Council under PM Ali al-Zaidi, characterising the strikes as an aggression and a sovereignty violation",
        "The Iraqi presidency, categorically rejecting any attacks on Iraqi territory",
        "Iran-aligned Iraqi factions, condemning the strikes as a dangerous escalation",
        "US Central Command, which announced the strikes jointly with Saudi Arabia and did not address the consultation question"
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "Publication by either government of a notification record, call readout or timestamp",
        "An on-record statement by CENTCOM, the US embassy in Baghdad or the State Department specifying whether, when and at what level Iraq was notified",
        "Testimony or a document produced to the Iraqi Council of Representatives in any inquiry into the strikes",
        "A Saudi statement on whether Riyadh notified Baghdad independently",
        "A change in the September 30 withdrawal timetable or the status-of-forces arrangement attributable to the dispute"
      ],
      "current_assessment": "Unresolvable from open sources as of August 1, 2026. Consultation disputes of this kind are almost never adjudicated publicly, both governments have strong and opposite incentives in their characterisations, and no documentary record has been released by either side. The project records the strikes, the casualties, the Iraqi National Security Council's characterisation, the suspension of the Riyadh visit and the direct contradiction of the President's statement as documented, and does not resolve the consultation question.",
      "first_listed": "2026-08-01"
    },
    {
      "id": "us-coastal-strike-set-shaping-or-interdiction-july-2026",
      "title": "The July 29-30 'heavy wave' concentrated on Iran's coastal surveillance, coastal defence and maritime capability along the Gulf littoral — is that the suppression phase of the reported limited coastal raid, or simply the interdiction campaign a blockading navy runs?",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "FIRST LISTED — July 31: CENTCOM announced it had \"successfully completed a heavy wave of strikes against Iran,\" striking \"dozens of Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) targets in Iran, including military command centers, missile and drone facilities, coastal surveillance and defense sites, and maritime capabilities\" over roughly two hours — the first strike on Iranian soil since July 24 and, per a US official, \"about twice the size of the previous\" bombardment. The reported strike map is almost entirely littoral: Bandar Abbas, Qeshm Island, Kish Island, Abu Musa, three cities of Bushehr province, Kazerun and Farashband in Fars, and Abadan and Ahvaz in Khuzestan. Since March 2026 the Pentagon has been reported to hold options for weeks of limited Special Operations and conventional raids on Kharg Island and coastal weapons sites, explicitly short of invasion — operations that would require exactly this suppression first. No order, embarkation, rehearsal, ARG repositioning or MEU tasking has been reported, and Kharg itself was not struck.",
      "synopsis": "This is the first cycle of the war in which the air campaign's target set maps cleanly onto the geography of the ground option the project has tracked as disconfirmed for 155 days. That makes it the most consequential ambiguity currently in the file: if the strikes are shaping, the ground-seizure prediction is closer to confirmation than at any prior point; if they are interdiction, they are the ordinary business of a navy that has been blockading Iranian ports since July 14 and escorting convoys through a strait where the IRGC struck three tankers on July 29. The strike list is identical under both hypotheses, because suppressing coastal radar, anti-ship missiles and maritime surveillance is simultaneously the precondition for an amphibious raid and the entire objective of protecting shipping. The project records the pattern as an ambiguous shaping signal and does not resolve it, while noting that every execution indicator remains at zero.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "Overnight July 29-30, 2026: CENTCOM announced it had \"successfully completed a heavy wave of strikes against Iran,\" striking \"dozens\" of IRGC targets over approximately two hours.",
        "CENTCOM's stated target categories were \"military command centers, missile and drone facilities, coastal surveillance and defense sites, and maritime capabilities.\"",
        "A US official told The Jerusalem Post the bombardment was \"about twice the size of the previous\" one.",
        "Reported strike locations: Bandar Abbas (Hormozgan), Qeshm Island, Kish Island, Abu Musa, three cities in Bushehr province, Kazerun and Farashband (Fars), and Abadan and Ahvaz (Khuzestan, seven locations with power outages). Nearly all sit on the Persian Gulf littoral or the approaches to the Strait of Hormuz.",
        "Kharg Island — the reported centrepiece of the ground-raid options and the handler of roughly 90% of Iran's crude exports — was NOT among the reported targets of this wave.",
        "Since late March 2026, The Washington Post and subsequent reporting have described Pentagon preparation of options for weeks of limited ground operations: Special Operations and conventional raids on Kharg Island and coastal weapons sites near the Strait, explicitly stopping short of full-scale invasion, pending presidential approval.",
        "Officials cited in that reporting said such missions would expose US personnel to Iranian drone swarms, missiles, ground fire and improvised explosives.",
        "The US naval blockade of Iranian ports, re-imposed July 14, 2026, remains active with 20+ warships; CENTCOM's cumulative tally is 18 commercial vessels stopped, 2 disabled and 2 boarded.",
        "On July 29 the IRGC said it had struck and stopped three oil tankers in the Strait of Hormuz, and Iran declared third-party mine-clearing vessels near the Strait \"legitimate targets\" — an active anti-shipping threat that independently justifies striking coastal defence and surveillance systems.",
        "No embarkation order, amphibious rehearsal, ARG repositioning toward the northern Persian Gulf, MEU tasking or landing has been reported. USS Boxer (LHD-4) remains in the Arabian Sea with the 11th MEU as the sole deployed ARG fleet-wide as of the July 27 USNI tracker; USS Tripoli is home in Sasebo with no relief announced.",
        "Neither the President, the Pentagon nor CENTCOM named a ground formation, amphibious operation or Kharg landing in connection with the wave.",
        "Troop strength in theatre remains approximately 50,000, unchanged."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "That the concentrated destruction of Iran's coastal surveillance, coastal defence and anti-ship capability along the Gulf littoral is the deliberate shaping phase of the limited raid package the Pentagon has held since March — the necessary precondition being executed before a decision on the raids themselves, with Kharg deliberately left intact because it is the objective rather than the obstacle.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "Former Defense Secretary Mark Esper, who has publicly read this war's operational pauses and repositioning as preparation for \"something much bigger\"",
        "Commentary outlets reading the July 30 wave as the opening of a sustained campaign against Iranian infrastructure (HotAir, \"'Heavy Wave' of Strikes: Will This (Finally) Be Infrastructure Week?\", July 30, 2026)",
        "Reporting since March 2026 (The Washington Post, Al Jazeera, UNITED24 Media, Asharq Al-Awsat) that the Pentagon has prepared Kharg and coastal-raid options",
        "The Quincy Institute for Responsible Statecraft and National Security Journal, publicly arguing the contrary case — that limited ground operations would not shift the war's balance and that a Kharg seizure is a myth"
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "An embarkation order, amphibious rehearsal or ARG/MEU movement toward the northern Persian Gulf",
        "A strike package against Kharg Island itself, or renewed Kharg targeting after months without it",
        "An on-record statement by the President, SecDef or CENTCOM naming a ground or amphibious operation",
        "Congressional notification of a ground operation, or a War Powers report describing one",
        "Conversely: a stated CENTCOM campaign objective framing the strikes purely as freedom-of-navigation interdiction, or a return to inland target sets"
      ],
      "current_assessment": "Unresolvable from open sources as of July 30, 2026, and structurally so. Suppression of coastal radar, anti-ship missiles and maritime surveillance is a prerequisite for an amphibious raid AND a complete objective in its own right for a navy blockading ports and escorting convoys through a strait where three tankers were struck the day before. The two purposes generate an identical target list, and no US official has characterised the campaign's objective beyond the strike announcement. The project continues to score the ground-seizure prediction DISCONFIRMED on the basis of execution indicators, all of which remain at zero, while recording this as the first ambiguous shaping signal of the war and the single most consequential open question now on the ground rung.",
      "first_listed": "2026-07-31"
    },
    {
      "id": "damietta-drone-attribution-july-2026",
      "title": "Who attacked Egypt's Damietta port? A drone hit two LNG vessels on July 29 in the first attack on Egyptian soil of the war; Trump blamed Iran without evidence, Egypt confirmed a drone and named no one, and no party has claimed responsibility.",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "UPDATED — August 1: The attribution moved AWAY from Tehran on July 31 without being resolved. Iranian Foreign Minister ABBAS ARAGHCHI DENIED Iranian involvement, writing that 'Egypt is an important friend and partner in the region,' that Cairo's security is of utmost importance to Tehran, and warning: 'We must all be vigilant against Israeli plots and FALSE-FLAG OPERATIONS designed to undermine regional peace.' EGYPT'S MINISTER OF STATE FOR INFORMATION DIAA RASHWAN PUBLICLY DISMISSED a Wall Street Journal report claiming the Egyptian government believed Iran was responsible. Egyptian authorities said no group had claimed responsibility, recovered the DRONE WRECKAGE FOR FORENSIC ANALYSIS, and warned against further escalation; PRIME MINISTER MOSTAFA MADBOULY said security agencies were examining the debris to determine its origin. Cairo has now condemned the strike, declined to attribute it, and explicitly rejected the attribution reported on its behalf. President Trump's July 30 attribution to Iran, made without published evidence, stands unretracted and uncorroborated. || FIRST LISTED — July 31: At approximately 14:20 UTC on July 29, 2026 a drone struck the US-owned, Marshall Islands-flagged floating storage and regasification unit Energos Winter at the Mediterranean port of Damietta, and fire spread to the Bermuda-flagged LNG carrier Gaslog Salem. Both crews were evacuated, the fire was brought under control, and no injuries were reported. Egypt's Cabinet confirmed on July 30 that preliminary investigations found the incident was caused by a drone, while noting that no party had claimed responsibility. President Trump publicly blamed Iran without presenting evidence. Port operations at one of Egypt's principal gas import terminals were disrupted.",
      "synopsis": "The attribution matters far beyond the incident. Damietta is the first attack on Egyptian soil since the war began on February 28, and the first anywhere in this conflict against an energy IMPORT terminal rather than an export chokepoint or production facility. If Iran launched it, the war has acquired a Mediterranean front roughly 2,000 km from the Strait of Hormuz, Iran has demonstrated reach against the receiving end of the global gas chain, and Egypt — a state that has stayed out of the fighting — has a casus belli. If it was a proxy acting without direction, or a party seeking to widen the war, the strategic reading is entirely different. Egypt investigated and declined to name anyone; the United States named Iran without publishing a basis. No open-source evidence adjudicates between them.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "July 29, 2026, ~14:20 UTC: a drone struck the US-owned, Marshall Islands-flagged FSRU Energos Winter at Egypt's Mediterranean port of Damietta.",
        "Fire spread from the Energos Winter to the Bermuda-flagged LNG carrier Gaslog Salem, which was also damaged.",
        "Both ships' crews were evacuated; the fire was brought under control; no injuries were reported.",
        "July 30, 2026: Egypt's Cabinet confirmed that preliminary investigations found the fire was caused by a drone.",
        "Egypt did not name a perpetrator, and no party has claimed responsibility.",
        "President Trump publicly blamed Iran for the attack without presenting evidence (reported in Just Security's July 30, 2026 Early Edition).",
        "This is the first attack on Egyptian soil since the war between Iran and the United States began on February 28, 2026.",
        "Damietta is one of Egypt's principal gas import terminals; operations there were disrupted.",
        "No debris analysis, forensic attribution, launch-point assessment, flight track, intercepted communication or on-record intelligence assessment has been published by any government.",
        "Context documented separately: Iran has conducted extensive one-way drone attacks against Gulf states and shipping throughout the war, and the Houthis have conducted drone and missile attacks in the Red Sea and against Saudi shipping; both have the demonstrated capability, and neither has claimed this attack.",
        "July 31, 2026: Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi denied Iranian involvement in the Damietta attack, calling Egypt 'an important friend and partner in the region' and stating Cairo's security is of utmost importance to Tehran.",
        "July 31, 2026: Araghchi warned: 'We must all be vigilant against Israeli plots and false-flag operations designed to undermine regional peace.'",
        "July 31, 2026: The Wall Street Journal reported that the Egyptian government believed Iran was responsible for the attack.",
        "July 31, 2026: Egypt's Minister of State for Information Diaa Rashwan publicly dismissed the Wall Street Journal report, rejecting the claim that Cairo believes Iran responsible.",
        "July 31, 2026: Egyptian authorities stated that no group had claimed responsibility, that drone wreckage had been recovered for forensic analysis, and that the investigation was ongoing; Prime Minister Mostafa Madbouly said security agencies were examining the debris to determine its origin.",
        "July 31, 2026: Egypt condemned the strike and warned against further regional escalation while continuing to decline to assign responsibility.",
        "As of August 1, 2026: no forensic finding, launch track, radar assessment or on-record intelligence attribution has been published by Egypt, the United States, Israel or any other government, and no party has claimed responsibility."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "That the attack was not Iranian-directed — either a proxy or third party acting independently, or an actor seeking to widen the war by drawing Egypt in — and that the US attribution reflects the default assignment of unclaimed attacks to Tehran rather than a specific intelligence finding.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi, denying Iranian involvement and alleging possible Israeli false-flag activity",
        "Egypt's Minister of State for Information Diaa Rashwan, publicly rejecting the Wall Street Journal's characterisation of Cairo's assessment",
        "Egyptian Prime Minister Mostafa Madbouly, confirming forensic analysis of recovered drone debris is under way",
        "The Wall Street Journal, reporting that the Egyptian government believed Iran responsible",
        "President Trump, attributing the attack to Iran without presenting evidence",
        "Egypt's Cabinet, confirming a drone caused the fire while stating that no party had claimed responsibility",
        "The Foundation for Defense of Democracies, reading the strike as part of an expanding Iranian pressure campaign",
        "Maritime trade press (Seatrade Maritime, The Maritime Executive) reporting the attack as unattributed"
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "Egyptian publication of debris analysis or forensic attribution identifying the airframe type and origin",
        "A launch-track or radar assessment released by Egypt, the United States, Israel or a NATO member",
        "A claim of responsibility by Iran, the Houthis or any other party",
        "A US sanctions designation, indictment or on-record intelligence assessment naming the perpetrator",
        "An Egyptian government statement formally attributing the attack, or formally declining to"
      ],
      "current_assessment": "Unresolvable from open sources as of August 1, 2026, and now contested on a second axis: not only who launched the drone, but what Egypt actually believes. Cairo has recovered wreckage, opened a forensic investigation, condemned the strike, declined to attribute it, and publicly rejected reporting that it privately blames Iran. Tehran has denied responsibility and offered an alternative attribution to Israel. Washington has attributed it to Iran without publishing a basis. Every actor's stated position is the one its own incentives predict — Cairo gains from non-attribution of an attack it would then be obliged to answer, Tehran from denial of a strike on a state it calls a friend, Washington from attribution to the adversary it is already bombing. A single unclaimed one-way drone against a fixed port target leaves an identical public signature regardless of origin. The project records the attack itself as documented and consequential — the first attack on Egyptian soil of the war and the first anywhere in this conflict against an energy IMPORT terminal — and does not resolve attribution.",
      "first_listed": "2026-07-31"
    },
    {
      "id": "irgc-al-azraq-f35-casualty-claim-july-2026",
      "title": "Did Iran destroy three F-35s and kill US personnel at Al Azraq Air Base in Jordan? The IRGC claims both; CENTCOM categorically denies the aircraft and says nothing about the personnel.",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "UPDATED — August 1: The same evidentiary structure repeated across three more bases. On July 31 Iran's army and the IRGC claimed one-way attack drone strikes on US facilities at SHEIKH ISA AIR BASE in Bahrain — which Iran's military called 'one of the most important US military installations in the Gulf region' — and at AHMAD AL-JABER and ALI AL-SALEM in Kuwait and AL AZRAQ in Jordan, with specific target descriptions (power generators, navigation systems, administrative and support buildings; aircraft shelters, satellite communication systems, equipment storage). Iran asserted the recent attacks had inflicted 'SIGNIFICANT DAMAGE' and caused 'serious damage to the base's combat and logistical support capabilities,' framing the Bahrain operation as retaliation for an Iranian Air Force Su-24 pilot killed at the start of the war in February. THERE WAS NO IMMEDIATE COMMENT FROM BAHRAINI OR US AUTHORITIES. The pattern established on July 30 — a categorical CENTCOM denial of the specific F-35 claim alongside silence on personnel and on general damage — now extends to a set of claims that has drawn no response at all. || FIRST LISTED — July 31: On July 30, 2026 Iran's Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps claimed its forces destroyed three F-35 fighter jets at Al Azraq Air Base in Jordan — the installation also identified as Muwaffaq Salti, which the IRGC named as a target of its July 28 ballistic strike — and killed US personnel there. CENTCOM rejected the aircraft claim categorically, stating that no US aircraft were destroyed or damaged and that all missiles and drones were intercepted. CENTCOM did not formally address the claim of US casualties, and The Washington Times reported that it had requested clarification from the command and had not received it by publication.",
      "synopsis": "The shape of the denial is what makes this unresolvable rather than merely disputed. CENTCOM issued a specific, categorical denial on airframes and a silence on personnel, and in a war where the same department has moved four deaths and 207 wounded into an unexplained 'Overseas Operations' category, a silence carries less exculpatory weight than it otherwise would. Against that, Iran has repeatedly claimed damage at bases where no independent evidence of damage subsequently emerged, and CENTCOM's interception claims for the July 28 salvo have not been contradicted by any third party. Jordan, on whose territory the base sits and whose air defences engaged the missiles, has said nothing about damage. No imagery has been published.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "July 30, 2026: the IRGC claimed its forces destroyed three F-35 fighter jets at Al Azraq Air Base in Jordan and killed US personnel there.",
        "Al Azraq is the Jordanian installation also identified as Muwaffaq Salti Air Base, which the IRGC named on July 29 as the target of its July 28 ballistic strike.",
        "CENTCOM rejected the aircraft claim, stating that no US aircraft were destroyed or damaged in the Iranian attacks on the air base and that all missiles and drones were intercepted.",
        "CENTCOM did not formally address the IRGC's claim of US military casualties at the facility; The Washington Times reported requesting clarification from the command.",
        "July 29, 2026 (prior cycle): the IRGC said its air force targeted Muwaffaq Salti Air Base and a CENTCOM centre in Jordan, and Jordan's air defences intercepted five missiles. No US casualties were reported at the time.",
        "July 28, 2026: CENTCOM said IRGC forces \"launched multiple ballistic missiles from Iran in an attempted surprise attack on U.S. forces\" and that \"all Iranian missiles were successfully intercepted,\" with no casualties.",
        "No imagery of damaged or intact aircraft at the base, no independent count of airframes present, and no Jordanian statement on damage has been published.",
        "Context documented separately: the Pentagon has moved 4 deaths and 207 wounded out of the official Iran-war casualty totals into a new 'Overseas Operations' category, a reclassification that remains unexplained and unreversed, with total wounded across both categories at 642.",
        "Context documented separately: three US soldiers were killed by Iranian ballistic missiles and drones at a base in Jordan on July 17, 2026 — an attack the US did acknowledge — establishing that Iranian strikes on Jordanian-based US forces have caused fatalities before.",
        "July 31, 2026: Iran's army said one-way attack drones struck US assets at Sheikh Isa Air Base in southern Bahrain, targeting power generators, navigation systems, and administrative and support buildings; Iran's military described the base as 'one of the most important US military installations in the Gulf region.'",
        "July 31, 2026: Iran's army said it targeted aircraft shelters, satellite communication systems and equipment storage facilities used by the US military at Kuwait's Ahmad al-Jaber Air Base; the IRGC claimed attacks on US assets at Ali al-Salem Air Base in Kuwait and Al Azraq Air Base in Jordan.",
        "July 31, 2026: Iran stated the operation against Sheikh Isa was carried out 'in retaliation for the killing of an Iranian Air Force Su-24 pilot at the start of the US-Israeli war on Iran in February.'",
        "July 31, 2026: Iran's military claimed repeated attacks on US bases in the region over recent days had inflicted 'significant damage' on military equipment and facilities and caused 'serious damage to the base's combat and logistical support capabilities.'",
        "July 31, 2026: There was no immediate comment from Bahraini or US authorities on the claims, and no casualties were reported.",
        "As of August 1, 2026: no battle-damage assessment, imagery, casualty report, denial or confirmation has been published by CENTCOM or by the Bahraini, Kuwaiti or Jordanian governments for the July 31 strikes."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "That the IRGC claim is wartime propaganda — Iran has repeatedly claimed base damage that no subsequent evidence corroborated — and that CENTCOM's silence on personnel reflects standard practice of not responding point-by-point to adversary claims rather than any concealed casualty.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "Iran's Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps, claiming three F-35s destroyed and US personnel killed",
        "U.S. Central Command, categorically rejecting the aircraft claim and asserting full interception",
        "The Washington Times, reporting the specific gap between CENTCOM's denial on aircraft and its silence on personnel"
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "Satellite or press imagery of the flight line at Al Azraq/Muwaffaq Salti showing intact or damaged airframes",
        "A DoD casualty announcement, or an explicit CENTCOM statement addressing the personnel claim",
        "A Jordanian government statement on damage or casualties at the base",
        "Restoration and reconciliation of the Pentagon's casualty categories, which would make the absence of a reported casualty meaningful",
        "An Iranian release of targeting or battle-damage imagery capable of independent verification"
      ],
      "current_assessment": "Unresolvable from open sources as of August 1, 2026, and now broader than the original F-35 claim. Iran has issued specific, checkable target descriptions for four bases in three countries and asserted significant damage; the United States denied only the single most falsifiable claim (three F-35s destroyed at Al Azraq) and has otherwise been silent, and the Gulf host governments have consistently declined to detail what Iranian attacks cost them. Absence of denial is not corroboration and absence of imagery is not refutation. Damage on host-nation soil is not independently observable, and every party with access has an interest in the account it gives. Only released imagery, a formal battle-damage assessment, or host-government disclosure would resolve it.",
      "first_listed": "2026-07-31"
    },
    {
      "id": "china-manpads-transfer-iran-july-2026",
      "title": "Is China shipping Iran up to 400 shoulder-fired air-defence launchers? Reuters reports a signed $60-70m contract with delivery in weeks; Beijing calls the report 'completely groundless' and Islamabad calls the transit claim 'absolutely concocted and false.'",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "FIRST LISTED — July 30: Reuters reported on July 28-29, citing three sources familiar with the deal, that Iran is expected to receive within weeks the first shipment of 300-400 Chinese-made man-portable air-defence systems (QW-12 and FN-16 infrared-guided launchers) under a contract worth $60-70 million, signed with the Hong Kong-based intermediary Zhongqing Baoshang International Investment and routed from Urumqi in western China through Pakistan. China's foreign ministry denied it outright — 'The relevant reports are completely groundless. China has consistently played a role in promoting peace and ending the conflict' — and Pakistan's military called any transit role 'absolutely concocted and false.' The intermediary's parent group did not respond to requests for comment. No interdiction, imagery, manifest, delivery or sanctions designation has been reported by any party.",
      "synopsis": "If accurate, this would be the first concrete Chinese military resupply of Iran during the 2026 war and the largest known Iranian effort to rebuild the short-range air defences the conflict exposed as inadequate. It would also sit directly athwart a functioning US-China diplomatic track — Xi Jinping's White House visit is still expected around September 24 and USTR has extended tariff exclusions on 178 Chinese products to November 10 — and would recast Beijing from commercial competitor to material supporter of a US adversary in an active shooting war. Two governments named in the reported transit chain deny it categorically. The reporting is specific enough to be checkable in principle (named systems, named intermediary, named air corridor, a delivery window of weeks) and entirely uncheckable in practice from open sources.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "July 28-29, 2026: Reuters reported, citing three sources familiar with the deal, that Iran is expected to receive within weeks the first shipment of up to 400 Chinese-made shoulder-fired air-defence missile launchers.",
        "The reported contract covers 300 to 400 man-portable air-defence systems (MANPADS), specifically Chinese-made QW-12 and FN-16 infrared-guided surface-to-air missiles designed to engage low-flying aircraft, helicopters and drones.",
        "The reported value is $60-70 million, described as one of Tehran's largest known efforts to strengthen short-range air defences since the outbreak of the war with the US and Israel exposed gaps in its ability to protect military sites and strategic infrastructure.",
        "The deal was reportedly signed with Zhongqing Baoshang International Investment, a Hong Kong-based company acting as intermediary between the Iranian side and the Chinese supplier; the parent company, Zhong Qing Bao Shang Group, did not immediately respond to requests for comment.",
        "The reported route would fly the systems from Urumqi in western China through Pakistan to Iran, with the mode of the final leg unspecified.",
        "China's Ministry of Foreign Affairs denied the report: 'The relevant reports are completely groundless. China has consistently played a role in promoting peace and ending the conflict.'",
        "Pakistan's military rejected any involvement, calling such claims 'absolutely concocted and false.'",
        "The report was carried by U.S. News & World Report, The Times of Israel, Japan Times, Business Recorder, Iran International and China Global South.",
        "No interdiction, flight record, manifest, imagery of the systems in Iranian service, sanctions designation naming the transaction, or on-record Western intelligence assessment has been published by any government.",
        "Context documented separately: The Intercept reported on July 28 that Iran has fired 8,000-9,000 drones and missiles since February and that Patriot and THAAD interceptor stocks are severely depleted — an environment in which Iranian acquisition of short-range air defence would be operationally significant to both sides."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "The mainline reading is that the reporting is accurate: a state-adjacent Chinese supplier is moving a modest, deniable, high-utility air-defence package to Iran through a commercial cut-out and a third-country corridor, precisely because MANPADS are small, cheap, hard to attribute and directly responsive to Iran's demonstrated vulnerability to US and Israeli air operations — and because Beijing can sustain the denial indefinitely. The counter-reading, asserted by both named governments, is that the report is false: no such transfer is occurring, the intermediary is a commercial entity being used to lend specificity to thin sourcing, and the story serves the interests of parties who want China implicated in the war — with Pakistan's denial notable because it has no stake in shielding Beijing and an active mediation role between Washington and Tehran to protect. A third reading, neither asserted nor excluded, is that a contract exists but delivery does not, whether because it was never executed or because it has been interdicted or deferred.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "Reuters, in an exclusive citing three sources familiar with the deal, carried by U.S. News & World Report, The Times of Israel, Japan Times, Business Recorder, Iran International and China Global South",
        "China's Ministry of Foreign Affairs, denying the report as 'completely groundless' and asserting China 'has consistently played a role in promoting peace and ending the conflict'",
        "Pakistan's military, denying any transit or facilitation role as 'absolutely concocted and false'",
        "Defence analysts quoted in the reporting, describing the operational value of portable air-defence systems for protecting military sites, energy infrastructure and strategic facilities through rapid small-team deployment"
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "Interdiction or seizure of a shipment, or a publicly announced inspection of an aircraft or cargo on the reported Urumqi-Pakistan-Iran corridor",
        "Imagery, video or battlefield recovery of QW-12 or FN-16 systems in Iranian service, or an Iranian claim of a shoot-down attributable to them",
        "A US, UK or EU sanctions designation naming Zhongqing Baoshang International Investment or the transaction",
        "An on-the-record US intelligence community assessment, congressional testimony or State Department statement confirming or contradicting the transfer",
        "A Chinese or Iranian acknowledgement of a defence-supply agreement, or documentary evidence of the contract",
        "An IAEA-style third-party monitoring finding or UN Panel of Experts report addressing the transfer"
      ],
      "current_assessment": "Unresolvable from open sources as of July 30, 2026, and structurally so. A covert arms transfer routed through a commercial intermediary and a third-country air corridor produces an identical public signature whether it is real or invented: anonymous sourcing with specific detail, a non-responsive corporate cut-out, and categorical denials from every named state. This project records the report and both denials without adopting either, and treats the transfer as the single most consequential unresolved question in the US-China file — because if confirmed it would convert a managed trade rivalry into indirect Chinese participation in an active war against US forces, on the eve of a scheduled Xi state visit.",
      "first_listed": "2026-07-30"
    },
    {
      "id": "us-saudi-iraq-strikes-pmf-status-july-2026",
      "title": "The July 28 joint US-Saudi strikes in Iraq — precision strikes on IRGC-directed militia logistics, or an attack on Iraq's own official state security institutions? The Popular Mobilisation Forces are formally both.",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "FIRST LISTED — July 30: CENTCOM announced that it 'and the Saudi Arabian Armed Forces conducted precision strikes in Iraq, July 28, against Iran-aligned terrorists that the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) directed to attack U.S. forces and Saudi energy infrastructure,' hitting multiple logistics hubs and weapons storage sites after more than 30 IRGC-directed drone attacks in 72 hours. Iraq's Popular Mobilisation Forces — a body legally incorporated into the Iraqi state security structure — said at least 20 of its members were killed and 32 wounded and called the strikes a violation of Iraqi sovereignty and of 'official security institutions.' The Iraqi presidency 'categorically rejected any attacks in Iraqi territory' while also opposing 'the use of Iraqi territory as a launching point,' and Prime Minister Ali al-Zaidi convened an emergency security meeting. No target list or battle-damage assessment has been published.",
      "synopsis": "The strikes are the first Saudi Arabia has claimed inside Iraq since the war began and the first significant US kinetic action since the July 24 halt of strikes on Iranian territory. Whether they hit Iranian-directed proxy infrastructure or elements of Iraq's own legally constituted security apparatus is not a semantic question — it determines whether this was a counter-proxy operation or an act against a third state's institutions, and it bears directly on whether Baghdad remains a permissive host for US forces. The PMF's dual character makes both characterisations defensible from the same facts, and neither government has published the evidence that would separate them.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "July 28, 2026: CENTCOM public release: 'U.S. Central Command and the Saudi Arabian Armed Forces conducted precision strikes in Iraq, July 28, against Iran-aligned terrorists that the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) directed to attack U.S. forces and Saudi energy infrastructure.'",
        "CENTCOM said the targets were multiple logistics hubs and weapons storage sites used by the armed groups, and cited more than 30 IRGC-directed aerial drone attacks in the preceding 72 hours, none of which succeeded, plus more than 600 attempted attacks on US personnel and facilities between February and April 2026.",
        "Saudi Maj. Gen. Turki Al-Maliki confirmed Saudi coordination with CENTCOM, said the strikes targeted militia positions directly linked to cross-border drone attacks, and said Saudi air defences had 'intercepted and destroyed multiple drones launched from Iraqi territory aimed at vital petroleum facilities in the Kingdom's Eastern Province and Riyadh region.'",
        "Saudi Arabia's defence ministry invoked Article 51 of the UN Charter, asserted its 'right to self-defence,' said the Kingdom 'does not seek escalation but will respond to any aggression it faces,' and reserved the right to respond 'at the appropriate time and place.'",
        "This is the first time Riyadh has claimed responsibility for launching strikes inside Iraq since the start of the Iran-US war.",
        "Iraq's Popular Mobilisation Forces said at least 20 of its members were killed and 32 wounded, and condemned the strikes as 'a highly dangerous escalation' and as violations of Iraqi sovereignty and of official security institutions.",
        "Strike sites were reported across Baghdad, Wasit, Nineveh, Basra, Kirkuk, Karbala and Diyala provinces.",
        "The Iraqi presidency 'categorically rejected any attacks in Iraqi territory' while also opposing 'the use of Iraqi territory as a launching point'; Prime Minister Ali al-Zaidi convened an emergency security meeting.",
        "Iran's foreign ministry condemned the strikes as 'aggressive attacks' violating Iraq's 'national sovereignty and territorial integrity.'",
        "Not documented: any target list, independent battle-damage assessment, identification of which specific units or facilities were struck in each named province, or independent verification of the casualty figures or the affiliations of the dead."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "The mainline reading, asserted by CENTCOM and Riyadh, is that the strikes hit the logistics and weapons infrastructure of Iranian-directed factions that operate inside but not on behalf of the Iraqi state, that the drone campaign against US bases and Saudi oil facilities originated from those sites, and that Article 51 self-defence applies to the launch points regardless of the nominal legal status of the groups occupying them. The counter-reading, asserted by the PMF, the Iraqi presidency and Tehran, is that the dead were members of a force legally incorporated into Iraq's security architecture, that striking their facilities is an attack on Iraqi state institutions rather than on a proxy, and that the operation therefore violates Iraqi sovereignty irrespective of who directed the drones. The Iraqi presidency's own formulation splits the difference — rejecting attacks on Iraqi territory while also rejecting its use as a launching point — which concedes the American factual premise while denying the legal conclusion.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "U.S. Central Command, in its July 28 public release characterising the targets as IRGC-directed terrorist logistics and weapons sites",
        "Saudi Arabia's Ministry of Defence and Maj. Gen. Turki Al-Maliki, characterising the operation as Article 51 self-defence against drones aimed at Saudi petroleum facilities",
        "Iraq's Popular Mobilisation Forces, characterising the dead as its own members and the strikes as an attack on official Iraqi security institutions and a 'highly dangerous escalation'",
        "The Iraqi presidency and Prime Minister Ali al-Zaidi, rejecting attacks on Iraqi territory while opposing its use as a launching point",
        "Iran's foreign ministry, characterising the strikes as aggression violating Iraq's national sovereignty and territorial integrity",
        "Gulf analysts writing in The Jerusalem Post, characterising the operation as a milestone in US-Saudi military cooperation"
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "Publication of the target list, or an independent battle-damage assessment identifying the struck facilities and their occupants in each of the seven named provinces",
        "An Iraqi parliamentary or judicial inquiry naming the units and facilities hit and the affiliations of the dead",
        "A UN or third-party investigation into the strikes and their legal basis",
        "An Iraqi government decision on the status of US forces or basing arrangements, which would reveal how Baghdad has adjudicated the question in practice",
        "Independent verification of the PMF casualty figures and the command relationships of the struck elements",
        "A subsequent Saudi operation or a public Saudi commitment not to repeat one, which would distinguish a posture change from a one-off self-defence action"
      ],
      "current_assessment": "Unresolvable from open sources as of July 30, 2026. The Popular Mobilisation Forces' dual legal status — an Iraqi state security institution containing factions that operate under Iranian direction — means both characterisations can be simultaneously accurate about different elements at the same sites, and neither Washington, Riyadh nor Baghdad has published target lists or damage assessments. This project records both the joint attribution and the sovereignty objection without resolving which describes the operation, and notes separately that the strikes are documented evidence of a US escalation delivered by air against a proxy on third-state territory rather than by ground forces against Iran — which is the ground-invasion-tracker significance regardless of how the target question resolves.",
      "first_listed": "2026-07-30"
    },
    {
      "id": "selective-service-rule-withdrawal-july-2026",
      "title": "The Selective Service's July 23 withdrawal of its automatic draft-registration rule, set against an unchanged statutory start date in December 2026 and a mobilisation 'Readiness Simulation' procurement that closed in August with no announced award — a political retreat from anything resembling a draft, a procedural reset alongside continuing preparation, or two separate instruments running on two separate tracks?",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "UPDATED — August 23: THE STATUTE AND THE RULE HAVE VISIBLY DECOUPLED, AND THE PROJECT NOW TREATS THEM AS TWO TRACKS RATHER THAN ONE INDICATOR. Reporting from April through August 2026 — The Hill, CNN, CNBC, UPI, Military Times, Military.com, Time and, on AUGUST 4, Black Voice News — continues to carry a start date for AUTOMATIC SELECTIVE SERVICE REGISTRATION under a NATIONAL DEFENSE AUTHORIZATION ACT provision that integrates federal databases, with the date given as DECEMBER 18, 2026 in most accounts and DECEMBER 19, 2026 in others, and the covered cohort given variously as MEN AGED 18 THROUGH 25 and 18 THROUGH 26. Meanwhile the SELECTIVE SERVICE SYSTEM'S OWN AUTOMATIC-REGISTRATION RULE, withdrawn on JULY 23, 2026 after the White House held it for nearly four months, HAS STILL NOT BEEN RE-PROPOSED and no explanation has been issued. The 'READINESS SIMULATION' VENDOR WINDOW CLOSED IN AUGUST WITH NO AWARD ANNOUNCED. The audit's position is that continued reporting of a December date is NOT evidence that the withdrawn rule has returned: a statutory effective date and an agency rule are distinct instruments and can move independently, and the open record does not say whether the agency is implementing on one track, two, or none. WHAT HAS NOT CHANGED IS DECISIVE: automatic registration BUILDS THE POOL AND INDUCTS NOBODY. Conscription requires Congress to amend the MILITARY SELECTIVE SERVICE ACT and the President to authorise inductions; neither has occurred, been requested or been scheduled, and no lottery, classification or induction machinery has been activated. || UPDATED — August 2: The two sub-indicators have now moved in opposite directions, which is why this question has hardened rather than resolved. REGULATORY SUB-RUNG, DOWN: the rule withdrawn on July 23, 2026 after the White House held it for nearly four months has still not been re-proposed and no explanation has been given by either the agency or the White House. ADMINISTRATIVE SUB-RUNG, UP: the Selective Service System has published a federal contracting request for a government-owned 'Readiness Simulation' — a model capable of testing staffing, processing and mobilisation scenarios across multiple draft scenarios before a national emergency — with vendor submissions due August 14, 2026. Military Times characterised this as the agency wanting to 'wargame the draft'; the agency's own framing is that the contract does not announce a draft and prepares for the possibility that Congress and the President could authorise conscription in a future emergency. The December 19, 2026 statutory start of automatic registration is unchanged. || FIRST LISTED — July 30: On July 23, 2026 the Selective Service System withdrew the proposed rules for 'automatic' registration of young men for a military draft that it had submitted to the White House for review on March 30, 2026. The White House held the proposal for nearly four months without acting on it and issued no public statement on the withdrawal or on implementation plans. The statutory mandate is unchanged: legislation Congress enacted in December 2025 requires the agency to identify, locate and register potential draftees automatically using federal agency data, with a start date of December 19, 2026. The agency is separately reported to be procuring a draft mobilisation simulator. No draft has been called and none is planned.",
      "synopsis": "This is the first backward movement on the draft indicator since the project began tracking it: every prior data point — the December 2025 NDAA mandate, the March 30 submission, the reported December 2026 implementation date — pointed one direction. A rule withdrawn without explanation, from an agency facing an unchanged statutory deadline and a White House that sat on it for four months, is consistent with two incompatible readings. It matters for prediction scoring because corpus predictions of a US ground war in Iran generally assume or imply mobilisation, and the registration machinery is the earliest observable precursor. It also matters that automatic registration is not itself a draft: it automates the register should one be called.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "March 30, 2026: The Selective Service System submitted proposed rules for automatic draft registration to the White House for review.",
        "July 23, 2026: The Selective Service System withdrew the proposed rules. The White House had held the proposal for nearly four months without acting on it.",
        "No public statement was issued by the White House or the agency regarding the withdrawal or subsequent implementation plans.",
        "Congress enacted legislation in December 2025 requiring the SSS to identify, locate and register potential draftees 'automatically' using federal agency data, with regulations required to 'reflect this statutory change.'",
        "The statutory deadline for automatic registration to begin is December 19, 2026, and is unchanged by the withdrawal.",
        "The agency faces a compressed timeline to complete multiple administrative proceedings before that deadline, or Congress must postpone the effective date or rescind the requirement.",
        "July 28, 2026: Separate reporting stated the Selective Service is seeking a draft mobilisation simulator as automatic registration nears.",
        "Automatic registration does not activate a draft; it automates the register should a draft be called. No draft has been called, no draft lottery has been scheduled, no classification or induction machinery has been activated, and there are no formal plans to reinstate conscription.",
        "Contextual fact documented separately: Reuters/Ipsos found on July 27 that 33% of Americans approve of US attacks on Iran against 62% disapproving, with nearly 70% saying Trump has not clearly explained the war's goals and overall approval at 37%.",
        "The Selective Service System published a federal contracting request for a government-owned 'Readiness Simulation' capable of testing staffing, processing and mobilisation scenarios before automatic registration begins, with vendor submissions due August 14, 2026.",
        "Military Times reported the procurement on July 24, 2026 under the headline 'Selective Service System wants to wargame the draft.'",
        "The contracting request does not announce that a draft has been ordered; it is stated to help the agency prepare for the possibility that Congress and the President could authorise conscription during a future national emergency.",
        "As of August 2, 2026 the withdrawn automatic-registration rule has not been re-proposed and no explanation has been issued.",
        "Reinstating conscription continues to require authorisation from both Congress and the President during a declared national emergency; no draft has been called, no lottery scheduled, and no classification or induction machinery activated.",
        "April through August 2026: The Hill, CNN Politics, CNBC, UPI, Military Times, Military.com, Time and WBRC all report that automatic Selective Service registration begins in December 2026 under a National Defense Authorization Act provision replacing self-registration with registration by the agency using other federal databases.",
        "The reported start date is given as December 18, 2026 in most accounts and as December 19, 2026 in others; the covered cohort is reported variously as male US residents aged 18 through 25 and men aged 18 to 26, in some accounts explicitly including refugees, green-card holders, asylum seekers and undocumented men.",
        "August 4, 2026: Black Voice News published 'National Automatic Draft Registration Set to Begin in December 2026,' restating the December 18, 2026 date and attributing it to the Defense Authorization Act provision.",
        "As of August 23, 2026 the Selective Service System's own automatic-registration rule, withdrawn on July 23, 2026, has not been re-proposed and no explanation has been issued by the agency or the White House.",
        "The 'Readiness Simulation' vendor proposal window closed in August 2026 with no award announced.",
        "Reinstating conscription continues to require Congress to amend the Military Selective Service Act and the President to authorise inductions. Neither step has occurred, been requested or been scheduled. No draft has been called, no lottery scheduled, and no classification or induction machinery activated.",
        "No call-up of the Individual Ready Reserve and no mobilisation of Reserve or National Guard combat formations for an Iran ground contingency has been reported."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "The mainline reading, asserted by Antiwar.com and echoed in commentary, is that the withdrawal is a political retreat: the White House recognised that visible movement toward a draft during an unpopular war would inflame opposition, sat on the rule until the agency withdrew it, and has no intention of advancing it before the midterms. The counter-reading is procedural: an agency that cannot get White House clearance withdraws and re-files rather than leaving a stalled proposal on the docket, the December 19 statutory deadline is unchanged and binding, the parallel procurement of a mobilisation simulator shows preparation rather than abandonment, and a re-proposal should therefore be expected. A third possibility, neither asserted nor excluded, is that the administration intends to let the deadline lapse and seek a legislative postponement without saying so. The August 2 development sharpens the split rather than resolving it: the same agency is simultaneously not advancing the rule it withdrew and procuring the capability to simulate a mobilisation, on a vendor timeline (August 14) that sits four months ahead of the statutory registration date. The retreat reading must explain the procurement; the procedural reading must explain the continuing absence of a re-proposed rule. AUGUST 23 ADDITION: a third structural reading has now become the most economical one and the project adopts it as its framing without adopting a verdict — that the statutory effective date and the agency rule are simply two different instruments on two different tracks, and that observers have been treating continued reporting of the December date as though it spoke to the fate of the withdrawn rule when it does not. On this framing the withdrawal tells us about the White House's appetite for a visible rulemaking during an unpopular war, while the December date tells us only what Congress legislated in an NDAA. The two can coexist, one can quietly override the other, or the date can arrive with no implementing rule in place — an outcome the record does not currently distinguish. The date discrepancy in the reporting itself (December 18 versus December 19) and the inconsistent age cohort (18-25 versus 18-26) are logged as a caution about how thinly this indicator is sourced.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "Antiwar.com, characterising the withdrawal as 'a sign that the White House has recognized that moving toward a draft will be politically costly' while cautioning that 'automatic draft registration is not yet a done deal'",
        "NaturalNews, reporting the withdrawal of the proposed rules",
        "[your]NEWS, reporting the agency's parallel procurement of a draft mobilisation simulator 'as automatic registration nears'",
        "CNBC and CNN Politics, which reported the December 2026 implementation plan in April and continue to state that automatic registration does not activate a draft",
        "Fast Company, addressing and rejecting circulating rumours of an imminent 2026 draft",
        "Military Times, reporting the readiness-simulation procurement as the agency wanting to 'wargame the draft'",
        "The Daily Caller, reporting that 'fears of a US draft grow as the Selective Service modernizes'",
        "The Selective Service System, whose contracting framing states the simulation does not announce a draft and prepares for a possible future authorisation",
        "The Hill, CNN Politics, CNBC, UPI, Military.com, Time and WBRC, reporting a December 2026 start for automatic registration under the NDAA provision",
        "Black Voice News, in an August 4, 2026 report restating the December 18, 2026 start date",
        "The Friends Committee on National Legislation, arguing that automatic draft registration is a problem in itself regardless of whether a draft follows",
        "Fact-checking outlets and the Selective Service System, stressing that registration is not induction and that no draft is being reinstated"
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "A re-proposed rule submitted to the White House or published in the Federal Register, which would establish the withdrawal as procedural",
        "An administration request to Congress to postpone or rescind the December 19, 2026 effective date, which would establish it as a retreat",
        "An on-the-record statement from the White House or the Selective Service System explaining the withdrawal",
        "The passage of December 19, 2026 with or without automatic registration in effect",
        "Congressional testimony or an appropriations record showing whether implementation funding and staffing are being sought or wound down",
        "Any Selective Service action on classification, lottery or induction machinery, which would move the question out of the registration domain entirely",
        "The award or cancellation of the Readiness Simulation contract after the August 14, 2026 vendor deadline, and whether implementation funding follows",
        "Whether the simulation's stated scope remains confined to registration and processing or extends to classification, lottery or induction",
        "A Federal Register entry, agency implementation notice or public guidance giving effect to the December 2026 statutory date, which would establish that the statute is proceeding without the withdrawn rule",
        "An authoritative statement resolving whether the effective date is December 18 or December 19, 2026 and whether the cohort is 18-25 or 18-26 — a small discrepancy that indicates how thinly the indicator is sourced",
        "The arrival of the December date with or without automatic registration operating in practice"
      ],
      "current_assessment": "Unresolvable from open sources as of August 23, 2026, and now understood as a two-track question rather than a single indicator. The regulatory track remains DOWN: the rule withdrawn on July 23 has not been re-proposed and the Readiness Simulation window closed with no award announced. The statutory track is reported as UNCHANGED, with automatic registration set to begin in December 2026 under an NDAA provision — though the reporting itself disagrees on the exact date and cohort, which is a caution rather than a finding. The project declines to net the two tracks into a single direction and declines to treat continued reporting of the December date as evidence that the withdrawn rule has returned. For prediction-scoring purposes corpus predictions of imminent US ground mobilisation remain DISCONFIRMED on the execution test: registration builds a database, induction requires an act of Congress and a presidential order, and neither exists. December 2026 remains the next scheduled test. || Unresolvable from open sources as of August 2, 2026, and now internally split. The project records the draft rung as having moved in two directions in the same cycle: the regulatory sub-rung stays down (rule withdrawn, not re-proposed, unexplained) while the administrative sub-rung rose (mobilisation simulator procured, vendor submissions due August 14). Neither is a draft. For prediction-scoring purposes corpus predictions of imminent US ground mobilisation remain DISCONFIRMED on the execution test, and the audit declines to net the two sub-indicators to a single direction. December 19, 2026 remains the next scheduled test. || Unresolvable from open sources as of July 30, 2026, because the only actor who could distinguish the readings — the White House — has said nothing. The project records the withdrawal as the first backward movement on the draft rung of this war while stressing what has not changed: the December 19, 2026 statutory deadline stands, automatic registration was never a draft, and no draft has been called or planned. For prediction-scoring purposes the withdrawal is treated as evidence against, not for, imminent US ground mobilisation, and the December deadline is flagged as the next scheduled test.",
      "first_listed": "2026-07-30"
    },
    {
      "id": "netanyahu-pickaxe-intelligence-escalation-july-2026",
      "title": "Did Netanyahu bring Pickaxe Mountain intelligence to the July 28 White House meeting, and is Israel working to pull the United States back into escalation? Israel's own public-diplomacy chief calls the intelligence story 'fake news'; Trump says 'we'll have to take out Pickaxe.'",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "A government contradicting its own briefed story within hours, about a closed-door meeting — July 29: Pre-meeting reporting across Israeli and US outlets held that Netanyahu would present Trump with updated Israeli intelligence on Pickaxe Mountain, the fortified site near Natanz to which Israeli assessments say Iran has moved centrifuges and enriched uranium. Trump pre-emptively dismissed it: 'I don't need Bibi to tell me that... Bibi's telling me that because he wants me to stay involved.' After the meeting Tzipi Hotovely, head of Israel's National Public Diplomacy Directorate, said Pickaxe did not come up and that 'the idea that we have some intelligence here is a sort of fake news that ran throughout the day' — while Trump said publicly, 'we'll have to take out Pickaxe.' Reporters were excluded from the meeting and no substantive readout was issued.",
      "synopsis": "The first Trump-Netanyahu meeting since the war began was preceded by a coordinated-looking Israeli briefing campaign about Iranian nuclear reconstitution at Pickaxe Mountain, publicly rebuffed by the President, and followed by an Israeli official statement that the intelligence story was fabricated and the subject never arose. Both a genuine warning and a lobbying operation would produce the same public trace: leaked pre-briefs, a warm joint characterisation of a closed session, and a subsequent denial that the sensitive item was discussed. The substantive question underneath is whether Iran is reconstituting its enrichment programme inside Pickaxe Mountain — which would justify the warning — or whether Israeli assessments are, as senior US officials put it, 'seeing what it wants to see.' Neither the intelligence nor the meeting record is public. The question matters to this project because it is the principal live mechanism by which the US air campaign could resume, and because Iran has been publicly asserting since July 26 that Israel is pushing Washington to restart strikes.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "July 28, 2026: Netanyahu met Trump at the White House — their first meeting since the war began on February 28 — arriving just before 11 a.m.; the closed-door session ran a little over an hour with Vice President JD Vance, Secretary of State Marco Rubio and Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent present. Reporters were excluded.",
        "July 28, 2026: Pre-meeting reporting (Ynetnews, Jewish Insider, The Times of Israel, Reuters) held that Netanyahu would present updated intelligence on Iran's efforts to rebuild its missile, drone and nuclear capabilities, centred on the fortified underground facility at Pickaxe Mountain near Natanz, to which Israeli assessments say Iran has transferred centrifuges and nuclear equipment and where Israel fears Tehran is moving its remaining enriched-uranium stockpile.",
        "July 28, 2026: Trump, on Fox before the meeting: 'I don't need Bibi to tell me that... I know exactly what's going on there,' and 'Bibi's telling me that because he wants me to stay involved,' asking 'Why didn't you just tell it to me, why did you have to announce it to the world?' On 'Fox & Friends' he said the US could take out Pickaxe Mountain 'very easily' and that Space Force has state-of-the-art cameras trained on the site, calling it 'not a big problem.'",
        "July 28, 2026: After the meeting, Tzipi Hotovely, head of Israel's National Public Diplomacy Directorate, said Pickaxe Mountain did not come up in the Netanyahu-Trump meeting and that 'the idea that we have some intelligence here is a sort of fake news that ran throughout the day.'",
        "July 28, 2026: Trump said after the meeting, 'We took out their nuclear sites and we'll have to take out Pickaxe. If we don't make a deal, we don't make a deal. We'll take it very easily' — indicating the site was on his mind whether or not it was formally raised.",
        "July 28, 2026: Senior US administration officials described the Israeli intelligence collection as 'amazing' but criticised its interpretation, saying Israel 'sees what it wants to see' and was 'not assessing the intelligence objectively,' giving insufficient weight to the possibility that Iran may genuinely be prepared to compromise; officials described Netanyahu as 'seeking another escalation against Iran, even if he avoids saying so explicitly because he does not want to appear to be pushing for war.'",
        "July 28, 2026: A senior Israeli source said the leaders 'held an excellent and comprehensive discussion on the key issues of the day, first and foremost Iran' and 'reaffirmed their ironclad commitment to ensuring that Iran never has nuclear weapons'; press secretary Karoline Leavitt called the meeting 'positive and productive'; Netanyahu called it 'one of the best we've ever had.'",
        "July 26, 2026: Iran publicly asserted that Israel is pushing the United States to resume strikes, threatening to expand the war if it does (The Times of Israel, 'Iran threatens to expand war if US resumes strikes, which it says Israel is pushing for').",
        "Standing: Israel did not participate in the July air campaign against Iran, which ran thirteen consecutive nights from July 7-8 before Trump halted it on July 24."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "The mainline reading is that Netanyahu brought genuine intelligence about Iranian nuclear reconstitution at Pickaxe Mountain, that Trump found the public framing of it presumptuous rather than the substance wrong, and that Hotovely's denial was ordinary damage control after the President objected to the leak. The counter-reading, asserted by senior US officials on background and by Iran publicly, is that the Pickaxe campaign is a lever rather than a warning — an effort to re-engage American air power at the precise moment a diplomatic off-ramp opened, with the intelligence interpreted to fit that end. A third reading takes Hotovely at her word: that no such intelligence was presented and the entire pre-meeting story was media confection, in which case the public record of two governments' briefings misdescribed the meeting's agenda before it happened.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "Ynetnews, Jewish Insider, The Times of Israel and Reuters, reporting that Netanyahu would present Pickaxe Mountain intelligence to Trump",
        "Tzipi Hotovely, head of Israel's National Public Diplomacy Directorate, saying Pickaxe did not come up and calling the intelligence story 'a sort of fake news'",
        "President Trump, attributing motive to Netanyahu ('he wants me to stay involved') and separately stating 'we'll have to take out Pickaxe'",
        "Unnamed senior US administration officials, saying Israel 'sees what it wants to see' and that Netanyahu is 'seeking another escalation against Iran'",
        "Iran's foreign ministry, asserting since July 26 that Israel is pushing Washington to resume strikes"
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "Publication of the Israeli intelligence assessment on Pickaxe Mountain, or an authoritative US intelligence community judgment on Iranian activity at the site",
        "An IAEA finding or inspection report covering Pickaxe Mountain and the disposition of Iran's remaining enriched-uranium stockpile",
        "A substantive readout, transcript or contemporaneous account of the July 28 Oval Office meeting from either government",
        "A US strike on Pickaxe Mountain, which would demonstrate that the threat assessment was operative regardless of who supplied it",
        "An on-the-record Israeli statement reconciling Hotovely's denial with the pre-meeting briefings attributed to Israeli officials"
      ],
      "current_assessment": "Unresolvable from open sources as of July 29, 2026. The observable facts are that Israel's messaging on Pickaxe Mountain contradicted itself within a single day, that the President publicly attributed a self-interested motive to the Israeli Prime Minister and then adopted the target anyway, and that senior US officials on background characterise Israel as pressing for escalation. None of that settles whether Iran is reconstituting enrichment at the site. This project records the contradiction without resolving it, and treats Pickaxe Mountain as the most likely named target of any US resumption — an AIR target, which is the relevant fact for the ground-invasion tracker.",
      "first_listed": "2026-07-29"
    },
    {
      "id": "us-iran-direct-talks-existence-july-2026",
      "title": "Are the United States and Iran actually negotiating? Trump says 'we are in very deep talks with Iran'; Tehran says it 'currently have no negotiations with the United States.'",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "THE DENIAL HARDENS INTO A DATED CLAIM AND THE HORMUZ CHANNEL BREAKS DOWN — July 30: On July 29 deputy foreign minister Kazem Gharibabadi said 'there was no begging from the Iranian delegation to continue the negotiations,' and Tehran stated it had not requested direct negotiations with Washington in FIFTEEN DAYS — a specific, dated denial rather than a general one. On the same day Iran REJECTED the Gulf-backed Omani proposal outright and submitted a counterplan demanding one shipping lane entirely within Iranian territorial waters plus part of the other, oversight of both inbound and outbound traffic, and a reported $1 million per ship 'service fee'; Gharibabadi tied it to war aims — 'If the Strait of Hormuz returns to its previous state, our success in this war is not complete' — and argued the June 17 MOU obliged Iran to provide free safe passage 'for only 60 days.' He also declared any third-party mine-clearing vessel near the Strait a 'legitimate target.' Reporting now describes US-Iran negotiations as STALLED. The channel whose existence Washington asserted and Tehran denied has, on the only substantive matter it was carrying, produced a documented rejection — which is itself evidence that proposals were being conveyed, without establishing that either side calls it a negotiation. || THE DENIAL HELD A SECOND DAY AND HARDENED — July 29: On July 28 Iranian deputy foreign minister Kazem Gharibabadi again said Tehran has not requested negotiations with the United States and has no current plans to negotiate directly with Washington, while continuing Iran-Oman talks on a Hormuz transit mechanism and saying Iran will take 'any action' to maintain control of the Strait. Oman has carried a Gulf-backed proposal to Washington; a person familiar with the US position said the proposals as delivered contain no mandatory transit tolls — an American characterisation of the content of an Iranian-facing proposal, which is itself evidence of a functioning channel that Tehran does not concede is a negotiation. || A flat contradiction on the record between the two governments about whether a negotiation exists — July 28: On July 27 President Trump told Axios the United States is 'in very deep talks with Iran' and told reporters he saw 'a good chance' of a deal, while Iranian foreign ministry spokesman Esmail Baqaei said Iran 'currently have no negotiations with the United States' and that the week's discussions were 'exclusively with Oman.' Both statements were made the same day. Mediators from Qatar, Pakistan and Oman are separately reported to be carrying messages.",
      "synopsis": "During the third day of a two-sided halt in strikes, Washington and Tehran gave irreconcilable public accounts of the same week. Trump described 'very deep talks with Iran' and warned that 'if they don't work out, we will go back to very strong military action.' Iran's foreign ministry denied any negotiation with the United States and characterised its only talks as bilateral consultations with Oman on Strait of Hormuz transit mechanisms. Iran's deputy foreign minister simultaneously described rejecting US 'extreme demands' — implying a channel that conveys American positions to Tehran without conceding it is a negotiation. Whether this is a semantic dispute over the word 'negotiation,' a domestic-audience management problem on one or both sides, or a genuine difference about what is occurring cannot be settled from public reporting.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "July 28, 2026: Iranian deputy foreign minister Kazem Gharibabadi said Iran will take 'any action' to maintain control of the Strait of Hormuz and that Tehran has not requested negotiations with the United States, with no current plans to negotiate directly with Washington.",
        "July 28, 2026: Oman has presented Iran with a Gulf-backed proposal for joint regional management of the Strait and carried it to Washington; a person familiar with the US position said the proposals as delivered do not include mandatory Hormuz transit tolls. US negotiators are reported to have 'concerns' but to be confident differences can be resolved at a technical level.",
        "July 28, 2026: Iran and Oman continued talks on establishing shipping mechanisms through the Strait.",
        "July 27, 2026: Trump told Axios, 'We are in very deep talks with Iran. If they don't work out, we will go back to very strong military action,' and on the available window, 'Not much time. Either it goes fast or not at all.'",
        "July 27, 2026: Trump told reporters aboard Air Force One, 'I have a lot of patience. I have plenty of time. Plenty of time,' and 'I think there is a good chance that something could happen. If it doesn't, we go back to doing what we were doing two days ago.'",
        "July 27, 2026: Iranian foreign ministry spokesman Esmail Baqaei said Iran 'currently have no negotiations with the United States,' and that 'the discussions we have had over the past few days have been exclusively with Oman.'",
        "July 27, 2026: Iranian deputy foreign minister Kazem Gharibabadi said the United States had presented 'extreme demands' that Iran rejected, describing Tehran's approach as 'dignified diplomacy' — an account that presupposes a channel carrying US positions.",
        "July 26-27, 2026: The Associated Press, citing regional officials, reported that mediators led by Qatar and Pakistan had made significant progress toward bringing the US and Iran back to negotiations and restoring the interim ceasefire deal that collapsed this month.",
        "July 24-26, 2026: Iranian and Omani deputy foreign ministers met in Tehran and exchanged views on 'shared principles and operational mechanisms for managing the safe passage of shipping through the Strait of Hormuz, while respecting the sovereign rights of the two coastal states'; two regional sources told CBS the talks were 'moving in a positive direction.'",
        "July 26, 2026: US Ambassador to the UN Mike Waltz said on Face the Nation that conversations are running 'at every level, from the more technical aspect of it all the way to the highest levels,' and that Oman is 'engaging in a helpful way.'",
        "Not documented by either government: a meeting record, venue, delegation list, agreed agenda, or joint statement of any US-Iran negotiating session in this period."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "Either (a) indirect but substantive US-Iran negotiation is under way through Omani, Qatari and Pakistani intermediaries, and Tehran's denial is a formal position for domestic and regional audiences — refusing to concede that it is negotiating under bombardment and blockade — which Trump is describing accurately if loosely as 'talks'; or (b) there is no negotiation in any meaningful sense, only message-passing plus a genuinely separate Iran-Oman bilateral track on Strait transit mechanics, and the American characterisation overstates the state of play for domestic and market audiences during a pause it needs to justify.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "President Donald Trump, describing 'very deep talks with Iran' and a 'good chance' of a deal",
        "Iranian foreign ministry spokesman Esmail Baqaei, denying any negotiations with the United States and confining the week's discussions to Oman",
        "Iranian deputy foreign minister Kazem Gharibabadi, describing rejected US 'extreme demands'",
        "the Associated Press, citing regional officials on Qatar- and Pakistan-led mediation progress toward restoring negotiations",
        "US Ambassador to the UN Mike Waltz, describing conversations at 'every level' including 'the highest levels'"
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "A published joint statement, agreed agenda, or announced venue and delegation for a US-Iran session",
        "Either government naming a negotiator and a counterpart for a specific meeting",
        "An Omani, Qatari or Pakistani foreign ministry readout that characterises its role as hosting rather than relaying",
        "A concluded or announced agreement whose text identifies its parties",
        "Explicit Iranian acknowledgement of indirect talks with the US, as occurred in earlier phases of this war",
        "A formal US statement that no direct or indirect negotiation is under way, contradicting the president"
      ],
      "current_assessment": "Unresolvable from open sources as of July 29, 2026, and the July 28 record sharpens rather than settles it. Washington is now publicly characterising the contents of proposals travelling between Tehran and the White House via Muscat, and describing its own negotiators' 'concerns' about them — the observable behaviour of a negotiation. Tehran continues to deny that a negotiation exists while conducting the talks that produce those proposals. Mediated message-passing through Oman, Qatar and Pakistan remains observationally indistinguishable from indirect negotiation, and both governments retain domestic incentives to describe the same contacts differently. Only a published meeting record, a joint statement, or an announced venue and delegation would settle it.",
      "first_listed": "2026-07-28"
    },
    {
      "id": "pentagon-casualty-reclassification-july-2026",
      "title": "The Pentagon's removal of four deaths and 207 wounded from the official Iran-war casualty totals into a new 'Overseas Operations' category — a routine accounting correction, or a presentational suppression of the war's visible cost?",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "UPDATED AUGUST 22 — THE PATTERN REPEATED, LARGER AND STILL UNEXPLAINED. On August 21 the Pentagon added 60 wounded to its rolls in a single day, taking the published wounded total from 697 to 757 and total US casualties to 775 (18 killed, 757 wounded), split between Operation Epic Fury (14 killed, 418 wounded since February 28) and 'Overseas Operations' since July 7 (4 killed, 339 wounded). The Department did not say when or where the additional injuries occurred and did not respond to a request for more information. The Intercept had reported on August 4 that 'The Pentagon Found a New Way to Undercount U.S. Casualties in Iran,' and CNN reported on July 26 that the count passed 600 as the Pentagon 'quietly changed accounting.' A large unexplained batch update is equally consistent with ordinary reporting lag and with systematic undercounting subsequently corrected; the two-category structure remains in place, no date for Epic Fury's formal conclusion has been published, and no casualty-reporting guidance has been released. (Stars and Stripes; The Washington Post, August 21, 2026; The Intercept, August 4, 2026.) || THE RATIONALE IS NOW ON THE RECORD; THE DATE AND THE RULE ARE STILL NOT — July 30: A Pentagon official stated that 'because Operation Epic Fury has concluded, the military has decided to classify casualties tied to the latest strikes in the new category,' but WOULD NOT OFFER A DATE for the operation's formal end. The department also put a combined figure on the record for the first time: TOTAL WOUNDED across the Operation Epic Fury and 'Overseas Operations' categories now stands at 642. The four reclassified deaths are identified as the three soldiers killed when Iranian ballistic missiles and drones struck a base in Jordan on July 17 and one killed in Iraq on July 18 during a controlled detonation of an Iranian drone. Marco Rubio's May statement that 'we achieved the objectives of that operation' remains the closest thing to a public end-point. Pentagon spokespeople continue to attribute the data changes to 'anomalies' and 'temporary data disruptions' rather than concealment. Still unpublished: any casualty-reporting guidance, operational order or naming convention establishing the post-July-7 phase as a separate operation, and any date on which Epic Fury ended. An operation cannot be shown to have concluded on a particular day if no day is given, which is precisely the gap that makes the accounting unverifiable. || AN INDEPENDENT ALLEGATION OF SYSTEMATIC UNDERCOUNTING — July 29: The Intercept reported on July 28 that at least nine US outposts across the Middle East have been struck since July 9, several sustaining 'significant' damage, that recent Iranian strikes killed four US service members across multiple bases including three air-defence personnel at Jordan's Muwaffaq Salti Air Base on July 17-18, and that the Pentagon drastically understates casualty figures. That is a second, independent line of assertion alongside the July 26 reclassification itself. The Pentagon has still neither restored the reclassified figures nor published the accounting rule behind the change. || A documented downward revision with an undocumented rule behind it — July 27: The Defense Casualty Analysis System's Iran-war totals fell from 18 killed and 482 wounded to 14 and 420, with four deaths and 207 wounded moved into a newly created 'Overseas Operations' category dated from July 7, 2026. The Pentagon called the changes 'anomalies' and a 'temporary data disruption.' No published casualty-reporting guidance, operational order or naming convention establishing the post-July-7 phase as a separate operation has been produced, and the figures have not been restored.",
      "synopsis": "On July 26, 2026 the Pentagon's official Iran-war death toll dropped from 18 to 14 and its wounded count from 482 to 420, with the difference — four service members killed in the July 17 Jordan base strike and the July 18 Iraq drone detonation, plus 207 wounded — placed in a new 'Overseas Operations' category beginning July 7. The department characterised this as a temporary data anomaly. Reporting and critics read it as of a piece with a pattern of degrading casualty transparency, including the unexplained May 11-12 revision in which the wounded tally rose to 428 then fell to 413.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "July 28, 2026: The Intercept reported that at least nine US outposts across the Middle East have been struck since July 9, with several sustaining 'significant' damage; that recent Iranian strikes killed four US service members across multiple bases, including three air-defence personnel at Jordan's Muwaffaq Salti Air Base on July 17-18; and that the Pentagon drastically understates casualty figures.",
        "July 28, 2026: The same report stated that the United States 'allows missiles through its defenses only in cases where a strike is judged not to imperil U.S. lives,' and that more than 11,000 munitions were expended in the first 16 days of the conflict, with Patriot and THAAD stocks requiring 'three or more years' to restore.",
        "July 28, 2026: The official Defense Casualty Analysis System Iran-war figures remained at 14 killed and 420 wounded, with 4 killed and 207 wounded still held in the separate 'Overseas Operations' category created July 26 and dated from July 7. No restoration and no published accounting rule.",
        "The Pentagon's Defense Casualty Analysis System Iran-war totals fell from 18 killed and 482 wounded to 14 killed and 420 wounded on or about July 26, 2026, with 4 deaths and 207 wounded moved to a new 'Overseas Operations' category dated from July 7, 2026. Seven of the remaining 14 are classified 'hostile.' (The Washington Post; CNBC; The Philadelphia Inquirer/AP; KSAT, July 26, 2026.)",
        "The four reclassified deaths are the three killed in the July 17 Iranian missile-and-drone strike on a base in Jordan and one killed July 18 in a drone detonation in Iraq; Sgt. Angel S. Rampersad of Ozone Park, New York is among them. (The Philadelphia Inquirer/AP, July 26, 2026.)",
        "Pentagon press secretary Joel Valdez characterised the changes as 'anomalies' and 'temporary data disruptions'; spokesman Sean Parnell called it a 'temporary data disruption' on social media. (The Washington Post; AP, July 26, 2026.)",
        "US officials say the actual number of wounded exceeds 500; CBS reported more than 400 service members have been airlifted for treatment since Operation Epic Fury began in February, most for non-combat causes. (The Philadelphia Inquirer/AP; CBS News, July 26, 2026.)",
        "The Pentagon had stated on July 23-24 that nearly 100 service members were injured (mostly minor) since July 7 — materially lower than the 207 later shown in the new category. (CNN; CENTCOM via CNN, July 24, 2026.)",
        "A prior unexplained revision occurred May 11-12, 2026, when the WIA tally rose to 428 then dropped 15 to 413 without explanation; The Intercept published two stories on counting discrepancies (April 22 and May 12, 2026)."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "Either (a) the reclassification reflects a genuine administrative distinction — the post-July-7 resumption is being tracked as a separate contingency operation, and the transfer is a bookkeeping consequence with no presentational intent, as the department's 'temporary data disruption' framing asserts; or (b) it is a presentational choice that removes the war's most politically salient deaths from the headline series at a moment when war approval stands at 35% and 67% of Americans want the conflict ended, continuing a documented pattern of degrading casualty transparency.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "the Department of Defense, via press secretary Joel Valdez and spokesman Sean Parnell, characterising the change as anomalous and temporary",
        "The Washington Post, CNBC and the Associated Press, reporting the change as raising questions about properly counting the impact on US service members",
        "The Intercept and other outlets treating the war's casualty accounting as systematically degrading, on the basis of the April and May 2026 discrepancies"
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "Publication of the DoD casualty-reporting guidance or operational order establishing the post-July-7 phase as a separately named contingency operation",
        "Restoration of the figures to the Iran-war series, which would support the 'temporary data disruption' account",
        "Persistence of the split beyond the period the department called temporary, which would undercut it",
        "An inspector general or congressional inquiry into the reclassification",
        "A reconciled official figure for total killed and wounded across both categories, published and maintained"
      ],
      "current_assessment": "As of Day 152 (July 28, 2026) the reclassified figures have not been restored and no DoD casualty-reporting guidance establishing the 'Overseas Operations' category has been published. A second cycle has now passed with the official Iran-war totals standing at 14 killed and 420 wounded while officials continue to say the true wounded figure exceeds 500. Persistence without explanation does not by itself distinguish a bookkeeping artefact from a presentational choice. || As of Day 151 (July 27, 2026) the reclassification is documented and its rationale is not. Whether it is bookkeeping or presentation turns on internal casualty-reporting guidance that has not been published, and the department's own 'temporary' characterisation is itself testable only by waiting. The prior May 11-12 episode and the gap between the Pentagon's 'nearly 100 injured since July 7' and the 207 wounded now shown in the new category are grounds for scepticism, but neither establishes intent. Treated as contested-unresolved; the briefing continues to carry both the pre- and post-revision figures.",
      "first_listed": "2026-07-27"
    },
    {
      "id": "gulf-states-direct-strikes-iran-july-2026",
      "title": "Did Bahraini and Kuwaiti warplanes carry out direct airstrikes inside Iran in July 2026, with UAE targeting intelligence and air cover — the first direct Gulf Arab military action against Iran — or is the report false, as Kuwait formally asserts?",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "Single-outlet report versus a formal state denial — July 26: The Wall Street Journal reported on July 24 ('Bahrain, Kuwait Warplanes Struck Iran in Rare Gulf Retaliation') that Bahraini and Kuwaiti warplanes secretly struck Iranian missile and drone storage depots and other military installations earlier in July, with the UAE supplying targeting intelligence and defensive air cover — described as a significant departure from the region's decades-long proxy-war playbook. On July 25, Kuwait's ambassador to the United States, Sheikha Zain Al-Sabah, sent a formal letter to the paper's editorial board calling the report 'entirely false.' Bahrain and the UAE have neither confirmed nor denied; no imagery, flight-tracking, battle-damage assessment or Iranian attribution of the strikes to Gulf aircraft has been published.",
      "synopsis": "On July 24, 2026 The Wall Street Journal reported that Bahrain and Kuwait had secretly flown airstrikes against targets inside Iran earlier in the month — missile and drone storage depots and other military installations — marking the first direct military action by Gulf Arab states against Iran, with the United Arab Emirates providing intelligence on targets and defensive air cover. Both Bahrain and Kuwait host US forces and had absorbed months of Iranian missile and drone attacks that Tehran justified on precisely that basis. The report was widely syndicated (TASS, ANI, The Jerusalem Post, Defence Security Asia). On July 25 Kuwait's ambassador to the United States formally rejected it in writing to the WSJ's editorial board, stating that Kuwait has not participated in any military operations against Iran and has not allowed the use of its territory, airspace or territorial waters to launch offensive operations against any neighbouring country. The question matters well beyond attribution: if accurate, it converts Gulf host-states from passive basing-and-interception partners into co-belligerents, which bears directly on corpus predictions about coalition composition and about whether Washington can outsource escalation ('we have other people that will do the ground campaign for us').",
      "documented_facts": [
        "The Wall Street Journal published 'Bahrain, Kuwait Warplanes Struck Iran in Rare Gulf Retaliation' on July 24, 2026, reporting that Bahraini and Kuwaiti warplanes struck Iranian missile and drone storage depots and other military sites earlier in July — their first direct military action against Iran — and that the UAE provided targeting intelligence and defensive air cover. (WSJ via TASS, ANI, The Jerusalem Post, Defence Security Asia, July 24-25, 2026.)",
        "Kuwait's ambassador to the United States, Sheikha Zain Al-Sabah, sent a formal letter to the WSJ editorial board on July 25, 2026 calling the report 'entirely false' and stating that Kuwait has not participated in any military operations against Iran and has not allowed the use of its territory, airspace or territorial waters to launch offensive operations against any neighbouring country. (Arab Times, July 25, 2026.)",
        "Bahrain and the UAE issued no public confirmation or denial as of July 25, 2026.",
        "Both Bahrain and Kuwait host US forces (Bahrain is home to the US Navy's Fifth Fleet) and have absorbed repeated Iranian missile and drone attacks since February 28, 2026 — Bahrain with 3 killed and 51 wounded reported cumulatively, Kuwait with 4 soldiers and 7 civilians killed and 82 soldiers and 105 civilians wounded from the July strikes on its power-and-water infrastructure — which Tehran has justified by treating US-hosting Gulf bases as legitimate targets. (NPR; ABC News; Casualties of the 2026 Iran war, June-July 2026.)",
        "No imagery, flight-tracking data, battle-damage assessment, wreckage, or Iranian government attribution of any strike to Bahraini, Kuwaiti or Emirati aircraft has been published as of July 26, 2026. Iran has not publicly named Gulf states as having struck its territory.",
        "The report is consistent in direction with a separately documented shift: Saudi Arabia entered an open exchange with the Iran-aligned Houthis on July 24-25 (Saudi strikes on Hodeidah and Kamaran Island; Houthi missile and drone strikes on Aramco facilities at Jizan and Yanbu), and the UAE has been intercepting Iranian missiles and drones since the war began."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "Either (a) the WSJ account is accurate and Gulf Arab states have crossed from hosting-and-intercepting into direct belligerency against Iran — a structural change in the war's coalition and a validation of the 'others will do the fighting' outsourcing model — or (b) the report is wrong as to Kuwait (and possibly more broadly), reflecting sourcing that overstates national participation or conflates coalition/US air activity with Kuwaiti and Bahraini national action, as Kuwait's government formally asserts.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "The Wall Street Journal, and the outlets syndicating its report (TASS, ANI, The Jerusalem Post, Defence Security Asia, voz.us, cryptobriefing)",
        "the Government of Kuwait, via its ambassador to the United States Sheikha Zain Al-Sabah, formally asserting the contrary in writing to the paper",
        "regional analysts treating the account as evidence that the Gulf has abandoned the proxy-war convention, and others treating the Kuwaiti denial as dispositive absent corroboration"
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "An on-the-record acknowledgement by Bahrain, Kuwait or the UAE of participation in strikes inside Iran",
        "Iranian government attribution of specific strikes to Gulf-state aircraft, with supporting evidence (wreckage, radar or intercept data)",
        "Published imagery, flight-tracking or battle-damage assessment tying the named depots to non-US, non-Israeli aircraft",
        "A WSJ correction, retraction or on-the-record sourcing that resolves the Kuwaiti denial either way",
        "US or CENTCOM confirmation of allied national sorties into Iranian airspace"
      ],
      "current_assessment": "As of July 26 (Day 150) the question is genuinely unresolvable from open sources. A single named outlet with anonymous sourcing reports a covert first; a sovereign government formally and specifically denies it; two other named participants say nothing; and none of the physical or attributional evidence that would normally settle an air-strike claim has been published. Covert allied air operations are precisely the class of event that leaves no open-source trace when the participants do not want one, so the absence of corroboration is weak evidence either way. The project records the WSJ report as reported-not-confirmed and the Kuwaiti denial as an on-the-record official position, and does not treat either as settled. Note the narrower point that is NOT in dispute and does not depend on this question: any such action is ALLIED AIR activity, not US ground forces, and therefore does not bear on the US ground-invasion prediction.",
      "first_listed": "2026-07-26"
    },
    {
      "id": "iran-errant-hardliners-mistake-claim-july-2026",
      "title": "Did Iran privately disown the Hormuz tanker strikes as the work of 'errant' hardliners, and were they 'a mistake'?",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "The 'mistake' off-ramp collides with a US escalation — July 15: rather than an Iranian climb-down, July 14 brought a US re-imposition of the naval blockade, a fourth night of US strikes, Trump's (reversed) 20% Hormuz toll, and three more Iranian tanker attacks — cutting against the 'errant hardliners'/'was a mistake' framing floated July 13. The private-messaging claim still rests entirely on anonymous US sourcing with no Iranian on-record confirmation, and events have moved toward mutual escalation rather than the acknowledgment the US said it expected. || US officials say Tehran privately blamed the tanker strikes on an 'errant' hardliner faction and expect Araghchi to acknowledge a 'mistake' after Oman — analysts call it 'implausible but convenient' — July 14: the closure ran into a third day, Brent jumped above $78, and a US-Iran Oman round was reported for Saturday, but the private-messaging claim rests entirely on anonymous US sourcing and has no Iranian on-record confirmation.",
      "synopsis": "As Iran's July 12 IRGC closure of the Strait of Hormuz ran into a third day, unnamed senior Trump-administration officials told US media that Iranian officials had privately said the Strait-of-Hormuz ship attacks were the work of an 'errant' faction of hardliners trying to undermine negotiations, and that Washington expected Iranian FM Abbas Araghchi to publicly or implicitly acknowledge, after his Oman meetings, that hitting the tankers and commercial ships 'was a mistake.' The framing matters because it lets both governments de-escalate — Iran without formally rescinding its closure, the US without a ground move — and because it feeds directly into the Saturday Oman round (Vance/Kushner/Witkoff/Rubio). Al Jazeera reported that analysts considered the private-messaging claim 'implausible but convenient' for the US, since blaming a hardline faction keeps the door to diplomacy ajar.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "July 13, 2026: Unnamed senior Trump-administration officials told US media that Iranian officials had privately said the Strait-of-Hormuz ship attacks stemmed from an 'errant' faction of hardliners trying to undermine negotiations (Al Jazeera; Fox News Digital).",
        "July 13, 2026: US reporting said the Trump team expected FM Araghchi, after his Oman meetings, to publicly admit or implicitly acknowledge that hitting the tankers and commercial ships earlier in the week was a mistake (Al Jazeera).",
        "July 13, 2026: Analysts said the claim about Iran's private messaging is 'implausible but convenient' for the US, because blaming a hardline faction leaves the door to diplomacy ajar (Al Jazeera).",
        "July 10-13, 2026: Trump directed a team led by VP JD Vance, Jared Kushner, envoy Steve Witkoff and SecState Marco Rubio to continue negotiations in Oman on Saturday; US officials said talks 'can't progress until ships are assured safe passage' (CBS News; CNN).",
        "July 12, 2026: Iran's IRGC Navy had declared the Strait 'closed until further notice… until regional interference by the US ceases,' and the closure persisted into July 14 (Al Jazeera; CNN)."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "One reading treats the US account as genuine intra-regime signalling: Iran's leadership, wanting to preserve the diplomatic track, quietly told Washington the ship attacks were the work of hardliners it does not fully control, and Araghchi's Oman appearance was expected to ratify that climb-down — evidence that Tehran's decision-makers want to keep talking. The other reading treats it as a US-constructed narrative: anonymous US officials, with an incentive to frame Iranian intent favorably and to justify continued diplomacy over escalation, floated an 'errant faction' story that conveniently lets both sides de-escalate without Iran conceding the closure — the 'implausible but convenient' framing analysts flagged. A third possibility is that the message was real but does not reflect Iran's actual chain of command (i.e., the 'errant faction' framing is itself Iranian face-saving).",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "Unnamed senior Trump-administration officials (via US media): Iran privately called the attacks an 'errant' hardliner faction's doing; Araghchi expected to acknowledge a 'mistake.'",
        "Regional analysts (via Al Jazeera): the private-messaging claim is 'implausible but convenient' for the US."
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "An on-record Iranian statement (Araghchi or the foreign ministry) either acknowledging the tanker strikes as a mistake / a rogue-faction act, or explicitly denying any such private message.",
        "Primary documentation (a leaked cable, readout, or corroboration from a mediator such as Oman/Qatar) of what Iran actually communicated privately.",
        "Whether the Saturday Oman round produces an Iranian move consistent with the 'mistake' framing (e.g., a public reopening or safe-passage assurance) or contradicts it (continued closure/enforcement)."
      ],
      "current_assessment": "As of July 15 the 'mistake' off-ramp looks increasingly unsupported by events: instead of an Iranian acknowledgment, July 14 brought a US re-imposition of the naval blockade, a fourth night of strikes, Trump's (reversed) 20% Hormuz toll, and three more Iranian tanker attacks — a mutual escalation, not a climb-down. The claim remains unresolvable from open sources (anonymous US sourcing only, no Iranian confirmation), and the direction of travel now cuts against it, though it is still possible Iran de-escalates at the pending Oman round. Either way it is a diplomatic-signalling/attribution question, not a ground indicator, and does not alter the disconfirmed reading of the US-ground-seizure thesis. || As of July 14 the claim is unresolvable from open sources: it rests entirely on anonymous US officials with a clear incentive to frame Iranian intent favorably, has no Iranian on-record confirmation, and was met with analyst skepticism ('implausible but convenient'). It is a diplomatic-signalling/attribution question, not a ground indicator, and does not alter the disconfirmed-and-inverted reading of the US-ground-seizure thesis — the coercion at the chokepoint remains Iran's while the US response stays air/diplomatic (blockade lifted, Day 26). The Saturday Oman round is the next hard test of whether the 'mistake' framing is borne out.",
      "first_listed": "2026-07-14"
    },
    {
      "id": "iran-succession-mojtaba-khamenei-2026",
      "title": "Who actually governs Iran after Ayatollah Ali Khamenei's death — is designated successor Mojtaba Khamenei exercising real power, or has his months-long public absence created a leadership vacuum filled by the IRGC/SNSC?",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "State funeral closed with burial at Mashhad; new Supreme Leader still absent — July 9: Khamenei was buried at the Imam Reza shrine in Mashhad, his birthplace, closing the six-day state funeral (Tehran July 3-5, Qom July 6-7, Najaf/Karbala July 8, Mashhad July 9). New Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei (named March 8-9) still had not appeared publicly through the rites, keeping the who-actually-runs-Iran question live and unresolved. || New Supreme Leader still absent as rites reach Qom — July 7: the funeral cortege moved to Qom (Jamkaran mosque, Fatima Masoumeh shrine) after the July 6 Tehran procession, and Mojtaba Khamenei (named successor March 9) had still not appeared publicly. Reporting now attributes his absence both to an Israeli assassination threat and to injuries from the Feb 28 strike — US officials in March said he was 'wounded and likely disfigured,' with later reports of severe facial/leg injuries — while three of Khamenei's other sons prayed at the coffin July 5 and the designated successor did not, sharpening questions over his health and who is actually running Iran. || New Supreme Leader still absent on the funeral's largest day — July 6: as the main Tehran procession drew up to a projected 20 million mourners, Mojtaba Khamenei (named successor March 9) had still not appeared publicly and his whereabouts remained unreported (CNN), while Israel renewed a threat to kill Iran's new leader — sustaining questions over his health and who is actually running Iran. || New Supreme Leader's whereabouts unknown as his father's state funeral opens — July 5: Mojtaba Khamenei was named to succeed his father on March 9 but has not appeared publicly since; as the six-day funeral opened, CNN reported his whereabouts were unknown and it was unclear whether he would attend, fueling questions over his health and who is actually running Iran.",
      "synopsis": "Ayatollah Ali Khamenei was killed in the Feb 28, 2026 US-Israeli opening strike; his son Mojtaba Khamenei was designated Supreme Leader on March 9. As Iran opened a six-day state funeral for the elder Khamenei (July 4-9, 15-20 million mourners projected), CNN reported that Mojtaba's whereabouts remained unknown and that he had not appeared publicly since taking the post — raising questions about his health, his physical security, and whether he is actually exercising the office's authority or serving as a figurehead while the IRGC and the Supreme National Security Council run the state. Because the question turns on non-public facts (his location, health, and real decision-making role), it cannot be resolved from open sources, but it is being asserted by non-fringe outlets and bears directly on whether Tehran can deliver on any US-Iran settlement.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "July 5, 2026: Reporting (CNBC, TIME, US News, The Tribune) noted Mojtaba Khamenei was absent from the funeral; three of Khamenei's other sons prayed at the coffin. His absence was tied both to an Israeli threat to assassinate Iran's new leader and to injuries from the Feb 28 strike (Defense Secretary Hegseth said in March he was 'wounded and likely disfigured'; later reports described severe facial/leg injuries, possibly loss of a leg).",
        "Feb 28, 2026: Ayatollah Ali Khamenei was killed in the US-Israeli opening strike of the 2026 Iran War.",
        "March 9, 2026: Mojtaba Khamenei was named Supreme Leader to succeed his father.",
        "July 4, 2026: Iran opened a six-day state funeral for Ali Khamenei (Tehran → Qom → Najaf/Karbala → Mashhad, ending July 9), with 15-20 million mourners projected — billed as the largest state funeral in Iranian history.",
        "July 4, 2026: CNN reported that the whereabouts of the new Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei remained unknown, that he had not appeared publicly since taking the post, and that it was unclear whether he would attend the funeral (CNN, 'Whereabouts of Iran's new supreme leader unknown').",
        "Context: through the war, Iranian decision-making has repeatedly been attributed to the Supreme National Security Council and the IRGC (e.g., the June 15 SNSC confirmation of the 14-point MOU), and IRGC chief Ahmad Vahidi resurfaced from hiding only on July 2."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "One reading treats Mojtaba's public absence as deliberate security prudence and mourning protocol — a wartime leadership keeping its principal out of predictable, crowd-dense target sets after the Feb 28 decapitation strike, with authority exercised through the SNSC and IRGC as designed. The other treats the months-long non-appearance since his March 9 designation as evidence of a genuine leadership vacuum or incapacity — that Mojtaba may be gravely unwell, sidelined, or a figurehead, and that the security establishment is effectively governing, which would bear on who can actually deliver on any US-Iran deal.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "CNN (July 4, 2026): the new supreme leader's whereabouts are unknown; he has not appeared publicly since taking the post.",
        "Iran-watchers and analysts: cite the non-appearance as a marker of possible IRGC ascendancy or a contested/hollow succession.",
        "Iranian state framing (implicit): treats the leadership as intact and the funeral as a demonstration of national cohesion and continuity."
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "A verified public appearance by Mojtaba Khamenei exercising the office's authority (or a credible confirmation of his incapacity/absence).",
        "Documented evidence of who is issuing binding decisions in Tehran (SNSC, IRGC, or the Supreme Leader personally).",
        "The post-funeral US-Iran round producing an Iranian commitment traceable to an identifiable, empowered decision-maker."
      ],
      "current_assessment": "As of July 10, the six-day state funeral has closed (burial at Mashhad's Imam Reza shrine July 9) and Mojtaba Khamenei — designated successor in early March — still has not appeared publicly, even at his father's burial, where three of Khamenei's other sons but not the designated successor prayed at the coffin. Reporting attributes his absence to an Israeli assassination threat and to injuries from the Feb 28 strike. The question stays contested_unresolved: who is actually exercising Supreme-Leader authority in Iran remains undetermined from open sources. Contested and unresolvable from open sources as of July 7. Through the Qom leg of the funeral, the new Supreme Leader remained publicly absent, with reporting now attributing the absence both to security (an Israeli assassination threat) and to Feb 28 strike injuries. The question bears on regime cohesion and deal durability — if Mojtaba is incapacitated or sidelined, it is unclear who can ratify and enforce a US-Iran settlement — but it is not a US ground indicator. His continued non-appearance is consistent with both prudent concealment/recovery and genuine incapacity; resolution awaits a verified account of his injuries, health, and real authority, which the open record does not currently provide. || Contested and unresolvable from open sources as of July 6. The question is one of Iranian regime cohesion and decision-making, not a US ground indicator, but it bears on deal durability: if the new Supreme Leader is absent or incapacitated, it is unclear who can ratify and enforce a US-Iran settlement. His continued non-appearance through the funeral's central day is consistent with both wartime security prudence and genuine incapacity; resolution awaits a verified account of Mojtaba Khamenei's status and role, which the open record does not currently provide.",
      "first_listed": "2026-07-05"
    },
    {
      "id": "kostiantynivka-control-contested-july-2026",
      "title": "Did Russia capture Kostiantynivka? Gerasimov told Putin the city is 'liberated'; Zelensky and Ukraine's General Staff call it 'another Russian lie.'",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "Capture claim STILL contested; city held despite Russian infiltration — July 14-15: Ukraine's General Staff (Maj. Andriy Kovalev) said some Russian infantry troops had entered the city and the situation was 'difficult,' but Kostiantynivka 'remained under the control of the Ukrainian Armed Forces,' with troops holding certain lines on the outskirts; Zelensky again called Putin's 'liberation' claim a lie for media attention, the Kremlin (Peskov) insisted the city was 'already part of Russia' and renewed its Moscow-meeting invitation, and Russia's MoD proposed a six-hour local ceasefire for a body handover that Ukraine rejected. No open-source development resolved control either way. || Capture claim still contested; Ukraine's deep-strike campaign continues — July 12-13: no July 12-13 development resolved the control dispute (Russia's July 3 'liberation' claim still denied by Kyiv; ISW: small-group infiltration, not consolidated control). Overnight July 12 Ukraine struck the Syzran refinery (Samara, ~800 km) and reportedly hit 10 tankers and 4 ferries in the Sea of Azov. || Capture claim still contested; local ceasefire rejected — July 6-7: Russia proposed a local ceasefire in Kostiantynivka (paired with a body handover) that Ukraine rejected; ISW mapping put Russian presence at ~37% of the city and only ~4.3% of Lyman, calling Putin's claim of 96% control 'greatly exaggerated' and describing gains as small-group infiltration (~50 m/day), not consolidated control. || Capture claim still contested — July 5-6: Russia's July 3 'liberation' claim remained disputed; Ukraine's 19th Army Corps commander Madyar reported pushing Russian infiltrators back out of the city at a ~3:53 loss ratio, and ISW/DeepState continued to assess small-group infiltration rather than consolidated control (DeepState: ~12 sq mi net Russian gain over the four weeks to June 23). || Capture claimed and denied — July 4: Russia's General Staff chief Gerasimov reported to Putin (July 3) that Russian forces had 'liberated' Kostiantynivka, a key Sloviansk-Kramatorsk-Kostiantynivka defensive hub; on July 4 Zelensky and Ukraine's General Staff flatly denied it, with Zelensky challenging Putin to meet him in the city, and ISW assessed no confirmed advances July 3.",
      "synopsis": "On July 3, 2026, Russia's General Staff chief Gen. Valery Gerasimov reported to Putin — filmed at a command post — that Russian forces had 'liberated' Kostiantynivka, calling it one of the main defensive hubs of the Sloviansk-Kramatorsk-Kostiantynivka fortified area, and said forces were closing on Lyman; Putin hailed it and Russia's MoD posted flag-planting imagery. On July 4 President Zelensky and Ukraine's General Staff flatly denied the claim — 'It is just another Russian lie' — and Zelensky challenged Putin to meet him in Kostiantynivka if Russia truly held it. ISW assessed no confirmed advances on July 3 and continues to characterize Russian progress there as small-group infiltration rather than consolidated control. Real-time ground truth over a contested urban front is not available from open sources.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "July 4-6, 2026: Russia proposed a local ceasefire in Kostiantynivka paired with a handover of bodies; Russia said Ukraine rejected the offer (Ukrainska Pravda; Al Jazeera).",
        "July 4-5, 2026: ISW mapping assessed Russian presence at ~37% of Kostiantynivka and ~4.3% of Lyman, calling Putin's claim of 96% control 'greatly exaggerated' and characterizing gains as small-group infiltration (~50 m/day), not consolidated control (ISW/Russia Matters; Euromaidan Press).",
        "July 3, 2026: Gen. Gerasimov reported to Putin that Russian forces had 'liberated' Kostiantynivka and were closing on Lyman; Russia's MoD posted flag-planting images (Reuters via Moscow Times/US News; Mezha).",
        "July 3, 2026: Putin, filmed at a command post, hailed the reported capture as a strategic achievement.",
        "July 4, 2026: President Zelensky and Ukraine's General Staff denied the capture — 'that is not true... just another Russian lie' — and Zelensky challenged Putin to meet him in the city (Moscow Times; Reuters via Arab News/CTV; UNITED24).",
        "July 3, 2026: ISW/Critical Threats assessed no confirmed advances on July 3 and continues to describe Russian gains at Kostiantynivka as small-group infiltration (~50 m/day), not consolidated control.",
        "July 3, 2026: Ukraine's General Staff reported 268 combat engagements along the front, with the Pokrovsk sector heaviest (22 assaults repelled)."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "Russia's reading (Gerasimov to Putin, MoD imagery): Kostiantynivka is 'liberated,' a major defensive hub of the Sloviansk-Kramatorsk line has fallen, and forces are pushing toward Lyman. Ukraine's reading (Zelensky, General Staff): the claim is 'another Russian lie' — Ukrainian forces still contest the city, Russian gains are small-group infiltration without consolidated control, and Zelensky's 'meet me there' challenge is meant to expose the claim as propaganda.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "Russia (Gen. Gerasimov to Putin; MoD; state media): Kostiantynivka 'liberated,' forces closing on Lyman.",
        "Ukraine (President Zelensky; General Staff): the capture claim is false; the city is still contested.",
        "ISW/Critical Threats: no confirmed advances July 3; Russian progress characterized as small-group infiltration, not consolidated control."
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "Geolocated combat footage or corroborated third-party confirmation of who physically holds Kostiantynivka.",
        "A stabilized front line in and around the city over the coming days.",
        "An updated ISW/DeepState control-map assessment moving the city into confirmed Russian control or confirmed Ukrainian contestation."
      ],
      "current_assessment": "Contested and unresolvable from open sources as of July 18. CNN (July 16) reports Putin's capture claim is false and 'exposes the slow pace of Russia's bloody advance,' with Russian and Ukrainian positions inside Kostiantynivka interspersed; Zelensky urged Putin to meet him in the town if it is truly captured. The Economist put Russia's gain at ~31 sq km over 30 days. The flatly opposed authoritative assertions persist. || Contested and unresolvable from open sources as of July 15. Ukraine's General Staff says the city remains under Ukrainian control despite Russian infantry infiltration; Russia and the Kremlin maintain it is 'already part of Russia'; the flatly opposed authoritative assertions persist and no independent geolocation has moved the city into confirmed control by either side. The dispute bears on Donbas control, not on any tracked US ground-invasion prediction; it stays logged as a recurring contested claim. Resolution awaits independent geolocation or a stabilized front. || Contested and unresolvable from open sources as of July 13; no July 12-13 development moved the city into confirmed control by either side. The dispute bears on Donbas control, not on any tracked US ground-invasion prediction; it stays logged because control claims over Kostiantynivka keep recurring with flatly opposed authoritative assertions. Resolution awaits independent geolocation or a stabilized front. || Contested and unresolvable from open sources as of July 7. Russia's control claim (now Putin's ~96% figure plus a 'local ceasefire' offer as the purported victor) remained opposed by Ukraine's denial and by ISW's ~37%-of-city mapping calling the Russian claim 'greatly exaggerated.' The dispute bears on Donbas control, not on any tracked US ground-invasion prediction; it stays logged because control claims over Kostiantynivka keep recurring with flatly opposed authoritative assertions. Resolution awaits independent geolocation or a stabilized front. || Contested and unresolvable from open sources as of July 6. The dispute bears on Donbas control, not on any tracked US ground-invasion prediction; it is logged because control claims over Kostiantynivka have been recurring and the record shows flatly opposed authoritative assertions (Gerasimov's claim vs. Ukraine's denial and Madyar's counter-report). Resolution awaits independent geolocation or a stabilized front.",
      "first_listed": "2026-07-05"
    },
    {
      "id": "iran-hormuz-administration-tolls-dispute-june-2026",
      "title": "Who governs the Strait of Hormuz after the war, and can Iran charge for transit?",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "THE DISPUTE MOVES FROM AMBIGUITY TO AN EXPLICIT, PRICED, REJECTED OFFER — July 30: The toll question is no longer inferential. On July 29 Iran REJECTED Oman's Gulf-backed proposal and published its own terms through deputy foreign minister Kazem Gharibabadi. Oman's offer, as he described it: 'Oman proposed to us the creation of a route in the Strait of Hormuz, with 50 percent of it located in our territorial waters and 50 percent in Omani territorial waters' — ships entering through Iranian waters and exiting through Omani, funded by VOLUNTARY industry contributions on the Strait of Malacca model, which generates roughly $70 million a year. Iran's counterproposal: 'Our proposal is for one route to lie entirely within the territorial waters of the Islamic Republic of Iran, with part of the other route also passing through Iranian territorial waters,' giving Tehran oversight of both inbound and outbound traffic, with a reported $1 MILLION PER SHIP 'service fee' earmarked for reconstruction. Gharibabadi argued the June 17 MOU obliged Iran to provide free safe passage 'for only 60 days,' asserted a residual right to charge for transit, and said mine-clearing is Iran's own responsibility — with any third-country mine-clearing vessel or equipment near the Strait to 'be considered legitimate targets.' Washington's stated position is unchanged: 'Iran cannot toll the Strait, which is an international waterway' (Anna Kelly), under 'existing international law' (Rubio). The gap between the parties is now quantified rather than merely asserted. || THE DISPUTE ACQUIRES A CONCRETE PROPOSAL AND A NAMED MODEL — July 29: Oman has put a Gulf-backed plan for JOINT REGIONAL MANAGEMENT of the Strait to Tehran and carried it to Washington. It draws on the MALACCA STRAIT/SINGAPORE model, in which VOLUNTARY industry contributions fund safe navigation, and a person familiar with the US position said the proposals as delivered contain NO MANDATORY TRANSIT TOLLS. Iran's stated preference remains unilateral control and mandatory tolls: deputy FM Kazem Gharibabadi said on July 28 that Iran will take 'any action' to maintain control of the Strait. White House spokesperson Anna Kelly: 'President Trump has been clear that Iran cannot toll the Strait, which is an international waterway.' Rubio: 'No country is allowed to charge tolls or fees on an international waterway. That's existing international law.' Mediators say Iran and Oman remain far apart on fees. || Oman carries the co-administration formula into Tehran — July 26 (Day 150): an Omani diplomatic delegation arrived in Tehran on July 24 to discuss establishing 'appropriate mechanisms for managing ship traffic in the Strait of Hormuz' (IRNA), reviving the Iran-Oman co-management concept that Washington and the Gulf states have consistently rejected — the US insisting Iran 'does not control' the waterway. This followed Iran's July 24 rejection of an Iraqi-brokered ceasefire precisely because it left Hormuz control unresolved. Hormuz remains functionally shut (15 transits July 19 against an 88/day baseline; ~85 tankers running dark; the IMO still assessing the waterway as too dangerous for commercial vessels), so the governing question is being negotiated over a chokepoint no one is actually using. || US RE-IMPOSES ITS BLOCKADE AND TRUMP CLAIMS THE US AS THE STRAIT'S 'GUARDIAN' — July 15: the governance dispute took a sharp new turn as, on July 14, the US restarted its naval blockade of Iranian ports (CENTCOM: 20+ warships, hundreds of aircraft) and Trump announced — then reversed the same day — a 20% 'toll'/reimbursement on Hormuz cargo, framing the US as the waterway's 'guardian' owed compensation by Gulf beneficiaries. The question of who governs the Strait is now contested not only between Iran's toll/closure claims and the US 'international waterway' line, but by a US bid to police and monetize the chokepoint itself — even as Iran's July 12 closure held and it struck three more tankers July 14. It is a maritime-governance/coercion contest run by BOTH sides (Iranian closure + US blockade), not a US ground seizure, and unresolvable from open sources while positions stay maximal and fluid (Trump reversed the toll within a day). || Closure enters a SECOND DAY; US and Iran publicly dispute whether the Strait is open — July 13: Iran's July 12 IRGC 'closed until further notice' declaration persisted, hardening into a public factual dispute (US CENTCOM: the Strait 'is open… traffic is flowing'; Iran's Persian Gulf Strait Authority: passage 'is currently not possible'), with vessel-tracking at only ~33-35% of the pre-war baseline. The July 11 IRGC strike on the container ship M/V GFS Galaxy left one crewman missing, and Iran's July 12 multi-state retaliation hit up to five US-partner states. Oman's two-corridor draft stayed under discussion (Tehran's sign-off pending). It is again Iran, not the US, asserting coercive control at the chokepoint — the structural inverse of the predicted US seizure. || Iran RE-CLOSES the Strait; Oman floats a two-corridor plan; the US demands Iran declare it open — July 12: the IRGC Navy declared Hormuz 'closed until further notice… until regional interference by the US ceases' after firing warning shots at a vessel on an 'unapproved route,' hours after Oman proposed a Southern Corridor (Omani waters, free navigation) and a Northern Corridor (Iranian waters, prior Iranian approval, no tolls); Washington demanded Tehran publicly declare all channels open and insisted Iran 'does not control' the Strait. It is again Iran, not the US, asserting coercive control of the chokepoint — the structural inverse of the predicted US seizure. || Iran holds to mid-August tolls the US rejects; UK/France back Oman on free navigation — July 7: with US-Iran talks paused for the funeral week, the governance dispute stayed unresolved. Iran maintained it will charge transit 'service fees' once the 60-day toll-free window ends (~mid-August), with 'special treatment' for 'friendly' nations, while the UK and France pledged to help Oman keep 'safe transit for ships of all nations' (a possible Franco-British-led multinational freedom-of-navigation mission). It is Iran, not the US, asserting coercive control over the chokepoint — the structural inverse of the predicted US seizure. || Iran sets a mid-August toll deadline the US rejects; Doha round leaves governance unresolved — July 4: The Doha round agreed a focus on restoring Hormuz passage but did NOT bridge the governance dispute — Washington rejects any Iranian-led toll mechanism while Tehran asserts joint sovereignty over the corridor with Oman and says it will impose transit tolls from mid-August once the MOU's toll-free window expires. Iran's joint military command warned all tankers to use its designated routes or face a 'forceful response,' and state media reported a foreign container ship ran aground outside the corridor; Germany declined to join demining, citing Iran's unwillingness to cooperate with other nations. || Second open friction in the 60-day roadmap — June 30: the cycle breaks into a Strait-management negotiation. Iran and Oman hold their first meeting on managing the reopened Strait, with demining and tolls disputed — Iran insists on demining alone and rejects a France-Oman initiative ('sensitive and complex'), while Oman and the GCC reject Iranian transit tolls as 'not acceptable' and the US keeps Hormuz a toll-free, open international waterway. || Second open friction in the 60-day roadmap — June 29: the kinetic cycle broadens. After the June 27 M/T Kiku strike and the June 28 US 10-target retaliation, Iran's IRGC strikes US bases in Kuwait and Bahrain and FM Araghchi reasserts the Strait is under Iran's 'total oversight and management' — while the US keeps Hormuz a toll-free, open international waterway and Gulf exports stay near ~75% of pre-war. || Second open friction in the 60-day roadmap — June 28: the two-sided kinetic cycle repeats. Iran fires on a second tanker (the M/T Kiku, June 27) and threatens 'a broader response,' the US retaliates with a second, larger airstrike, and Bahrain condemns an Iranian drone strike on its sovereignty — while the US keeps the Strait a toll-free, open international waterway (JMIC widened the transit route) and Gulf exports climb to ~75% of pre-war. || Second open friction in the 60-day roadmap — June 26: the dispute turns two-sided kinetic. Iran fires on shipping (the M/V Ever Lovely) and turns back three tankers crossing 'unauthorized routes,' the US retaliates with airstrikes, and Iran vows to resume transit fees after the 60-day window — while the US insists the Strait is a toll-free international waterway",
      "synopsis": "As the 60-day Bürgenstock roadmap proceeded, the question of who controls the reopened Strait of Hormuz hardened into a second running dispute alongside the nuclear-inspections clash. On June 24 Secretary of State Marco Rubio, touring the Gulf, said no country may charge for passage ('It's an international waterway. No country is allowed to charge tolls or fees') and warned there would be no final deal if Iran proposes tolls, framing Strait security as a matter for Iran and the Gulf Coast Coalition. The same day Iran's chief negotiator Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf said the Strait 'will never return to its pre-war conditions and will be administered by the Islamic Republic of Iran,' and Iran's Persian Gulf Strait Authority said its planned transit fees are suspended — but only for the duration of the 60-day MOU window. The MOU itself defers Strait governance to the 60-day negotiation, so both sides are asserting maximal positions without a binding text.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "July 28, 2026: Oman presented Iran with a Gulf-backed proposal for joint regional management of the Strait of Hormuz, suggesting voluntary fees for passage support and drawing in part on the Malacca Strait and Singapore model, in which voluntary industry contributions help fund safe navigation.",
        "July 28, 2026: A person familiar with the US position said the proposals delivered to Washington by Oman do not include mandatory Hormuz transit tolls.",
        "July 28, 2026: White House spokesperson Anna Kelly said, 'President Trump has been clear that Iran cannot toll the Strait, which is an international waterway.' Secretary of State Marco Rubio told reporters, 'No country is allowed to charge tolls or fees on an international waterway. That's existing international law.'",
        "July 28, 2026: Iranian deputy foreign minister Kazem Gharibabadi said Iran will take 'any action' to maintain control of the Strait of Hormuz.",
        "July 28, 2026: Mediators and one US official said Iran and Oman remain far from agreement on some aspects, including whether to charge transit fees.",
        "July 28, 2026: Trump claimed control of the Strait by naval means — 'We control the Strait. Our navy is incredible. Not one ship has gotten to Iran during any blockade' — while CENTCOM reported 20+ warships, 18 commercial vessels stopped, 2 disabled and 2 boarded.",
        "July 14, 2026: The US restarted its naval blockade of Iranian ports in and around the Strait of Hormuz (4pm ET; CENTCOM cited 'more than 20 US Navy warships and hundreds of military aircraft operating' in the region), ending the ~27-day period the blockade had been lifted since the June 17 MOU (NPR; CBS News; CNBC).",
        "July 13-14, 2026: Trump announced the US would charge a 20% 'toll'/reimbursement on cargo transiting Hormuz in exchange for US protection, casting the US as the Strait's 'guardian' and saying Gulf beneficiaries (Saudi Arabia, UAE, Qatar, Bahrain, Kuwait) should compensate the US — then reversed the fee the same day ('I don't think anybody should be able to charge a fee'), substituting Gulf-state investment deals (Al Jazeera; CBS News; NPR).",
        "July 14, 2026: The US Department of Energy said 8.5 million barrels passed through the Strait the previous day 'with military assistance'; Iran's July 12 closure held and Iran struck three more tankers (Stolt Magnesium; Mombasa B; Al Bahyah) (CBS News; en.wikipedia.org).",
        "July 12-13, 2026: Iran's July 12 IRGC 'closed until further notice' declaration persisted into a second day. US CENTCOM said the Strait 'is open... traffic is flowing'; Iran's Persian Gulf Strait Authority said passage 'is currently not possible' — a direct public contradiction. Independent vessel-tracking (iransitrep.com) put transit at ~33-35% of the pre-war baseline (Al Jazeera; Time; CNBC, July 12, 2026).",
        "July 11, 2026: The IRGC disabled the Cyprus-flagged container ship M/V GFS Galaxy with an engine-room strike and fire; 23 crew were rescued (largely by Oman) and one Indian national was reported missing/presumed dead (Iran Focus; Al Jazeera, July 12, 2026).",
        "July 11, 2026: In Muscat, Oman proposed a two-corridor Hormuz scheme — a Southern Corridor through Omani waters with free pre-war navigation, and a Northern Corridor through Iranian waters requiring prior Iranian approval (no tolls); both to remain open under a not-yet-finalized draft. FM Araghchi and Omani FM Badr Albusaidi agreed to further technical/political talks (Al Jazeera; Oman Observer; The Tribune).",
        "July 11, 2026: Washington demanded Tehran issue a public statement that all shipping channels are open and that it will stop firing at ships, and insisted Iran 'does not control' the Strait (RFE/RL).",
        "July 12, 2026 (local): Iran's IRGC Navy declared the Strait of Hormuz 'closed until further notice and until regional interference by the US ceases,' citing 'outside interference from foreign powers' via ships on 'unauthorized routes' and a recent incident in which the Guards fired warning shots at one such vessel (Times of Israel; Haaretz; JFeed; CNN; Al Jazeera).",
        "June 22, 2026: US and Iran agreed at Bürgenstock to a communications mechanism / 'telephone hotline' to keep the Strait of Hormuz open and avoid incidents (Ghalibaf; Vance).",
        "June 24, 2026: Secretary of State Rubio, opening a Gulf tour in the UAE, said the Strait is 'an international waterway' on which 'no country is allowed to charge tolls or fees,' and that if Iran proposes tolls 'there's not going to be a final deal'; he said US-Iran technical talks would resume next week in Geneva.",
        "June 23-24, 2026: Iran's chief negotiator/parliament speaker Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf said the Strait 'will never return to its pre-war conditions and will be administered by the Islamic Republic of Iran, in accordance with international law.'",
        "June 24, 2026: Iran's Persian Gulf Strait Authority said planned fees for ships using the waterway are suspended during the 60-day MOU negotiation window (a deferral, not a withdrawal).",
        "Tehran is allowing toll-free transit during the 60-day window; tanker traffic reached a war-period high (109 vessels Sat-Mon), the northern (Iranian) and southern (Omani) routes are open, and the central channel remains closed pending ~80 mines.",
        "June 25, 2026: Iran fired on a cargo vessel ~7.5 nm SE of Oman's port of Dahit (a US official told Reuters Iran was behind it); the UN International Maritime Organization paused its Strait-of-Hormuz ship-shepherding/evacuation program, and the IRGC Navy warned that safe passage would be permitted only via Tehran-designated routes and that violators would 'face action' — Iran beginning to enforce its authorization claim by fire, not only rhetoric. Oil reversed higher (Brent +2.1% to $75.26; WTI +2% to $71.92).",
        "June 25, 2026 (identified): the struck vessel was the Singapore-flagged cargo ship M/V Ever Lovely (Evergreen Marine); Iran's IRGC fired at least four one-way attack drones, one hitting the upper deck (bridge windows damaged, no injuries) while US forces downed the other three.",
        "June 26, 2026: the IRGC forced three foreign oil tankers to turn around for attempting to cross the Strait 'without coordinating with Iranian authorities'; Iran's Persian Gulf Strait Authority warned that 'consequences arising from passage through unauthorized routes shall be the responsibility of the owner, operator, and vessel commander.'",
        "June 26, 2026: Iranian negotiator Hossein Ghorbanzadeh said Tehran intends to resume collecting Strait transit fees after the 60-day suspension expires, having accepted the pause only 'so that the agreement could be reached.'",
        "June 26, 2026: the US retaliated for the June 25 drone attack with airstrikes on Iranian missile/drone storage facilities, coastal radar sites and Qeshm Island (CENTCOM: a 'powerful response') — the first US strikes on Iran since the June 17 MOU, in the air/naval register. Oil eased to ~$74/bbl (Brent -1 to -2% on June 26)."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "One reading treats the clash as a normal negotiating gap inside a live 60-day process: Iran's 'administered by Tehran' language and its suspended-for-now fee schedule are a face-saving sovereignty posture that the agreed communications mechanism and the US no-tolls redline will reconcile before a final deal — the blockade is lifted, traffic is at a war-period high, and oil is falling. The other reading treats Ghalibaf's 'will never return' and the merely-suspended (not abandoned) fees as a genuine, unresolved sovereignty claim that could harden into a toll regime after the 60-day window and collapse the talks Rubio said would end if Iran charges. UPDATE (June 25): Iran's strike on a cargo vessel and the IRGC 'designated routes / face action' warning strengthen the second reading — that the sovereignty claim is a genuine, hardening assertion Tehran is now willing to enforce coercively — though it remains possible the strike is a contained or localized incident inside an otherwise-holding process. UPDATE (June 26): the dispute became a two-sided kinetic exchange — Iran turned back three tankers crossing 'unauthorized routes,' vowed to resume fees after the 60-day window, and the US retaliated for the Ever Lovely strike with airstrikes on Iranian soil. This further strengthens the second reading (a hardening sovereignty claim Iran is enforcing by fire) and adds a deal-durability dimension: the first ceasefire breach met by US fire. It still does not establish whether Iran will impose a toll regime after the window or whether the breach-retaliation cycle repeats.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "US side (Trump, July 14): the US is the Strait's 'guardian'; a 20% toll/reimbursement is owed by Gulf beneficiaries (announced, then reversed the same day) — a bid to police and monetize the chokepoint.",
        "US side (CENTCOM, July 14): the US re-imposed its naval blockade of Iranian ports (20+ warships, hundreds of aircraft).",
        "US side (CENTCOM, July 12-13): the Strait 'is open' and 'traffic is flowing.'",
        "Iran side (Persian Gulf Strait Authority, July 12-13): passage 'is currently not possible'; the Strait is 'closed until further notice.'",
        "Iran side (IRGC Navy, July 12): the Strait is 'closed until further notice… until regional interference by the US ceases,' with transit only via Iran-approved routes.",
        "US side (July 11): Iran 'does not control' the Strait; Tehran must publicly declare all channels open and stop firing.",
        "Oman (July 11): a two-corridor scheme (free Omani-waters Southern Corridor; Iran-approval Northern Corridor, no tolls).",
        "US side (Secretary of State Rubio): the Strait is a toll-free international waterway; any Iranian toll forfeits a final deal.",
        "Iran side (chief negotiator Ghalibaf): the Strait will be administered by Iran and will 'never return' to its pre-war status.",
        "Iran's Persian Gulf Strait Authority: planned fees suspended only for the 60-day MOU window."
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "The final-deal text specifying who governs the Strait and whether any charge is permitted.",
        "Iran either formally withdrawing the planned fees or reinstating/levying them after the 60-day window.",
        "Whether the next Geneva technical round produces an agreed Strait-security framework with the Gulf Coast Coalition or breaks over the tolls question."
      ],
      "current_assessment": "Still unresolved as of July 29, 2026, but for the first time the dispute has a concrete instrument and a named precedent. The Omani plan converts the question from 'may Iran charge tolls' into 'can a voluntary navigation-fund model on the Malacca precedent be made acceptable to a state that wants control,' with the United States holding a categorical legal position against tolls, Iran holding a categorical political position on control, and Oman offering a structure that finesses neither cleanly. Whether the resulting regime is described as Iranian administration, joint regional management or restored freedom of navigation will determine how several corpus predictions about Hormuz governance score. No agreement exists, and the Strait remains effectively closed.",
      "first_listed": "2026-06-25"
    },
    {
      "id": "iran-nuclear-inspections-dispute-june-2026",
      "title": "Did Iran agree to IAEA inspections of its US-bombed nuclear sites as part of the 60-day roadmap? VP Vance and Trump say yes; Iran's Foreign Ministry says no inspection is scheduled — a closable wording gap, or a substantive refusal that threatens the deal?",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "First public friction in the 60-day roadmap — June 24: claims and denials run in parallel; resolvable only when the IAEA does or does not actually enter Iran.",
      "synopsis": "The day after mediators announced a 'roadmap toward a final deal within 60 days,' the US and Iran openly diverged on whether Iran had agreed to let IAEA inspectors examine the nuclear sites the US bombed in 2025. VP JD Vance said June 22 that Iran 'agreed to invite IAEA inspectors back,' with the agency due 'this week.' On June 23 Iran's Foreign Ministry spokesman Esmail Baghaei said no meeting with IAEA chief Rafael Grossi had occurred in Switzerland and that no inspection of the bombed sites was scheduled. Trump insisted Iran 'will agree to have major weapons inspections... to ensure nuclear honesty long into [the] future' 'despite their protestations and false statements,' and threatened to cut off talks 'immediately' — 'there would be no further negotiations!' — absent the concession. The dispute is the cleanest live test yet of whether the 60-day process is genuine consolidation or another deferral.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "June 22, 2026: VP Vance publicly stated Iran had agreed to invite IAEA inspectors back into the country, with the agency expected 'this week' (CBS News; ms.now live blog).",
        "June 23, 2026: Iran's Foreign Ministry spokesman Esmail Baghaei told reporters in Tehran that no meeting with IAEA Director-General Rafael Grossi had taken place in Switzerland and that UN inspectors were not scheduled to examine the nuclear sites the US bombed in 2025 (Washington Post; Times of Israel).",
        "June 23, 2026: Trump insisted Iran 'will agree to have major weapons inspections in order to ensure nuclear honesty long into [the] future' 'despite their protestations and false statements to the contrary,' and said he would cut off talks immediately / there would be 'no further negotiations' if Iran had not agreed (NBC News; CNN; PBS NewsHour).",
        "June 23-24, 2026: Despite the dispute, technical teams continued work after the Bürgenstock session and Iran's President Pezeshkian met Pakistani mediators in Tehran; a further round was reported for June 23-25 in Washington (NPR; CBS News).",
        "Context: the June 17 interim MOU and the June 22 'roadmap' open a 60-day window in which a nuclear/sanctions deal is to be negotiated; the US naval blockade remains lifted and oil fell to ~$75/bbl over the same window."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "One reading treats the dispute as ordinary early-stage friction — a sequencing/wording gap (Vance describing an in-principle commitment, Baghaei denying any scheduled site visit) that technical teams will close, with the roadmap, lifted blockade, and falling oil intact. The other treats it as the first crack in an over-sold settlement: a substantive Iranian refusal to admit inspectors to the bombed sites that exposes the roadmap as 'a commitment to negotiate' over unresolved core terms, with Trump's 'cut off talks' threat revealing how thin the agreement is.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "US side (VP Vance, President Trump): Iran has committed to IAEA inspections long into the future",
        "Iran's Foreign Ministry (spokesman Esmail Baghaei) and FM Araghchi: no IAEA site inspection is scheduled; sovereignty preserved",
        "Skeptical analysts: note the recurring pattern of US-claimed-then-Iran-denied concessions in this negotiation (e.g., the Lebanon-withdrawal and Hormuz-toll disputes)"
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "IAEA inspectors actually entering Iran and gaining (or being denied) access to the bombed nuclear sites",
        "A published final-deal text specifying inspection terms, or an IAEA/Iran agreement confirming or denying scheduled visits",
        "Trump either cutting off talks (confirming a substantive breach) or the talks proceeding to a signed nuclear deal (confirming the gap was closable)"
      ],
      "current_assessment": "Contested and unresolvable from open sources as of June 24. The dispute is diplomatic/nuclear, not a ground indicator, and does not by itself reverse the war-ending settlement (blockade lifted, oil at war-period lows, US forces drawn down). It is the cleanest live test of whether the 60-day roadmap holds; its resolution awaits the IAEA's actual access — or non-access — to the bombed sites, which has not yet occurred.",
      "first_listed": "2026-06-24"
    },
    {
      "id": "iran-us-deal-reached-june-2026",
      "title": "The June 14 US-Iran 'deal reached' to end the war — a durable, war-ending settlement (blockade lifted, Hormuz reopened by negotiation, US forces withdrawn), or another unconsummated/contested announcement in a series of ~39 slipped deal timelines?",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "Materially breached and rhetorically repudiated, yet not dead — the Hormuz closure enters a second day but the parties keep talking — July 13: Iran's July 12 IRGC Strait closure persisted, the IRGC's July 11 GFS Galaxy strike left one crewman missing, and Iran ran a July 12 multi-state retaliation — yet the MOU was not formally cancelled, the blockade stayed lifted (Day 25), Qatar and Pakistan pressed mediation, and a further US-Iran round was reported possible next week (Switzerland floated). The OPEN question — durability — stays at high strain but unresolved: breached and repudiated, with the parties still talking. || Materially breached and rhetorically repudiated, yet not dead — mediators reconvene as the war widens regionally — July 10: on July 9 the breach-retaliation cycle went two-sided and regional (Iran hit Kuwait, Qatar, Bahrain and Jordan; US ran a ~90-target second-day wave, ~14 killed in Iran; Trump said the US 'attacked Kharg Island' and 'maybe we'll take over Kharg Island' while sparing its oil terminals). Yet by July 10 Pakistan and Qatar were making fresh contacts to halt strikes and salvage the Islamabad MOU (Pakistan said it expected it to hold), Iran had reportedly sought a new deal, the MOU was not formally cancelled, the blockade stayed lifted (Day 22), and no US ground move followed. The OPEN question — durability — is at its highest strain yet but unresolved: breached, repudiated, but with the parties still talking. || Off the kinetic track; talks paused for the funeral, core resolution intact — July 7: the ceasefire held a fifth straight day off the kinetic register as the funeral moved to Qom. US-Iran talks stayed paused for the funeral week (Doha round: 'positive progress,' no breakthrough; no frozen funds moved; IAEA-access dispute over the bombed sites unresolved). The executed MOU still holds, the blockade stays lifted (Day 19), Hormuz stays open, and Brent held near pre-war ~$72/bbl. The OPEN question stays durability over the 60-day window — whether the post-funeral round convenes and bridges the toll and inspection disputes. || Off the kinetic track — Doha round yields 'positive progress' but no breakthrough; talks paused for the funeral — July 4: The July 1-2 US-Iran round in Doha (indirect, via Qatar and Pakistan; Witkoff and Kushner present) concluded with Qatar reporting 'positive progress' and implementation mechanics (a frozen-asset goods-purchase channel, a violations-deconfliction line, a Hormuz-passage focus), but NO breakthrough — the sessions stayed indirect, negotiators revisited already-'resolved' issues, no frozen funds actually moved, and the US agreed to halt talks for a week as Iran opened a six-day state funeral for Khamenei (July 4-9). The ceasefire held throughout. || Breach-retaliation cycle BREAKS into a stand-down, core resolution intact — June 30: On June 29 the US and Iran agreed to halt mutual attacks and let vessels move freely through Hormuz, and Trump announced a fresh high-level meeting in Doha for June 30 (Witkoff and Kushner attending), even as Iran's deputy FM Gharibabadi denied any technical talks were scheduled. Iran received $6B of $12B in frozen Qatari-held assets and oil eased toward a four-month low. The OPEN question is durability — now whether the Doha meeting actually convenes given the Gharibabadi-vs-Trump contradiction. || Breach-retaliation cycle enters a third day and BROADENS, core resolution still intact — June 29: On June 28 CENTCOM struck 10 Iranian targets near Hormuz and Iran's IRGC retaliated with ballistic missiles and drones against US bases in Kuwait (Ali Al Salem) and Bahrain (Fifth Fleet HQ) — the first Iran-on-US-base strikes since June 10-11 (all intercepted/failed, no US injuries; one Qatari civilian killed). Iran threatened a 'complete halt' to talks and boycotted the technical round; the US said talks are 'still planned.' This is the sharpest durability stress yet, but it again runs on the air/missile/maritime rung and does NOT reverse the core resolution: the executed MOU still holds, the blockade stays lifted (Day 11), Hormuz stays open, and oil stays near a four-month low. The OPEN question is durability over the 60-day window — now whether the broadened cycle keeps escalating and whether the boycotted talks reconvene. || Breach-retaliation cycle repeats a second straight day, core resolution still intact — June 28: After Iran's June 27 drone strike on the M/T Kiku tanker (~2M barrels of Qatari crude), the US launched a second, LARGER round of airstrikes on Iran — the day-on-day repeat of the June 25-26 cycle is the sharpest durability stress yet, but it again runs on the air/maritime rung and does NOT reverse the core resolution: the executed MOU still holds, the blockade stays lifted (Day 10), Hormuz stays open (JMIC widened the transit route), and oil stayed below $73/bbl. The OPEN question stays durability over the 60-day window — now specifically whether the breach-retaliation cycle keeps repeating. || First friction in the 60-day roadmap, core resolution intact — June 24: A US-Iran dispute erupted over whether Iran agreed to IAEA inspections of the bombed nuclear sites (Vance/Trump say yes; Iran's Baghaei says no), capped by Trump's threat to 'cut off talks immediately.' This is the first open friction in the roadmap and the cleanest live durability test (see [[iran-nuclear-inspections-dispute-june-2026]]), but it does NOT reverse the core resolution: the executed MOU still holds, the blockade stays lifted, tanker traffic rose to a war-period high, and oil fell to ~$75/bbl. The OPEN question stays durability over the 60-day window. || Resolved toward de-escalation and now further elaborated — a 60-day roadmap to a final deal — June 23: The first round of technical talks at Bürgenstock CONCLUDED June 22 with mediators Qatar and Pakistan announcing a 'roadmap toward reaching a final deal within 60 days,' plus three concrete mechanisms — a US/Iran/Lebanon deconfliction cell (Araghchi's '1st real test'), a communications channel to keep Hormuz open (defusing the June 20 closure dispute), and a reaffirmed sanctions/reconstruction package. This consolidates the core resolution (a real, executed settlement now being operationalized, not another slipped announcement) and the US side stays de-escalatory (blockade lifted, Boxer ARG disaggregated). The OPEN question narrows to durability over the 60-day window: Lebanon fighting continues (the deconfliction cell is unproven; Israel is not a party), and a roadmap is a process, not a finished peace — so it cannot yet be adjudicated as confirmed. || Resolved toward de-escalation but the deal stayed visibly stressed through a precarious weekend — June 22: The executed interim MOU still does the load-bearing work (signed and in effect since June 17; US naval blockade stays lifted), and the process did NOT collapse: the postponed in-person technical talks RECONVENED Sunday June 21 at Bürgenstock (US/Iran/Pakistan/Qatar; Vance + Ghalibaf), with Vance claiming \"great progress\" and \"a new leaf,\" though no final agreement landed. But durability stayed under real pressure: Iran's June 20 Hormuz closure declaration is DISPUTED by the US (CENTCOM: \"Iran does not control the Strait... traffic continues to flow,\" 55 ships / >17M barrels June 20), the renewed Lebanon ceasefire was violated (~145 killed over two days), the toll dispute stayed open, and rhetoric sharpened (Trump: \"hit Iran very hard again\"). This does NOT reverse the core resolution (a real, executed settlement, not another slipped announcement) and the US side remains de-escalatory (blockade lifted, Boxer ARG disaggregated, talks reconvening). || Resolved toward de-escalation but the deal is now visibly stressed — June 20-21: The executed interim MOU still does the load-bearing work (it was signed and took effect June 17; the US naval blockade stays lifted), but durability came under real pressure: Iran's IRGC RE-CLOSED the Strait of Hormuz on June 20 — reversing the four-day-old reopening from its own side — in protest at Israel's continued Lebanon strikes, which Tehran cast as a US failure to enforce the MOU, and warned of 'reciprocal action.' The Lebanon ceasefire renewed on June 19 was violated within a day (≥32 killed June 20, 'putting US-Iran talks in peril'), and the toll dispute stayed open (Trump: Iran won't charge Hormuz tolls but the US might). Yet the process did not collapse: VP Vance departed for the postponed Bürgenstock technical talks, which Pakistan said were due to reconvene Sunday June 21, and Iran said it was 'ready to move forward' if Israel abides. This does NOT reverse the core resolution (a real, executed settlement, not another slipped announcement) and the US side remains de-escalatory (blockade lifted, naval drawdown) — but it sharpens the DURABILITY question: whether the re-closure and ceasefire violation are coercive bargaining the Sunday talks resolve, or the first substantive unravelling. Kept on the register until the reopening durably normalizes and the Lebanon/toll disputes resolve. || Largely resolved toward de-escalation, but follow-on talks stumble — June 19-20: The signed-and-in-effect interim MOU continued to do the load-bearing work (blockade lifted, Hormuz reopening, Lebanon ceasefire renewed), but the in-person follow-on process hit an early snag: Switzerland's foreign ministry said the planned Bürgenstock meeting of the U.S., Iran, Qatar and Pakistan 'has been postponed' with no new date, after VP Vance canceled his trip (White House citing 'logistics') and Iran delayed its delegation over Israel's Lebanon operations; the 60-day nuclear/sanctions window did not formally open. This does not reverse the core resolution (a real, executed settlement, not another slipped announcement) but reopens the DURABILITY question: whether the postponement is a brief reschedule or the first crack. Kept on the register until the talks resume, the reopening normalizes, and the toll/Lebanon disputes resolve. || Largely resolved toward de-escalation — June 18-19: The deal moved from contested announcement to substantial CONSUMMATION. Trump and Iranian President Pezeshkian signed the MOU electronically (June 17; Trump at a candlelit G7 dinner outside Paris, Pezeshkian remotely) and it took immediate effect, cancelling the in-person Friday June 19 Geneva ceremony (teams still convene at Bürgenstock Friday for a 60-day nuclear/sanctions window); US Central Command announced the naval blockade of Iranian ports has been LIFTED (military vessels remaining only to monitor compliance); and the Strait of Hormuz began reopening — ~18 vessels transited June 17-18 (Windward), the first transit in ~110 days (Lloyd's List), via the northern (Iranian) and southern (Omani) routes while ~80 mines on the central channel are cleared. This substantially answers the core question (a real, executed settlement, not another slipped announcement) and is the cleanest disconfirmation yet of the imminent-ground-invasion / seizure thesis. Residual contestation is now about DURABILITY, not existence: full Hormuz normalization is 'weeks' away (central route still mined), the toll/fee question is disputed (Vance 'no tolls or no deal' vs. Iran's 'charge fees for services' with Oman), and Netanyahu's refusal to withdraw from Lebanon keeps a resumption tripwire live. Kept on the register (not retired) until the reopening durably normalizes and the toll/Lebanon disputes resolve. || Contested — June 17-18: The deal survived the close of the G7 but stayed short of consummation. Trump wrapped the summit defending 'the Trump deal' (14-point; Iran 'never' a nuke; Hormuz reopens) and warned the US 'may restart its bombing campaign' if Iran breaks it; allies endorsed it (Macron: 'a very good deal'). But the full MOU text was still unreleased days after the 'within 24-48 hours' pledge, officials described it as merely 'a commitment to negotiate' with no plan yet for Iran's nuclear capabilities, the in-person Friday June 19 Swiss signing (Vance for the US) had not occurred, Hormuz remained closed in practice (ADNOC: ~230 tankers waiting), and oil fell a fourth straight session to ~$79 (a three-month low). Whether the war has durably ended cannot be adjudicated from open sources until the ceremony, the text release, and the actual Hormuz reopening hold. || Contested — June 16-17: The deal held through the opening of the G7 summit. At the G7 (June 16) Trump touted the MOU ('the oil is plummeting down and the stock market is shooting up like a rocket today'), said the US 'will not invest money' in Iran, and a senior US official said the full text would be released 'within 24 to 48 hours' — narrowing the prior 'some time after Friday.' The in-person signing stayed set for Friday June 19 in Switzerland (Swiss FM: the Bürgenstock resort near Luzern; some reports still cite Geneva), with each side to sign electronically and deputy FM Gharibabadi saying the text was finalized. Oil fell a fourth straight session to near $80 (lowest since early March). But the text is still unreleased, the ceremony has not occurred, Hormuz stays closed (~12-15 ships/day) pending a 40-50-day international mine-clearing mission, and a new dispute opened over whether the deal obliges an Israeli withdrawal from southern Lebanon (Iran says yes, a US official says no) — keeping a resumption tripwire live. Whether the war has durably ended cannot be adjudicated from open sources until the ceremony and the actual Hormuz reopening hold. || Contested — June 15-16: The deal firmed toward consummation. VP Vance disclosed June 15 that the MOU had already been 'digitally' signed June 14 by Trump, Vance and Iran's Parliament Speaker Qalibaf; Trump said Khamenei had approved it ('I understand the answer is yes'); and Iran's SNSC confirmed a finalized 14-point deal (permanent cessation on all fronts incl. Lebanon, blockade lifted within 30 days, US forces withdrawn, Hormuz reopened, oil sanctions suspended, nuclear deal within 60 days, ~$24B released). Oil hit a three-month low and Asian equities soared. But the full text is unreleased ('some time after Friday'), the in-person Geneva ceremony is not until Friday June 19, the blockade lifts only 'within 30 days' with mine-clearing that could run a year, and Israel's continued Lebanon strikes plus Netanyahu's 'freedom of operation' stance and ministers' 'won't be bound' line keep a resumption tripwire live. Whether the war has durably ended cannot be adjudicated from open sources until the ceremony and the actual Hormuz reopening hold. || Contested — June 14-15: A deal was jointly announced by Trump and Pakistan's PM Sharif and broadcast by Iranian state media, with both sides declaring a permanent termination of operations, Trump authorizing the blockade lift and toll-free Hormuz reopening, and a Friday June 19 Geneva signing scheduled — yet the text is unsigned, Khamenei's approval is unconfirmed, Iran's Fars/FM had denied an 'approved text' earlier the same day, and an Israeli Beirut/southern-Lebanon strike plus Netanyahu's non-binding stance keep a resumption tripwire live. Whether the war has actually ended cannot be adjudicated from open sources until the signing either occurs and holds or slips.",
      "synopsis": "On June 14, 2026 (Day 108→109), President Trump and Pakistani PM Shehbaz Sharif jointly announced that the US and Iran had reached a deal to end the war; Iranian state media broadcast it. Both sides declared 'the immediate and permanent termination of military operations on all fronts, including in Lebanon.' Trump authorized the toll-free reopening of the Strait of Hormuz and the immediate removal of the US naval blockade, with a formal signing set for Friday June 19 in Switzerland (Geneva). The question is whether this is a genuine war-ending settlement — which would be the cleanest disconfirmation yet of Predictive History's imminent-ground-invasion / Hormuz-and-Kharg-seizure thesis, since the chokepoint would be reopened by negotiation and US forces withdrawn rather than seized — or the latest in a documented series of unconsummated Trump deal-announcements that have slipped repeatedly since late May.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "July 8, 2026: Trump, at the NATO Ankara summit, called the ceasefire/MOU 'over' and 'a waste of time,' Iran 'vicious, violent,' warned 'every time they hit us, we hit them 20,' and threatened renewed bombing/blockade.",
        "July 8, 2026: same day, Trump said the fighting would not lead to long-term military action — 'I don't think it's going to start again.'",
        "July 8, 2026: CENTCOM ran 'additional strikes' (aggregated 80+ targets, 60+ IRGC small boats); no ground move, Kharg assault, or blockade reimposition followed; blockade stayed lifted (Day 21).",
        "June 22: The first round of US-Iran technical talks at the Bürgenstock resort concluded with mediators Qatar and Pakistan announcing a 'roadmap towards reaching a final deal within 60 days,' with technical talks to continue through the week at Bürgenstock and a further round reported for June 23-25 in Washington. Concrete outputs: a US/Iran/Lebanon deconfliction cell (mediator-facilitated) to enforce the cessation of operations in Lebanon — Iran's FM Araghchi called it the '1st real test'; a communications channel to keep the Strait of Hormuz open; and, per Araghchi, terms under which 'oil and petrol exports are waived, blockade lifted, some frozen assets released, and a major reconstruction & development plan launched for Iran.' Talks were led by VP Vance and Speaker Ghalibaf. Brent eased ~0.4% to ~$80.26 on the roadmap. (CNBC; NPR; Al Jazeera; Iran International, June 22, 2026.)",
        "June 18: Trump and Iranian President Pezeshkian signed the MOU electronically (June 17, Trump at a candlelit dinner outside Paris during the G7, Pezeshkian remotely); the agreement took immediate effect and the in-person Friday June 19 Geneva ceremony was cancelled, though teams still convene at Bürgenstock Friday for a 60-day nuclear/sanctions window. US Central Command announced the naval blockade of Iranian ports has been lifted (military vessels remain only to monitor compliance). Commercial tankers transited Hormuz for the first time in ~110 days (Windward: ~18 vessels June 17-18; Lloyd's List) via northern/southern routes, the central channel still mined (~80 mines); VP Vance cited 12.5M barrels overnight but warned 'no tolls or no deal,' against Iran's signal it will 'charge fees for services' with Oman. Brent held ~$77. (Al Jazeera; CBS News; CNBC; Business Standard; TradingEconomics, June 17-18, 2026.)",
        "June 17: At the close of the G7 in the French Alps, Trump defended 'the Trump deal' and warned the US 'may restart its bombing campaign' if Iran does not abide; Macron called it 'a very good deal'; no full MOU text was released days after the '24-48 hours' pledge (officials: ~a page and a half, 'a commitment to negotiate'); the Friday June 19 Swiss signing (Vance) remained pending; Brent fell a fourth straight session to ~$79; ADNOC's Al Jaber said Hormuz is still closed with ~230 tankers waiting. (WGME/Sinclair; NBC News; PBS NewsHour; IG International, June 17, 2026.)",
        "June 15: VP Vance disclosed on ABC's 'Good Morning America' that the MOU had already been 'digitally' signed on Sunday June 14 by Trump, Vance and Iran's Parliament Speaker Mohammad Baqer Qalibaf (per a senior US official); the in-person ceremony remains set for Friday June 19 in Geneva (Vance + Qalibaf). (The Times of Israel; Fox News, June 15, 2026.)",
        "June 15: Trump said Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei had approved the deal ('I understand the answer is yes') and that it was endorsed 'in concept and great detail' by the US, Israel, Saudi Arabia, the UAE, Qatar and others; Trump said the text would be released 'some time after Friday.' (The Times of Israel; Jerusalem Post, June 15, 2026.)",
        "June 15: Iran's Supreme National Security Council confirmed a finalized 14-point MOU (Mehr): immediate/permanent cessation of hostilities on all fronts incl. Lebanon; complete naval-blockade lifting within 30 days; US force withdrawal from around Iran; Hormuz reopening; oil-sales-sanctions suspension; nuclear agreement within 60 days; ~$24B frozen-asset release during talks. (Al Jazeera, June 15, 2026.)",
        "June 15: Oil hit a three-month low — WTI -4.8% to ~$80.75, Brent -4.7% to ~$83.17 (lowest since early March) — and Asian/global equities soared (Nikkei +5.5%, Kospi +5.7%). Analysts cautioned Hormuz mine-clearing could take 'months' to 'over a year.' (NBC News; Al Jazeera, June 15, 2026.)",
        "June 14: Trump and Pakistan's PM Shehbaz Sharif jointly announced a deal to end the war; Iranian state media broadcast it (NBC News; PBS NewsHour; CBS News; Axios).",
        "Sharif posted the peace deal 'has been REACHED' and that 'both sides have declared the immediate and permanent termination of military operations on all fronts, including in Lebanon.'",
        "Trump posted the deal is 'now complete... I hereby fully authorize the toll free opening of the Strait of Hormuz' and authorized 'the immediate removal of the United States Naval blockade' ('Let the oil flow!').",
        "Reported MOU: 60-day ceasefire extension; Hormuz reopened without tolls (Iran clears its mines); US lifts the blockade and withdraws forces from the Gulf region; ~15-20 yr Iranian enrichment lockout + nuclear-site dismantling; staggered ~$25B frozen-asset release tied to compliance.",
        "Signing set for Friday June 19 in Switzerland (Geneva), electronically by Trump or in person by VP Vance.",
        "Oil plunged: WTI >4.5% to ~$80 and Brent ~4% to ~$83 — both lowest since early March.",
        "Counter-facts: the text is unsigned; Khamenei's approval was not publicly confirmed; earlier June 14 Iran's Fars/Foreign Ministry said 'no text... has been approved'; Israel struck Hezbollah in Beirut's Dahieh and killed a senior commander in southern Lebanon; Netanyahu told Trump Israel would not be bound to halt Lebanon operations; some Hormuz tankers reportedly paused."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "Skeptics read the June 14 announcement as another unconsummated, contested deal-claim rather than a settled end to the war: Trump has called a deal imminent roughly 39 times; the MOU is unsigned until Friday; Khamenei's ratification is unconfirmed; Iran's own state-linked outlets denied an approved text the same day; and the live Lebanon track (Israeli strikes, Netanyahu non-binding) could still trigger an Iranian 'full-scale resumption' before signing — so the war may not actually be over.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "Trump administration (Truth Social; WSJ)",
        "Pakistan PM Shehbaz Sharif",
        "Iranian state media / deputy foreign minister",
        "skeptical analysts and commentators citing prior slipped deal timelines"
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "The post-funeral US-Iran round convening on schedule and producing a documented, executed frozen-asset tranche plus a durable Hormuz-passage arrangement (promotes toward confirmed of de-escalation).",
        "Conversely, the post-funeral round failing to convene or collapsing over the toll or IAEA-inspection disputes (returns the question to active friction, though in the diplomatic/air/maritime register, not a ground invasion).",
        "The in-person Friday June 19 Geneva ceremony and public release of the full MOU text, with the ceasefire holding (promotes toward confirmed of de-escalation / disconfirmed of ground-invasion).",
        "Commercial tanker transits resuming through Hormuz and a documented physical lifting of the US naval blockade within the 30-day window (confirms the reopening).",
        "Conversely, a collapse of the ceremony or an Iranian 'resumption' over the Lebanon strikes (returns the question to active kinetic conflict, not a ground invasion)."
      ],
      "current_assessment": "As of July 11 the breach-retaliation spike gave way to a diplomacy-forward pause and the framework was breached-but-not-dead. No major new US-Iran kinetic exchange was confirmed for July 10-11; US officials said Washington was 'deliberately striking and then pausing to avoid escalation and let diplomacy work' and 'letting diplomacy take the lead,' technical nuclear talks continued, Pakistan and Qatar pressed both sides back toward negotiations, and Trump said Iran had contacted the US seeking a new deal ('Iran wants to make a deal so bad'). The MOU is materially breached and rhetorically repudiated by the US president but not formally cancelled; talks are paused, not terminated. Brent eased toward $77/bbl and Hormuz tanker traffic was near a standstill. Stays contested_unresolved: durability turns on whether the mediation reconvenes formal talks, whether the US reinstates the blockade, and whether Iran mounts a materially escalatory follow-on. || As of July 10, durability risk stayed at its highest point yet but the framework was not dead. On July 9 the exchange widened from a US-Iran duel into a regional one: Iran retaliated against Kuwait, Qatar, Bahrain and Jordan (mostly intercepted; one stable injury in Kuwait), the US ran a ~90-target second-day wave (~14 killed in Iran), and Trump said the US had 'attacked Kharg Island' and 'maybe we'll take over Kharg Island' — his sharpest seizure rhetoric since June 11 — while deliberately sparing Kharg's oil terminals and doubting US 'stomach' for a ground fight. Yet the MOU was not formally cancelled, no US ground move followed, the blockade stayed lifted (Day 22), Brent eased from $78.02 back toward $77, and by July 10 Pakistan and Qatar were in fresh contact with both sides to stop the strikes and restore talks, with Iran reportedly seeking a new deal. The framework is materially breached and rhetorically repudiated by the US president, but talks are paused rather than terminated. The question stays contested_unresolved: the near-term test is whether the US reinstates the blockade or moves to a ground/amphibious seizure of Kharg, whether Iran mounts a materially escalatory follow-on, and whether the Pakistan/Qatar track reconvenes formal negotiations.",
      "first_listed": "2026-06-15"
    },
    {
      "id": "iran-deal-israel-lebanon-withdrawal-june-2026",
      "title": "Does the US-Iran MOU require Israel to withdraw from southern Lebanon? Iran (FM Araghchi) says the deal is not complete without it; a US official says the deal 'did not call for an Israeli withdrawal.'",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "Still contested, but a concrete enforcement mechanism was created — June 23: The June 22 Bürgenstock round established a US/Iran/Lebanon 'deconfliction cell' (mediator-facilitated) to enforce the cessation of operations in Lebanon, which Araghchi called the deal's '1st real test.' This is a process to manage the Lebanon front, not a resolution of the underlying withdrawal dispute: Netanyahu still maintains Israel will 'not withdraw' from its southern-Lebanon security zone, Iran still holds that continued Israeli operations violate the deal, Israel is not a party to the cell's parent agreement, and fighting continued. Whether the deal obliges an Israeli withdrawal remains unadjudicated from open sources; the deconfliction cell's performance is now the thing to watch. || Still contested and now the deal's active tripwire — June 20-21: The renewed Israel-Hezbollah ceasefire (June 19) was violated within a day — Israeli strikes killed ≥32 in Lebanon on June 20, 'putting US-Iran talks in peril' — and Iran cited the continued Israeli operations as its reason for RE-CLOSING the Strait of Hormuz, making the withdrawal/operations dispute the proximate cause of a concrete deal setback for the first time. The underlying question is unchanged and unresolved: Netanyahu maintains Israel will 'not withdraw' from its southern-Lebanon security zone and retains 'complete military freedom of action,' while Iran holds that an Israeli presence/continued strikes violate the deal; the US announced a new round of Israel-Lebanon talks in Washington. The determinative MOU text remains unadjudicated publicly and Israel is not a party, so open sources cannot resolve whether the deal obliges an Israeli withdrawal. || Still contested — June 19-20: A sharp Lebanon flare-up (≥16 killed in Lebanon overnight, four IDF soldiers killed) nearly derailed the US-Iran Switzerland talks before Trump phoned Israel and Israel and Hezbollah agreed to RENEW their ceasefire. The renewal capped the immediate fighting but left the underlying dispute unresolved: Netanyahu reiterated Israel will 'not withdraw' from its southern-Lebanon security zone and retains 'complete military freedom of action,' while Iran maintains an Israeli presence violates the deal. The withdrawal question remains a live tripwire. || Contested — June 18: The Lebanon dispute stayed the deal's live fault line even as the MOU was signed and the blockade lifted. The agreement calls for an 'immediate and permanent termination of military operations on all fronts, including in Lebanon,' but Netanyahu declared Israel will 'not withdraw from' its southern-Lebanon security zone, Iran's foreign minister said Israeli forces remaining would violate the agreement, and Hezbollah claimed it repelled Israeli advances Thursday (Lebanon toll ~3,912 killed / 11,873 wounded since March 2). With the full text still not adjudicated publicly and Israel not a party, the two readings cannot be reconciled from open sources; the question now bears directly on whether the just-consummated ceasefire holds. || Contested — June 17: The Lebanon dispute hardened. Israeli ministers said Israel 'won't be bound' by the US-Iran deal and threatened to hit Hezbollah with 'full force' if attacked, while Netanyahu pledged to hold 'security zones'; Iran's Araghchi reiterated the deal requires an Israeli withdrawal from Lebanon, and a US official continued to say it does not; Trump repeated he is 'not happy' with Israel's handling of Lebanon. With the full text still unreleased and Israel not a party to the US-Iran deal, the readings cannot be reconciled from open sources. || Contested — June 16: On the day the deal held through the G7, a documented dispute opened over what the still-unreleased MOU requires on Lebanon. Iran's FM Abbas Araghchi said Israel's continued occupation of southern Lebanon would violate the agreement — 'Without the withdrawal of Israeli forces from the territories they occupied during this war, the war has not fully come to an end' — while an anonymous US official said 'the deal did not call for an Israeli withdrawal.' Netanyahu said Israel would remain in Lebanon 'as long as necessary' and hold 'security zones'; Trump said he was 'not happy' with Israel's handling of Hezbollah ('it just goes on forever'). Because the full text is unreleased and Israel is not a party to the US-Iran deal, the two readings cannot be reconciled from open sources.",
      "synopsis": "On June 16, 2026 (Day 110→111), as the June 14 US-Iran war-ending MOU held through the opening of the G7 summit, a sharp interpretive dispute surfaced over the deal's Lebanon terms. Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi publicly asserted that the agreement requires Israel to withdraw from the southern-Lebanon territory it occupied during the war, and that without such a withdrawal 'the war has not fully come to an end.' A US official, speaking anonymously, countered that 'the deal did not call for an Israeli withdrawal.' Israeli Prime Minister Netanyahu, who is not a party to the US-Iran MOU, said Israel would stay in Lebanon 'as long as necessary' and hold 'security zones.' The question is whether an Israeli withdrawal from southern Lebanon is a binding condition of the US-Iran deal (as Iran asserts) or a separate matter outside it (as the US official asserts) — a dispute that bears directly on whether the ceasefire holds, since Iran has tied a 'full-scale resumption' to the Lebanon track. It is tracked as contested_unresolved because the determinative MOU text is unreleased and the two governments' public assertions are flatly opposed.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "June 16-17: Israeli ministers said Israel will not be bound by the US-Iran deal and threatened Hezbollah with 'full force'; Netanyahu pledged 'security zones'; Araghchi reiterated the Israeli-withdrawal precondition; a US official said the deal does not require a withdrawal; Trump said he is 'not happy' with Israel. (NPR; CBS News; The Times of Israel; PBS NewsHour, June 16-17, 2026.)",
        "June 16, 2026: Iranian FM Araghchi said Israel's continued occupation of southern Lebanon would violate the deal — 'Without the withdrawal of Israeli forces from the territories they occupied during this war, the war has not fully come to an end' (PBS NewsHour, June 16, 2026).",
        "June 16, 2026: An anonymous US official said 'the deal did not call for an Israeli withdrawal' (PBS NewsHour; NBC News, June 16, 2026).",
        "June 15, 2026: Netanyahu said Israel would remain in Lebanon 'as long as necessary,' hold 'security zones,' and that 'the struggle is not over' (The Times of Israel; NBC News).",
        "June 16, 2026: At the G7, Trump said 'I'm not happy with the way Israel has handled themselves with Lebanon and with Hezbollah' and 'it just goes on forever' (PBS NewsHour; CNBC).",
        "The full MOU text is unreleased (a senior US official said it would be published 'within 24 to 48 hours'); Israel is not a signatory to the US-Iran agreement.",
        "Iran has repeatedly tied a 'full-scale resumption' of hostilities to the Lebanon track (FM Araghchi's earlier warnings; Qalibaf's June 15 warning that Israeli Lebanon strikes threaten the deal).",
        "The reported MOU summaries (e.g. Mehr's 14-point list) describe a 'permanent cessation of hostilities on all fronts including Lebanon' but do not, in public reporting, specify an Israeli withdrawal mechanism or timetable."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "Read one way, an Israeli withdrawal from southern Lebanon is a binding term of the US-Iran MOU's 'cessation on all fronts including Lebanon' clause, making Iranian compliance contingent on an Israeli pullback. Read the other way, the MOU binds only the US and Iran, treats Israel's Lebanon presence as a separate Israeli-Lebanese matter outside the deal, and a US official's denial reflects the actual text — leaving Israel free to remain in its 'security zones' without breaching the US-Iran agreement.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "Iran's Foreign Ministry (FM Abbas Araghchi)",
        "an anonymous US official",
        "Israeli government (PM Netanyahu)"
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "Public release of the full MOU text showing whether an Israeli withdrawal from southern Lebanon is a stipulated condition (expected 'within 24-48 hours' per a US official).",
        "A documented Israeli withdrawal from, or formalized continued presence in, southern Lebanon following the Friday June 19 signing.",
        "An Iranian decision to either proceed with the signing despite Israel's Lebanon presence (suggesting it is not a binding condition) or to make signing/compliance explicitly contingent on a withdrawal (confirming Iran's reading)."
      ],
      "current_assessment": "Still contested at June 23, but the parties created a concrete enforcement mechanism: the June 22 Bürgenstock round established a US/Iran/Lebanon deconfliction cell (mediator-facilitated) to enforce the cessation of operations in Lebanon — Araghchi's '1st real test.' This manages the front without resolving the underlying dispute: Netanyahu's 'will not withdraw' and Iran's 'that violates the deal' remain flatly opposed, Israel is not a party, fighting continued, and the determinative MOU text is unadjudicated publicly. The cell's performance over the 60-day window is now the key durability signal; it is a ceasefire-enforcement question, not a US ground move; closely coupled to iran-us-deal-reached-june-2026. || Still contested and now the deal's active tripwire at June 21: the renewed Lebanon ceasefire was violated within a day (≥32 killed June 20) and Iran cited Israel's Lebanon operations as its reason for re-closing Hormuz — so the withdrawal/operations dispute has become the proximate cause of a concrete deal setback, not merely a latent risk. Netanyahu's 'will not withdraw' and Iran's 'that violates the deal' remain flatly opposed, the determinative text is unadjudicated publicly, and Israel is not a party; open sources cannot resolve whether the deal obliges an Israeli withdrawal. It is a ceasefire-durability dispute, not evidence of a US ground move; closely coupled to iran-us-deal-reached-june-2026. || Still contested and now the leading durability risk at June 19: with the MOU signed and the blockade lifted, Lebanon is the most likely trigger for any unraveling. Netanyahu's 'will not withdraw' and Iran's 'that violates the deal' remain flatly opposed, the determinative text is unadjudicated publicly, and Israel is not a party — so open sources cannot resolve whether the deal obliges an Israeli withdrawal. It is a ceasefire-durability dispute, not evidence of a US ground move; closely coupled to iran-us-deal-reached-june-2026. || Still contested and hardening at June 18: Israeli ministers' 'won't be bound' line sharpened the dispute, but with the MOU text unreleased and Israel not a party, whether the deal obliges an Israeli withdrawal from Lebanon cannot be resolved from open sources. As of June 16-17 this is genuinely unresolved: the MOU text that would adjudicate it is unreleased, and the US and Iranian governments have made flatly opposed public assertions. It is a live deal-risk question — Iran has tied a resumption to Lebanon — but it is an interpretation/ceasefire-durability dispute, not evidence of a US ground move; it bears on whether the war ends cleanly, and is closely coupled to iran-us-deal-reached-june-2026.",
      "first_listed": "2026-06-17"
