    {
      "id": "kharg-seizure-total-control-threat-june-2026",
      "title": "Trump's June 11 declaration that the US 'will be taking Kharg Island' and assume 'total control' of Iran's oil and gas (Venezuela model) — a genuine statement of seizure/occupation intent, or maximalist coercive-bargaining rhetoric retracted the same day?",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "The campaign that was supposed to precede a seizure STOPS — July 26 (Day 150): after 13 consecutive nights, Iran reported no US strikes overnight into July 25 ('Iran had a peaceful night') and CENTCOM announced nothing and declined comment. Trump said the US is 'locked and loaded' but that the threatened 'massive' attack 'may be unnecessary,' calling Iranian negotiators 'by far the most serious that we've seen them'; The Jerusalem Post reported he has NOT decided on large-scale attacks ('finger on trigger'). The July 24 Pentagon ground-ops OPTIONS reporting (Kharg among possible raid targets) still stands but remains unexecuted, and Kharg itself receded from the cycle as the war's kinetic centre moved to the Red Sea. Ex-SecDef Esper read the pause the other way — possible repositioning for 'something much bigger.' No landing, no seizure, no new ARG/MEU order, no draft; 50,000+ personnel unchanged. || Planning signal rises toward Kharg, still no landing — July 25 (Day 149): reporting that the Pentagon is readying OPTIONS for 'weeks' of limited ground operations — SpecOps/conventional raids explicitly framed as NOT a full-scale invasion, with possible targets including Kharg Island and coastal weapons sites near Hormuz — pushed the seizure question from rhetoric toward planning. Trump told Axios he is 'considering' restarting major combat operations 'bigger than Operation Epic Fury' (no decision); Iran rejected an Iraqi-brokered ceasefire over Hormuz control and FM Baghaei warned of 'repercussions' over Kharg ('we never accept humiliation'). Yet execution stayed at zero: no US troops in Iran, no Kharg landing/seizure, no new ARG/MEU order, no draft; ~50,000 personnel unchanged. Planning up, execution unchanged — the intent-vs-execution gap widens rather than closes. || Air campaign intensifies, diplomacy cools, still no landing — July 23 (Day 147): the US ran an 11th straight night (~75 min) while Rubio called Tehran 'not serious' and Trump dismissed ceasefire calls; Brent passed $95 and the Houthi Bab el-Mandeb threat opened a second chokepoint. Trump reiterated 'we have other people that will do the ground campaign for us,' displacing any ground effort onto proxies. No US troops in Iran, no Kharg landing or new ARG/MEU order, no draft; ~50,000 personnel unchanged. || US-casualty tripwire crossed, still no landing — July 20: the war took its first US combat deaths of the resumed phase (2 killed in Jordan July 17, 1 in Iraq July 19), meeting the condition reporting had tied to authorizing a Kharg strike — yet the US answered with an eighth 'punishment' air night (a first strike on a nuclear plant, Darkhovin; widening into Khuzestan), NOT a landing. Newsweek reported Trump 'floats a ground campaign' (declared intent). No landing ordered; ~50,000 personnel unchanged; Kharg plans stay 'continuously shelved' as too risky. || Closest interdiction approach to Kharg yet, still no landing — July 17: on a sixth straight day of strikes the US widened its air campaign into northern Iran and, under the re-imposed blockade, DISABLED an unladen tanker HEADED FOR KHARG ISLAND that was trying to run the blockade — the interdiction campaign's closest approach to the island, but a maritime action, not a landing or seizure. Trump told Fox News the US would 'keep the Strait' and be its 'Guardian Angel' (and be 'reimbursed'); Araghchi retorted Iran remains 'the GUARDIAN of the Strait... FOREVER.' Kharg plans stay 'continuously shelved' as too risky (Task & Purpose: >=1,200 troops needed). || Contested — June 14-15: The seizure pathway was further superseded by the June 14 deal: Trump authorized the toll-free reopening of Hormuz and the immediate removal of the US naval blockade, routing the chokepoint/oil question through a negotiated reopening rather than a Kharg seizure; no US ground troops landed on Kharg and no assault was ordered. The salience-vs-execution gap is now near-moot if the deal holds, but cannot be fully closed until the Friday signing. || Contested — June 13-14: On the war's scheduled signing day the seizure language stayed unexercised: Trump said the deal would be signed Sunday with Hormuz to reopen 'OPEN TO ALL' and the MOU to withdraw US forces and lift the blockade — the inverse of a seizure. The only US move was C-17s carrying signing-ceremony equipment to Europe, not assault staging. Trump's restated (but undated, 'when all is calm') intent to 'go in and get the Nuclear Dust' keeps a future in-country-entry scenario nominally alive without moving execution. Salience-vs-execution gap unresolved from open sources while nothing is signed. || Contested — June 12-13: One day after the Kharg-seizure / 'total control' threat, the US advanced a negotiated settlement (Pakistan: 'final, agreed upon text') whose reported terms reopen Hormuz and withdraw US forces — leaving the June 11 statement unexercised and the deal route dominant. The salience-vs-execution gap stands: whether the seizure language was operational intent (now deferred) or pure leverage still cannot be adjudicated from open sources. || Contested — June 11-12: The most explicit US territorial/resource-seizure statement of the war was issued and walked back within hours; whether it reflected an operational intent (since deferred) or pure negotiating leverage cannot be adjudicated from open sources.",
      "synopsis": "On June 11, 2026, amid the sharpest US-Iran kinetic cycle of the war, President Trump posted that 'we will be taking Kharg Island, and other oil infrastructure points, and assume total control of their Oil and Gas Markets, much like we have with Venezuela,' and vowed to hit Iran 'VERY HARD TONIGHT.' Kharg handles ~90% of Iran's crude exports. Within hours he told Fox News he was 'not sure America has the stomach' to take Kharg, cancelled the threatened strikes, and announced a negotiated 'settlement.' The question is whether the seizure language reflected a genuine plan to seize and occupy Iranian oil infrastructure (the strongest validation yet of the 'resource-seizure / occupation' thesis, consistent with the standing 82nd Airborne forward HQ and the long-rumored Kharg contingency) or maximalist coercive-bargaining rhetoric timed to extract final concessions before a deal.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "Trump posted on June 11, 2026: 'At some point in the not too distant future, we will be taking Kharg Island, and other oil infrastructure points, and assume total control of their Oil and Gas Markets, much like we have with Venezuela,' and that the US military would hit Iran 'VERY HARD TONIGHT.' (Euronews; CNBC; Axios; Times of Israel, June 11, 2026.)",
        "Within hours Trump told Fox News he was 'not sure America has the stomach' to take Kharg, cancelled the threatened Thursday-night strikes citing discussions brought to Iran's 'highest level' and approved, and announced 'a great settlement of the war with Iran.' (NPR; NBC News, June 11, 2026.)",
        "No US ground troops landed on Kharg and no ground assault was ordered or executed; the standing capability (~50,000 in CENTCOM, three carrier strike groups, USS Tripoli ARG / 31st MEU, the 82nd Airborne forward HQ as a Kharg forcible-entry contingency) was unchanged.",
        "Kharg Island handles ~90% of Iran's crude exports; the US had previously struck 90+ military targets on Kharg (Mar 13, 2026) while deliberately sparing oil infrastructure, and Kharg occupation had been reported 'under serious consideration' (Axios, Mar 20, 2026).",
        "On July 24, 2026 the Pentagon was reported to be preparing OPTIONS for potential US ground operations in Iran that could last weeks if Trump approves — reportedly limited raids by special operations and conventional forces (NOT a full-scale invasion), with possible targets including Kharg Island and coastal weapons sites near the Strait of Hormuz; Iran's FM spokesman Baghaei warned of 'repercussions' if the US moves against Kharg and said Tehran would 'never accept humiliation.' No landing was ordered or executed. (Foreign Policy; Just Security; Fox News, July 24, 2026.)"
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "Either (a) a genuine statement of strategic intent to seize and control Iranian oil territory — the leading public indicator yet of a planned resource-grab / occupation — or (b) maximalist coercive-bargaining rhetoric / a bluff, given the same-day 'no stomach' walk-back, the cancelled strikes, and the immediate pivot to announcing a negotiated settlement.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "Commentators and Iran-hawk voices treating the Kharg/'total control' statement as a declared war aim and resource-seizure signal",
        "Administration framing and market reaction treating it as pressure tactics that immediately gave way to a negotiated settlement",
        "Skeptics noting the recurrent pattern of escalatory Trump threats that resolve into deal announcements (the May-June deadline cycle)"
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "An actual order to, or execution of, a ground landing or occupation of Kharg Island or other Iranian oil infrastructure (would confirm seizure intent)",
        "A signed settlement that reopens Hormuz and lifts the blockade without any seizure (would confirm the bargaining-bluff reading)",
        "Credible reporting of a real, since-deferred operational plan tied to the June 11 statement",
        "Continued indefinite stalemate with neither a seizure nor a signed deal (would leave the question contested)"
      ],
      "current_assessment": "As of July 26 (Day 150) the coercion/stand-off reading is further favored in execution terms, and for the first time the trend line moved away from seizure: the 13-night air campaign that the planning reporting framed as the shaping phase simply stopped, with no CENTCOM announcement and no successor operation, while Trump paired 'locked and loaded' with 'may be unnecessary' and two mediation channels opened (an Omani delegation in Tehran on Hormuz traffic mechanisms; a Pakistan-led, China-encouraged push to restart talks). The July 24 Pentagon options for weeks of limited SpecOps/conventional raids — Kharg among possible targets, explicitly NOT a full-scale invasion — remain reported-but-undecided, and The Jerusalem Post reported Trump has not decided on large-scale attacks. Trump's standing public position on Kharg is conditional and outsourced ('I would' if US forces 'degrade them' enough; 'sometimes you need a ground campaign' but 'other people ... would do the campaign for us'). A seizure is still reported to require at least ~1,200 ground troops that have not been ordered in, and the takeover plans have been reported as repeatedly shelved as too risky. Against this, former Defense Secretary Mark Esper publicly read the pause as possible repositioning and reloading for 'something much bigger,' which keeps the intent question live. Execution: still zero. || As of July 25 (Day 149) the coercion/stand-off reading remains favored in execution terms even as the planning signal rose. The Pentagon is reported readying options for weeks of limited SpecOps/conventional raids (possible targets Kharg Island and coastal weapons sites near Hormuz), and Trump told Axios he is 'considering' a 'bigger than Operation Epic Fury' escalation — but has NOT decided, and the reported options are planning, not a movement of troops. Washington ran a 13th consecutive AIR night; Iran rejected an Iraqi-brokered ceasefire over Hormuz control; oil retreated ~4% to ~$97 after briefly topping $100. No US troops entered Iran, no Kharg landing or seizure, no new ARG/MEU order, and no draft; ~50,000 personnel unchanged. The question stays contested_unresolved and the intent-vs-execution gap now widens: an executed US ground seizure of Kharg/Hormuz would confirm it, while a signed ceasefire or a war that ends via air/maritime coercion alone would disconfirm it.",
      "first_listed": "2026-06-12"
    },
    {
      "id": "june3-kuwait-kinetic-escalation-2026",
      "title": "The June 2-9 US-Iran/Israel-Iran kinetic exchanges — the strikes on Kuwait and Bahrain, the June 7 Israel-Beirut strike and Iran's first barrage at Israel, and the June 8 direct two-sided Israel-Iran territorial exchange — coercive bargaining inside a still-live negotiation, or the leading edge of a return to open war?",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "Widened and sharpened July 9-10 — retaliation goes multi-state, then mediators re-engage — July 10: Iran's four-state retaliation (Kuwait, Qatar, Bahrain, Jordan; mostly intercepted) and Trump's 'we attacked Kharg Island / maybe we'll take over' push the escalatory reading to its broadest point, while the deliberate sparing of Kharg's oil terminals, the absence of any ground move, and the July 10 Pakistan/Qatar re-engagement (Iran reportedly seeking a deal) push the coercion/deterrence reading just as hard. The exchange widened horizontally, not up the rung; the ambiguity is unresolved. || Pattern revives — June 29: Iran's IRGC struck US bases in Kuwait (Ali Al Salem) and Bahrain (Fifth Fleet HQ) with ballistic missiles and drones on June 28 (first since June 10-11), in retaliation for US strikes on Iran; all intercepted/failed with no US injuries, but one Qatari civilian killed — a return to the Gulf-base-strike pattern, still on the air/missile rung. || Contested — June 13-14: The cycle de-escalated further toward closure as Trump set the signing for Sunday in Geneva and the US energy secretary reported rising Hormuz traffic — but the strikes were still not formally closed out (the text is unsigned, US confidence is sub-'100%,' and Iran has 'not yet reached a final decision'). Coercive-bargaining and return-to-war readings both survive on the unsigned text. Not resolvable from open sources until a deal is signed or the war visibly resumes. || Contested — June 12-13: The cycle de-escalated further as mediators reported a 'final, agreed upon text' and a weekend signing in Europe — yet the strikes were not formally closed out: overnight Iranian drones fired on Indian Hormuz shipping (US downed two), and Trump and Iran publicly disputed the deal's terms. Coercive-bargaining and return-to-war readings both survive on the unsigned, contested text. Not resolvable from open sources. || Contested — June 11-12: The cycle's sharpest US-Iran kinetic phase yet — an Apache downing trigger, two nights of US strikes (~49 Tomahawks June 10), Iran's Hormuz 'closed to all ships' declaration, and Iranian barrages on US bases in Bahrain, Kuwait and Jordan — escalated VERTICALLY and then RESOLVED the same day toward a negotiated 'settlement' when Trump cancelled the threatened strikes and announced a deal to be signed 'maybe in Europe.' Return-to-war and coercive-bargaining readings both survive; Iran's non-confirmation and the unsigned text keep the re-escalation channel open. Not resolvable from open sources. || Contested — June 10-11: The Israel-Iran mutual halt held into a third day with no new direct exchange; Iran stayed at the table via Pakistan and signaled willingness to keep talking while Trump pressed a near-term deal — continuing the de-escalatory resolution of the June 7-8 cycle. Return-to-war and bargaining-pressure readings both survive; the unsigned MOU and continued Israeli Lebanon strikes keep a re-escalation channel open; not resolvable from open sources.",
      "synopsis": "On June 2-3, 2026 the still-unsigned US-Iran 60-day MOU turned kinetic: the US struck two Iranian command-and-control sites near Hormuz (reported at Goruk and Qeshm Island) following an MQ-1 downing, and on June 2 a CENTCOM Hellfire disabled a Kharg-bound tanker; Iran said the US hit 'Kharg and Abu Musa' and a vessel, the IRGC claimed it struck a US airbase, and at dawn June 3 Iran fired 13 ballistic missiles and 17 drones at Gulf targets, with debris damaging Kuwait International Airport (1 killed, 63 wounded). The question is whether this is coercive bargaining pressure inside a negotiation both sides still expect to close (Trump/Rubio say talks continue; Vance calls a deal 'very close'; Ghalibaf frames missiles as the lever for 'concessions') or the leading edge of a genuine return to open war after Iran's June 1 suspension of talks.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "A US Apache helicopter was downed in the Strait of Hormuz (~June 9, 2026; both pilots rescued uninjured); Washington blamed Tehran and used it as the predicate for renewed strikes. (Al Jazeera; NBC News, June 10, 2026.)",
        "The US struck Iran on two consecutive nights: June 9 (Qeshm Island; ports of Sirik, Jask, Bandar Abbas) and June 10 (a ~4-hour campaign from ~22:15 GMT using ~49 Tomahawk missiles against surveillance/communications/air-defense sites at Karaj and Hormuz ports, some ~40 miles from Tehran). Trump: 'We were really close to a deal. But they keep tapping us along.' (Al Jazeera; CNBC; NBC News, June 10, 2026.)",
        "Iran's IRGC declared the Strait of Hormuz 'closed until further notice' to all ships and barraged US bases in Bahrain (Sheikh Isa), Kuwait (Ali Al Salem + Ahmad Al-Jaber) and Jordan (Al-Azraq, 12 ballistic missiles), claiming via Tasnim 'eighteen important targets' destroyed; two oil tankers reportedly hit; Bahrain interceptions injured an 11-year-old girl. (Al Jazeera; NBC News, June 10-11, 2026.)",
        "On June 11 Trump cancelled the threatened 'very hard tonight' strikes and announced 'a great settlement of the war with Iran' subject to document finalization, a signing 'maybe in Europe' within days; Iran did not confirm any deal and Netanyahu said Israel is not a party. (NPR; NBC News; Times of Israel, June 11, 2026.)",
        "On June 8, 2026 Israel struck Iranian territory for the first time since the April 8 ceasefire — hitting Tehran, Isfahan, Karaj and Tabriz with air-launched ballistic missiles, plus a petrochemical plant at Mahshahr (Khuzestan) and truck-based SAM launchers — in response to Iran's June 7 barrage. Iran fired ~30 ballistic missiles in total since Sunday night, its IRGC branding the campaign 'Operation Nasr' ('Victory') and claiming two Israeli military bases; most were intercepted, debris reached Jordan and the West Bank, and no casualties were confirmed from the exchange itself. (Times of Israel; Al Jazeera; ABC News, June 8, 2026.)",
        "On June 8 Trump blocked a larger Israeli strike that had been prepared for late that afternoon ('I made Netanyahu stop,' 'I call the shots,' 'we are close to doing something good in terms of a deal'); by June 8-9 both sides halted — Iran's military announcing 'the cessation of armed forces operations is hereby announced' (with a warning it would resume if Israel continues operations in southern Lebanon), Israel halting its attacks without formally acknowledging a ceasefire, and Trump posting that both are 'looking to do an immediate CEASEFIRE.' Brent crossed ~$98 intraday then eased to ~$94. (MS NOW; Arab News; CNBC, June 8, 2026.)",
        "On June 7, 2026 Israel struck Beirut's southern suburbs (Dahieh) without warning — despite a US request to stand down — killing 2 and wounding ~20 (incl. 4 women, 4 children), calling it retaliation for Hezbollah fire on northern Israel. Iran responded with its FIRST direct missile barrage at Israel since the April 8 ceasefire, actualizing FM Araghchi's June 4 'full-scale resumption' warning; Iron Dome/Arrow intercepted ('defense not hermetic'), with divergent casualty reporting (IDF: no immediate casualties; NPR: a cluster-warhead missile killed 2 in Ramat Gan and debris damaged the Old City of Jerusalem). The IRGC warned any repeat would target 'all American and Zionist targets throughout the region.' (NPR; PBS NewsHour; Axios; CSMonitor, June 7, 2026.)",
        "On June 7-8, 2026 Iran's Foreign Ministry called the June 6 US strikes on radar sites at Goruk and Qeshm Island 'a clear violation of the 8 April ceasefire and an act of military aggression,' explicitly drawing the US into the frame; Trump pushed de-escalation, distancing the US from the Beirut strike ('not coordinated... I'm not happy') and telling Iran 'you've shot your missiles, that's enough... get back to the table and make a deal.' Oil held near ~$94-97 Brent and did not spike. (CBS News live updates; Axios; Al Jazeera, June 7-8, 2026.)",
        "On June 6, 2026 CENTCOM said US forces shot down four Iranian one-way attack drones launched toward the Strait of Hormuz and intercepted six of seven Iranian ballistic missiles fired at Kuwait and Bahrain (the seventh failed to reach target); Bahrain's Defense Ministry intercepted three missiles and a number of drones — the first direct targeting of Bahrain. US forces then struck Iranian coastal radar sites at Goruk and on Qeshm Island 'to defend against further attacks.' Oil EASED to ~$94.81 rather than spiking. (CENTCOM; Fox News; RFE/RL, June 6, 2026.)",
        "Overnight June 2-3 the US struck two Iranian command-and-control sites near Hormuz, reported at Goruk and Qeshm Island, after the downing of a US MQ-1 Predator drone (The National; CBS News, June 3, 2026).",
        "On June 2 CENTCOM said a Hellfire missile from a US fighter disabled an oil tanker heading for Kharg Island after it 'ignored repeated warnings' under the blockade (Wikipedia, '2026 Strait of Hormuz campaign'; CNBC, June 2, 2026).",
        "Iran said the US had attacked 'Kharg and Abu Musa' islands and an Iranian vessel overnight; the IRGC said it struck a US airbase in retaliation, claiming 'all predetermined targets were destroyed' (The National; NBC News, June 3, 2026).",
        "At dawn June 3 Iran fired 13 ballistic missiles and 17 drones at Gulf targets; Kuwait intercepted them but falling debris damaged Kuwait International Airport, killing 1 and wounding 63 (NBC News; CBS News; Travel And Tour World, June 3, 2026).",
        "Trump and Rubio said the Iran talks remained ongoing (CNBC, June 2); VP Vance said June 4 the US and Iran were 'very close' but 'not there yet' on the MOU and still 'going back and forth on the nuclear stuff' (The Hill, June 4, 2026).",
        "Ghalibaf said Iran obtains 'concessions not through talks, but through missiles,' and that 'negotiations are only for making them understand those concessions' (CBS News; ABC7, June 3-4, 2026).",
        "The House passed a war-powers resolution 215-208 on June 3 directing Trump to end hostilities with Iran (four Republicans crossing over) — a counter-escalatory domestic signal (CNN; NPR, June 3, 2026).",
        "Oil rose more than 3% on the flare-up into the high-$90s/~$100 (Fortune; The National, June 3, 2026)."
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "The bargaining-pressure reading holds the strikes are coercive signaling within a recoverable negotiation — consistent with Trump/Rubio saying talks continue, Vance's 'very close' framing, Ghalibaf's explicit 'concessions through missiles' logic, and oil rising only ~3% rather than spiking to wartime peaks. The return-to-war reading holds that Iran formally suspended talks June 1, is now striking Gulf states and US bases and threatening a second chokepoint (Bab al-Mandeb), and that the tit-for-tat strikes mark the start of a durable breakdown rather than a sequencing fight.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "Bargaining-pressure reading: the Trump administration (Trump, Rubio, Vance); markets (oil up only ~3%).",
        "Return-to-war reading: analysts noting Iran's June 1 suspension, the strikes on Kuwait and a claimed US airbase, and the Bab al-Mandeb threat; Iranian officials (Ghalibaf, Qa'ani) framing missiles and chokepoints as the operative levers."
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "The MOU publicly signed by both Trump and Mojtaba Khamenei, with the strikes treated as a closed episode (resolves toward bargaining-pressure).",
        "A durable, visible resumption of large-scale kinetic exchange and a formal end to mediation (resolves toward return-to-war).",
        "A renewed, holding ceasefire and resumption of the mediator-routed text exchange (validates the leverage reading).",
        "A US ground-deployment order, third-ARG order, new MEU order, BCT-scale Guard activation, or Selective Service language (would indicate escalation beyond the named air/strike register — also bears on the ground-invasion question).",
        "Activation (or visible non-activation) of the threatened Bab al-Mandeb second front."
      ],
      "current_assessment": "Widened and sharpened July 9-10: Iran's first multi-Gulf-state retaliation since June 28-29 (Kuwait, Qatar, Bahrain, Jordan) and Trump's renewed Kharg-takeover threat push the escalatory reading to its broadest point — while the mostly-successful intercepts, the deliberate sparing of Kharg's oil infrastructure, the absence of any US ground move or formal MOU cancellation, and the July 10 Pakistan/Qatar re-engagement (with Iran reportedly seeking a new deal) push the bargaining/deterrence reading just as hard. The exchange widened horizontally (more states, ~90 targets) rather than climbing to the ground rung. Whether this is a wider-but-contained coercive round or the leading edge of a return to open regional war cannot be resolved from open sources as of July 10; it turns on whether the US reinstates the blockade or lands on Kharg, whether Iran escalates materially, and whether formal talks reconvene.",
      "first_listed": "2026-06-04"
    },
    {
      "id": "iran-suspends-us-talks-lebanon-june-2026",
      "title": "Iran's June 1 suspension of US talks over Israel's Lebanon offensive — a recoverable bargaining pause, or the start of a collapse of the 60-day MOU?",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "Contested — June 13-14: The endgame firmed to a scheduled date: Trump said the deal was 'scheduled to get signed tomorrow [Sun June 14]' with Hormuz to reopen 'OPEN TO ALL' immediately after, the signing set for Geneva with VP Vance attending and C-17s already moving equipment to Europe — the strongest pro-settlement posture of the war. But it stayed unsigned and unratified by Tehran: a senior US official put confidence below '100%,' Iran's Foreign Ministry said it had 'not yet reached a final decision,' Khamenei's approval was unconfirmed, and Trump's new 'no money will exchange hands' line cut against Iran's stated $24B precondition — after ~39 prior 'imminent' claims. The recoverable-endgame reading is at its strongest; the collapse/slip reading survives on the unsigned, unratified text. Unresolvable until a text is publicly signed and holds. || Contested — June 12-13: Mediators reported the deal had firmed from 'announced settlement' to a 'final, agreed upon text' (Pakistan's PM Sharif: peace 'has never been this close'), with Iran in 'final' deliberations, Khamenei reportedly 'comfortable,' and a signing floated 'maybe over the weekend in Europe' (VP Vance attending) — the strongest pro-settlement signal yet. But it stayed unsigned and openly contested: Trump denied Iran's account of the terms ('NOTHING to do' / 'dishonorable people'), Iran's Mehr conditioned final talks on a prior $12B release and a blockade end, Israel reiterated it is not a party, and overnight Iranian drones fired on Hormuz shipping. The recoverable-endgame reading strengthened; the collapse/slip reading survives on the unsigned text and the sequencing dispute. Unresolvable until a text is publicly signed. || Contested — June 11-12: After the sharpest kinetic flare of the war, Trump announced 'a great settlement of the war with Iran' to be signed 'maybe in Europe' within days, citing approval at Iran's 'highest level' and support from Saudi Arabia, Qatar, Egypt, Turkey and Pakistan — but Iran did not confirm any deal and Netanyahu said Israel is not a party. The recoverable-pause reading is strengthened by the settlement announcement; the collapse reading survives on the still-unsigned, Iran-unconfirmed text and continued Israeli Lebanon strikes (~3,696 killed since Mar 2). Unresolvable until a text is publicly signed. || Contested — June 10-11: The mutual halt held into a third day and Iran signaled it has 'no problem' continuing talks if the US is 'sincere,' yet the 60-day MOU is still unsigned over the $24B frozen-assets and HEU questions and Trump's 'two to three days' timeline has run a third day without a signature, while Israel's intensifying Lebanon campaign (firmer ~17-killed June 9 toll; Tyre Christian-quarter evacuation) keeps Iran's resumption tripwire live. Unresolvable until the MOU is publicly signed or the talks visibly collapse.",
      "synopsis": "On June 1, 2026 Iran suspended its mediator-routed talks with the United States over the unsigned 60-day MOU, conditioning resumption on Israel halting its southern-Lebanon offensive. Trump publicly minimized the rupture (telling CNBC he 'didn't care' if talks were over and they had 'gotten boring'), but insisted a deal was reachable 'over the next week,' claimed a 'very productive call' with Netanyahu and — via intermediaries — Hezbollah to pause attacks, and said there would be 'no troops going to Beirut.' The question is whether the suspension is tactical leverage to force a Lebanon ceasefire and the $12B frozen-assets release before a signature both sides still expect, or the first concrete step toward a genuine breakdown of the MOU and a slide back to open hostilities.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "June 7 — Israel struck Beirut's southern suburbs, crossing the line Araghchi (June 4) had named as a 'full-scale resumption' trigger; Iran responded with its first direct missile barrage at Israel since the April 8 ceasefire. Despite this, the 60-day MOU was neither signed nor formally repudiated, and the talks remained deadlocked over Iran's $24B frozen-assets demand. Trump pushed de-escalation ('you've shot your missiles, that's enough... get back to the table'); Grossi reiterated that IAEA inspector access remains limited but still saw possible movement toward a preliminary framework; mediation continued via Pakistan, Qatar, Turkey and Egypt. (NPR; PBS NewsHour; CBS News; Iran International, June 7-8, 2026.)",
        "June 5 — Mohsen Rezaei, military adviser to Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei, told CNN in Tehran 'The negotiations are at a deadlock and Trump must break this deadlock' and 'the ball is in Trump's court,' and said an interim deal now hinges on the US releasing $24 billion in frozen Iranian assets ($12B on signing + $12B later) as 'a test of trust … America must pass'; he warned Iran would 'drag the war' beyond the Gulf and 'attack these other American bases' if fighting resumed (CNN; Newsmax; The Express Tribune, June 5, 2026).",
        "June 5 — Trump disclosed he had considered but rejected a plan to send US troops into Iran to collect enriched-uranium 'nuclear dust,' calling the material 'entombed' with 'no reason to' proceed; IAEA's Grossi said the track may be inching toward a preliminary nuclear framework, and the US sanctioned Iranian oil/LPG-smuggling networks (ABC News live updates; RFE/RL, June 5, 2026).",
        "June 4 — The US State Department announced, after the June 2-3 Washington trilateral talks, that Israel and Lebanon had agreed a conditional ceasefire framework: 'contingent on a complete cessation' of Hezbollah fire and the evacuation of all Hezbollah operatives south of the Litani, with 'pilot' security zones under exclusive Lebanese Armed Forces control (Al Jazeera; Washington Post, June 4, 2026).",
        "June 4 — Hezbollah Secretary-General Naim Kassem rejected the framework on Al-Manar TV as 'absurd, humiliating and insulting,' said leaving the south under fire would mean 'surrender, defeat,' and demanded a full Israeli withdrawal; Israel and Hezbollah traded fresh strikes (NPR; NBC News; TIME, June 4, 2026).",
        "June 4 — Lebanese President Joseph Aoun called the agreement the 'last chance' for a comprehensive truce (Al Jazeera, June 4, 2026).",
        "June 4 — Iranian FM Araghchi said 'no tangible progress' had been made in the talks and warned that any Israeli attack on Beirut would trigger a 'full-scale resumption' of the US-Iran conflict (CBS News, June 4, 2026).",
        "June 4 — VP Vance said a deal was 'very close' but 'not there yet' ('hard to say... if the president's going to sign'); Trump said Iran had 'agreed not to pursue a nuclear weapon' and floated a possible meeting with Mojtaba Khamenei (The Hill; Fortune, June 4, 2026).",
        "Iran's negotiating team announced June 1 it was halting communications and the mediator-routed text exchange with the US, citing 'the continuation of the Zionist regime's crimes in Lebanon' and conditioning resumption on Israel stopping its Lebanon offensive (NPR; India TV News, June 1, 2026).",
        "Trump told CNBC he 'did not care' if the negotiations were over and that the process 'started to get very boring,' urged the public to 'just sit back and relax,' and insisted a deal was reachable 'over the next week' (CNBC; The New Daily, June 1-2, 2026).",
        "Trump said he spoke to Netanyahu and, via intermediaries, to Hezbollah to pause attacks and re-enable US-Iran discussions, claimed a 'very productive call,' and stated there would be 'no troops going to Beirut'; CNN reported a heated, expletive-laden Trump-Netanyahu exchange (CNN, June 1, 2026).",
        "Netanyahu said Israel would keep striking southern Lebanon 'as planned'; Defense Minister Katz denied any Lebanon ceasefire (India TV News; GlobalSecurity.org, June 1-2, 2026).",
        "Iran's chief negotiator / Parliament Speaker Ghalibaf warned Tehran could move beyond suspending talks to 'direct confrontation' if Israel's Lebanon campaign continued, and warned Lebanese Speaker Berri that Iran might retaliate.",
        "The IRGC's June 1 threat against Hormuz shipping stood; Iranian state media said Tehran was preparing to fully close the Strait; Lloyd's List assessed the waterway closed since May 4.",
        "Oil jumped about 5% on June 1 (intraday near 7%) on the suspension, then eased toward ~$95/bbl as Trump signaled the deal was 'back on track.'"
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "The tactical-leverage reading holds the suspension is a temporary, recoverable pause meant to extract an Israeli Lebanon ceasefire and the $12B frozen-assets release before a signature both sides still expect — consistent with Trump's 'back on track' / 'reachable over the next week' framing and with oil easing after its initial spike. The genuine-breakdown reading holds that the MOU, 'imminent' for over a week without a signature, is now hostage to a Lebanon ceasefire Israel publicly refuses, and that Iranian escalation signals (Ghalibaf's 'direct confrontation,' the IRGC's full-closure threat) point to a slide back toward open hostilities.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "Tactical-leverage reading: the Trump administration; a regional source cited by CNN ('back on track'); markets (oil easing after the spike).",
        "Genuine-breakdown reading: analysts noting the deal has been 'imminent' for over a week without a signature and now depends on an Israeli Lebanon ceasefire; Iranian officials (Ghalibaf) signaling escalation."
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "The MOU publicly signed by both Trump and Mojtaba Khamenei (resolves toward recoverable-pause).",
        "A durable, visible collapse of the talks and/or resumption of large-scale kinetic exchange (resolves toward breakdown).",
        "An Israeli Lebanon ceasefire that visibly unblocks the US-Iran track (validates the leverage reading).",
        "Iranian movement on HEU disposition or the $12B mechanism that shows the suspension was a sequencing fight, not a deal-killer (resolves).",
        "A US ground-deployment order, third-ARG order, BCT-scale Guard activation, or Selective Service language (would indicate the deal-failure branch has moved beyond the named air/strike register — would also bear on the ground-invasion question)."
      ],
      "current_assessment": "As of June 13-14 the recoverable-pause reading is at its strongest — Trump set a Sunday Geneva signing with Vance attending and Hormuz to reopen on signing — but the collapse/slip reading is not foreclosed: the text is unsigned, US confidence is sub-'100%,' Iran has 'not yet reached a final decision,' Khamenei's approval is unconfirmed, and the 'no money' vs. $24B dispute is unresolved, all after ~39 prior 'imminent' claims. Unresolvable from open sources until a deal is publicly signed and holds, or the talks visibly collapse.",
      "first_listed": "2026-06-03"
    },
    {
      "id": "nyt-intense-preparations-iran-resumption-may-2026",
      "title": "NYT 'intense preparations' report (May 15) — genuine US/Israel pre-execution preparation for renewed Iran hostilities, or escalation-leverage signaling during a stalled negotiation?",
      "status": "contested_unresolved",
      "status_label": "Contested — June 2-3: Iran SUSPENDED its mediator-routed US talks on June 1 over Israel's Lebanon offensive, raising the odds the unsigned 60-day MOU collapses rather than closes; Trump insists a deal is reachable 'over the next week,' explicitly rules out 'troops going to Beirut,' and names no ground option, while Ghalibaf warns of 'direct confrontation' and the IRGC threatens to fully close the Strait. Deescalatory at the ground rung (no new troop movements; USS Boxer ARG still INDOPACOM; Ford home; no third ARG; no draft); the deal-failure alternative stays an air/strike package, not a ground order; see also [iran-suspends-us-talks-lebanon-june-2026]. || Contested — June 1-2: At the 'turn of the week' the negotiated 60-day MOU remains UNSIGNED. Trump's May 31 requested edits (HEU hand-over specifics/timing + Hormuz-reopening wording) carried into the new week without a signature, and the disputes widened: Iranian sources say the draft contains 'no such clause' on a toll-free Strait and 'no reference to Iran dismantling or destroying its nuclear materials,' while Iranian state media reframed the immediate payment of $12B of Iran's frozen assets as 'the most important part of the agreement' and a precondition for further talks; the two sides exchanged renewed low-grade fire over the weekend. Deescalatory at the ground rung (no new troop movements; USS Boxer ARG still INDOPACOM; Ford home; no third ARG; no draft); the deal-failure alternative stays an air/strike package, not a ground order; the leverage-signaling / deal-close reading holds without resolving — the MOU is still unsigned by both leaders || Contested — May 30-31: A SECOND consecutive day passes without Trump's 'final determination' on the 60-day MOU; White House says Trump 'will only make a deal that is good for America and satisfies his redlines'; a possible Sunday (May 31) announcement is floated but NOT delivered; Lara Trump hopes for a wrap-up 'in the next couple hours.' Iran's Mohsen Rezaei publicly accuses Trump of stalling with 'excessive demands' and 'betraying diplomacy' ('by continuing the naval blockade and making excessive demands in negotiations, he has once again proven that he is not inclined toward negotiation and is pursuing other objectives'). At the Shangri-La Dialogue, Hegseth says the US is 'more than capable' of resuming strikes if talks fail ('our stockpiles are more than suited for that both there and around the globe') and the blockade is 'very much still in place'; CENTCOM tally rises to ~115-116 vessels redirected + 5 disabled (M/V Lian Star). Oil markets closed for the weekend after capping a ~19% monthly drop (worst since March 2020). Deescalatory at the ground rung (no new troop movements; USS Boxer ARG still INDOPACOM; Ford home; no third ARG; no draft); the deal-failure alternative is explicitly framed as an air/strike package, not a ground order; the leverage-signaling / deal-close reading holds without resolving — the MOU remains unsigned by both leaders || Contested — May 29-30: Trump's promised 'final determination' Situation Room meeting (Fri May 29, ~2 hours) ENDS WITH NO DECISION on the 60-day MOU; senior admin official (NYT) says the agreement is 'still close' but needs further debate including the unfreezing of Iranian funds; Trump (Truth Social) restates maximalist demands (no nuclear weapon; Hormuz 'immediately open, no tolls'; remove mines); Iran Fars: post 'contradicts the provisions of the agreement's text'; Tasnim: 'final text... not yet completed or approved'; Vance: signing 'TBD,' 'a couple of language points'; Mojtaba Khamenei out of public view, approval unclear. Low-grade kinetic skirmishing persists in the AIR/MARITIME register (US drone shoot-downs + Bandar Abbas ground-control-station strike; Iran's CENTCOM-denied aircraft-downing claim near Bushehr); oil keeps sliding (Brent ~$92.05 / WTI ~$87.36, Brent -19% on the month — worst since March 2020). Deescalatory at the ground rung (no new troop movements; USS Boxer ARG still INDOPACOM; Ford home; no third ARG; no draft); the leverage-signaling / deal-close reading holds without resolving — the MOU is still unsigned by both leaders || PRIOR Contested — May 28-29: deal-close track produces a negotiator-level 60-day MOU (Axios, May 28) but the deal is DOUBLE-PENDING — Trump has not signed ('a couple of days to think about it'), Iran has not confirmed acceptance, and Mojtaba Khamenei's approval is also outstanding; ABC: 'negotiators believe they have a deal, but leaders haven't signed off.' MOU terms route Hormuz through a NEGOTIATED reopening ('unrestricted' shipping / no tolls / Iran clears mines in 30 days) rather than a ground seizure. Framing sits against the May 27 White House dismissal of an Iranian-state-media MOU leak as a 'complete fabrication' and Trump's 'no sanctions relief for HEU' line; oil partly rebounded ~2% (Brent ~$96.29 / WTI >$90). Deescalatory at the ground rung (no new troop movements; USS Boxer ARG still INDOPACOM; USS Gerald R. Ford home; no third ARG; no draft); the leverage-signaling / deal-close reading gains weight without resolving || PRIOR Contested — May 26-27: first kinetic US-Iran exchange of the Project Freedom pause punctures but does not break the ceasefire — CENTCOM 'self-defense strikes' early Tue May 26 on 2 IRGC mine-laying boats in Hormuz + SAM site near Bandar Abbas (4 killed per Iranian state TV) in response to '24 hours of missile, drone and small boat launches'; CENTCOM ceasefire 'remains in place'; IRGC 'flagrant violation' / 'legitimate and certain' right of response; Shekarchi 'far more severe' / 'beyond the region'; Mojtaba Khamenei 'US bases no longer safe'; Trump 'just a love tap'; Iranian delegation (Araghchi + Qalibaf + Hemmati) continues Doha track 'generally positive' on ~$24B frozen assets + nuclear stockpile; Fox News / senior US officials: deal '95% there'; Trump May 27 'close to finalizing' / 'strong inspections'; Araghchi May 27 unsure deal imminent; Shamkhani 'fantasy'; Austria DSN: Iran advanced nuclear weapons program (rejected); Iran internet blackout partially lifted after 87 days; Brent +3%+ to $99.58 / WTI -3% to $93.89 (mixed). Deescalatory at the ground rung (US response was AIR/MARITIME 'self-defense' package — not a ground order; no new troop movements; USS Boxer ARG still INDOPACOM; USS Gerald R. Ford home; no third ARG; no draft) with retaliation risk RE-ELEVATED and the deal-track NARROWED but not closed",
      "synopsis": "Whether the New York Times report (May 15, amplified May 16-17 by The Times of Israel + The Jerusalem Post) that the US and Israel are in 'intense preparations' for a potential resumption of hostilities — described by two anonymous Middle East officials as the most significant since the April ceasefire, with US commandos to retrieve Iranian nuclear material, troops to target Kharg Island, and increased bombing as options under consideration on a 'next week' timeline — reflects (a) genuine pre-execution preparation for a ground-inclusive resumption, or (b) escalation-leverage signaling timed to pressure Tehran in a stalled negotiation.",
      "documented_facts": [
        "May 29-30 — TRUMP 'FINAL DETERMINATION' SITUATION ROOM MEETING ENDS WITH NO DECISION: Trump convened a ~2-hour White House Situation Room meeting Fri May 29 to make a 'final determination' on the tentative 60-day MOU and ended it without announcing one; a senior administration official told the NYT the agreement was 'still close' but required further debate over issues including the unfreezing of Iranian funds. Trump (Truth Social) restated demands that Iran 'must agree' to never have a nuclear weapon and that the Strait of Hormuz 'must be immediately open, no tolls, for unrestricted shipping traffic' with remaining mines removed. Iran's Fars said the post 'raised issues that contradict the provisions of the agreement's text'; the IRGC-linked Tasnim said the 'final text of the agreement has not yet been completed or approved,' describing the MOU as 'in the final stages of approval in Iran' with 'no final decision yet.' VP Vance said it was 'TBD' whether Trump would sign and that the sides were negotiating 'a couple of language points'; Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei has remained out of public view since Feb 28 and his approval is unclear. Low-grade kinetic skirmishing continued in the AIR/MARITIME register: US forces downed Iranian one-way-attack drones near Hormuz and struck an Iranian ground-control station at Bandar Abbas (further launch prevented); Iran's claim (Jam/Bushehr governor Tangestani) that a US aircraft was downed near Bushehr was flatly denied by CENTCOM ('No U.S. aircraft were shot down. All U.S. air assets are accounted for'); strikes were reported around Qeshm/Abu Musa/Bandar Abbas/Lavan refinery/Asaluyeh. Oil fell again: Brent ~$92.05 (-1.77%) / WTI ~$87.36 (-1.73%) May 29 close, Brent -19% on the month (worst since March 2020). US response register stays AIR/MARITIME, NOT ground: no new troop movements; USS Boxer ARG still INDOPACOM; USS Gerald R. Ford home at Norfolk; USS Abraham Lincoln + USS George H.W. Bush CSGs + USS Tripoli ARG in the Arabian Sea; no third ARG; no BCT-scale Guard activation; no Selective Service/draft action; Murkowski draft AUMF would BAN ground troops. (CNN; CNBC; Axios; Times of Israel; Euronews; Jerusalem Post; Bloomberg; Haaretz; OANN; CENTCOM; NYT, May 29, 2026)",
        "May 26-27 — FIRST KINETIC US-IRAN EXCHANGE OF PROJECT FREEDOM PAUSE: CENTCOM announced 'self-defense strikes' early Tuesday May 26 on two IRGC mine-laying boats in the Strait of Hormuz and a surface-to-air missile site near Bandar Abbas reportedly targeting US aircraft, in response to '24 hours of missile, drone and small boat launches' by the IRGC; Iranian state TV / Fars reported four killed in Bandar Abbas; CENTCOM said the strikes were defensive and that the ceasefire 'remains in place.' The IRGC called the strikes a 'flagrant violation' and asserted a 'legitimate and certain' right of reciprocal response; army spokesperson Shekarchi (Fars) warned any new attack would generate a 'far more severe' response 'beyond the region'; Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei warned US bases in the region are 'no longer safe.' Trump downplayed the strikes as 'just a love tap' and insisted the ceasefire was intact. Iranian delegation (Araghchi + Qalibaf + Hemmati) arrived in Doha on Monday May 25 and met Qatari mediators 'generally positively' on Sheikh Tamim's instructions, addressing ~$24B in frozen Iranian assets + nuclear stockpile. Fox News (citing senior US officials, May 26): deal '95 percent there'; Trump (May 27): 'close to finalizing' agreement involving 'strong inspections'; US officials: Mojtaba Khamenei 'endorsed the broad template of the deal,' though formal approval could take 'several additional days.' Iran FM Araghchi (May 27): unsure deal is imminent; FM spox Baghaei: 'to say this means an agreement is about to be signed — no one can make such a claim'; Khamenei adviser Shamkhani: Trump nuclear demands 'fantasy.' Austria DSN (May 26): Iran pursuing advanced nuclear weapons program with long-range BMs; Iran rejected as 'false and baseless.' Iran's internet blackout partially lifted after 87 days. Oil split: Brent +3%+ to ~$99.58/bbl on retaliation vow; WTI -3% to ~$93.89/bbl. US response register stays AIR/MARITIME, NOT ground: no new troop movements; USS Boxer ARG still INDOPACOM per TWZ/USNI; USS Gerald R. Ford home at Norfolk; USS Abraham Lincoln + USS George H.W. Bush CSGs + USS Tripoli ARG in Arabian Sea; no third ARG; no BCT-scale Guard activation; no Selective Service/draft language; Murkowski draft AUMF would BAN ground troops. (CENTCOM; CBS News; CNBC; CNN; Al Jazeera; NBC News; The Aviationist; Xinhua; IBTimes UK; The National (UAE); PressTV; ABC News; Wikipedia '2025-2026 Iran-United States negotiations'; Wikipedia '2026 Iran war ceasefire'; Wikipedia '2026 Lebanon war,' May 25-27, 2026)",
        "May 25-26 — Trump (Truth Social, Mon May 25) tied any final Iran deal to a WIDENED Abraham Accords, 'mandatorily requesting' that all countries sign and naming Egypt/Jordan/Pakistan/Qatar/Saudi Arabia to establish full diplomatic ties with Israel; said negotiations were 'proceeding nicely' but gave no sign a deal was imminent. The emerging 60-day MOU drew sharp pro-Israel criticism (Cruz 'a disastrous mistake'; Wicker '60-day ceasefire a disaster'; Pompeo 'not remotely America First' / likened to 2015 JCPOA) even as Graham reversed from 'nightmare' to 'simply brilliant' and Levin praised the normalization as 'a truly massive accomplishment.' Rubio said a deal 'could materialize today' while keeping 'alternatives' open if diplomacy fails; Iran's FM spokesman said the sides had 'reached a conclusion on a large portion of the issues' but that 'an agreement is not imminent,' accusing Washington of shifting positions. Pakistan's Munir + PM met Chinese leaders in Beijing on the Iran file. NPR reported 'no major military posture changes' for May 25; USS Boxer ARG still INDOPACOM; USS Gerald R. Ford home; no third ARG; no BCT-scale Guard activation; no Selective Service/draft language; Murkowski draft AUMF would BAN ground troops. (CNBC; NPR; Washington Post; Al Jazeera; CBS News live updates, May 25, 2026)",
        "May 24-25 — DECISION SUNDAY: one day after the 'largely negotiated' announcement, Trump (Truth Social, Sun May 24) told his representatives 'not to rush into a deal' with Iran ('both sides must take their time and get it right'), framed the talks as 'orderly and constructive' / 'time is on our side,' and declared the US naval blockade would 'remain in full force and effect until an agreement is reached, certified, and signed.' White House officials said no deal would be finalized Sunday but expected it 'signed within days' pending Khamenei approval (senior US official: 'we are in a very good place — but there are ways in which the deal can be undermined'). Axios reported the emerging deal: a 60-day MOU; Hormuz reopened without tolls + Iranian mine-clearing; US lifts the port blockade + sanctions waivers ('relief for performance'); US forces remain 60 days and withdraw only on a final deal; Lebanon war must end. Iran Tasnim: 'one or two clauses' persist; no first-step asset release = 'no agreement'; IRGC-linked outlets: Hormuz to 'remain under Iranian supervision.' (Axios; CNN; Reuters via Detroit News/Star-Advertiser; Israel Hayom)",
        "May 23-24 — DECISION WEEKEND: Trump said 'an Agreement has been largely negotiated' and a framework would be 'announced shortly' (incl. Hormuz reopening), but framed the odds a 'solid 50/50' between a 'good' deal and hitting Iran 'harder than they have ever been hit,' saying he would decide by Sunday after a Saturday Witkoff/Kushner/Vance meeting + a Gulf-leaders call (Axios; CNBC; ABC News; PBS). Mediators converged on a 60-day ceasefire-extension framework / 14-clause MOU (Iran FM spox Baqaei) with the nuclear file deferred to a separate ~30-60-day track; gradual Hormuz reopening without tolls; Iran to DISCUSS diluting/transferring its ~440 kg HEU; limited sanctions relief + phased asset release (The Week; Ynet; Jerusalem Post; House of Commons Library CBP-10637). Iran Fars dismissed the announcement as 'incomplete and inconsistent with reality' (Hormuz to 'remain under Iranian management'). CENTCOM milestone (May 23): 100+ commercial vessels redirected since Apr 13 (+4 disabled, 26 humanitarian), >15,000 US troops enforcing the blockade. NO new ground signals: USS Boxer ARG still INDOPACOM; USS Gerald R. Ford home; no third ARG; no BCT-scale Guard activation; no Selective Service/draft language; Murkowski draft AUMF would BAN ground troops.",
        "May 21-22: Trump relaxed the 'two or three days' deadline into 'I'm in no hurry' / willing to wait 'a few days' to 'get the right answers' (CNBC, NBC News); Rubio: 'president's preference is to do a good deal'; Vance reiterated 'locked and loaded' (Fox News); Iran FM spox Baghaei said Tehran is 'reviewing' the US views and gaps were 'reduced' (NBC News); Pakistan Army Chief Asim Munir traveled to Tehran (mediation escalation, Fox News/CNBC); oil fell >6% intraday (Brent ~$104.64 / WTI ~$97.66, Fox News/Fortune); Treasury sanctioned nine Hezbollah-linked individuals; Fox poll found 60% of US voters oppose further military action. No ground troops staged; USS Boxer ARG still INDOPACOM; USS Gerald R. Ford home at Norfolk; no third ARG; no Selective Service/draft language.",
        "May 18 — Trump (Truth Social) called off the strike on Iran that had been scheduled for Tuesday May 19, after the leaders of Qatar, Saudi Arabia and the UAE asked him to 'hold off,' citing 'serious negotiations are now taking place' (CNN, CBS, ABC, Euronews, Newsweek, CNBC, AP)",
        "May 18 — Trump simultaneously instructed the Pentagon to 'be prepared to go forward with a full, large scale assault of Iran, on a moment's notice, in the event that an acceptable Deal is not reached' (CNN Politics)",
        "May 18-19 — the Tuesday May 19 Situation Room/NSC meeting (Cooper to brief the option-set) remained on the calendar; weekend strategy sessions at Trump's Virginia golf club involved VP Vance, SecState Rubio, CIA Dir Ratcliffe, Envoy Witkoff",
        "May 18 — HEU/enrichment impasse unresolved: WH Anna Kelly 'Iran must renounce their nuclear ambitions for good'; Iran FM Baghaei 'rights are not something we are to negotiate or compromise over,' no nuclear discussions yet, terms conveyed via Pakistani intermediaries",
        "May 18-19 — still no observed deployment change: no third ARG order, no flank-speed expedite, no BCT-scale Guard activation, no Selective Service/draft language; USS Gerald R. Ford continues departing theatre; the postponed/threatened operation framed as a 'full, large scale assault' (air/strike-package register), not a ground-deployment order",
        "May 15 — NYT: US and Israel are intensifying preparations for potential attacks on Iran; operations possibly starting 'as soon as this week' / 'as early as next week'",
        "Two anonymous Middle East officials told the NYT this is the most significant set of preparations since the Pakistani-brokered April ceasefire",
        "Options reported under consideration: deploying US commandos to retrieve nuclear material (risky, 'extensive support,' 'could lead to casualties from engaging Iranian forces'); troops targeting Kharg Island; increased bombing of Iranian military + infrastructure sites",
        "Defense Secretary Hegseth: 'We have a plan to escalate, if necessary. We have a plan to retrograde, if necessary. We have a plan to shift assets'",
        "Trump (Fox News): Iran can make a deal or 'get annihilated'; Iranian leadership potentially 'crazy'; prefers to 'get' the enriched uranium rather than leave it 'entombed' in Iranian rubble",
        "Iran Parliament National Security Committee spokesperson threatened to boost enrichment to 90% if attacks resume",
        "May 16 — Trump administration reportedly requested the UAE seize Kharg Island; US officials reviewing operational concepts for occupying/blockading Kharg",
        "No observed deployment change: no third ARG order, no flank-speed expedite, no BCT-scale National Guard activation, no Selective Service language; USS Gerald R. Ford continues departing theatre",
        "Murkowski-led GOP AUMF (in drafting) would BAN US ground troops + set a time limit; Senate war-powers vote 49-50 May 13",
        "War on the Rocks ('The Folly of Seizing Kharg Island', Apr 2026) and US officials previously flagged high casualty risk of a Kharg ground operation"
      ],
      "alternate_reading": "One reading (some hawkish and some antiwar commentary; implicit in Iran-state framing) treats the report — most-significant-since-ceasefire preparations, named commando-retrieval + Kharg troop-targeting options, 'next week' timeline, Hegseth's plan confirmation, Trump's 'deal or get annihilated' — as genuine pre-execution preparation for a ground-inclusive resumption. The opposite reading (mainstream defense-press caution, this tracker) treats it as escalation-leverage signaling timed to a stalled negotiation: anonymous-official sourced, options 'under consideration' not orders, no observed deployment, Hegseth's language generic contingency-readiness, carrier presence reducing. Both rest on the same authentic NYT reporting.",
      "public_assertion_by": [
        "New York Times (reporting the preparations)",
        "US/Israeli anonymous Middle East officials (sourced in NYT)",
        "Defense Secretary Hegseth",
        "The Times of Israel / The Jerusalem Post (amplifying)",
        "Some hawkish US commentary (genuine pre-execution reading)",
        "Some antiwar commentary (engineered-pretext reading)",
        "Iran state media (genuine-aggression reading)"
      ],
      "what_would_resolve": [
        "An executed commando/Kharg operation or strike order within the reported window (validates pre-execution reading)",
        "A third ARG order / flank-speed expedite / BCT-scale Guard activation / Selective Service language (validates pre-execution reading)",
        "The reported window passing without action while diplomacy resumes (validates leverage-signaling reading)",
        "Iran HEU down-blend / retrieval-cooperation move that defuses the commando-retrieval rationale (validates leverage-signaling reading)",
        "On-record administration confirmation or denial of an operational decision (resolves)",
        "A signed final MOU text + timeline, or a visible collapse of the talks, that shows whether the May 25 'mandatory' Abraham Accords demand was a genuine new precondition or a rhetorical sweetener (resolves the goalpost-move-vs-coalition-building reading)"
      ],
      "current_assessment": "JUNE 4-5 — Still unresolved. The June 4 Israel-Lebanon conditional ceasefire framework is deescalatory at the ground rung (partially meeting the Lebanon condition behind Iran's June 1 talks suspension), though Hezbollah rejected it; the genuine-preparation reading is neither validated (the named deal-failure branch remains an air/strike package; Araghchi's 'full-scale resumption' threat is a conditional missile/air tripwire, not a US ground order) nor closed (no MOU signed by both leaders). The ground rung is unchanged (no new troop movements; no third ARG; no draft; USS Boxer ARG still INDOPACOM; Ford home). Noted, not endorsed. || JUNE 2-3 — Still unresolved, with deal-collapse risk up after Iran's June 1 suspension of talks over Israel's Lebanon offensive. The genuine-preparation reading is not validated (the administration's named deal-failure branch remains an air/strike package and Trump explicitly ruled out troops to Beirut) nor closed (no MOU has been signed by both Trump and Mojtaba Khamenei, and the suspension keeps the path open-ended). The ground rung is unchanged (no new troop movements; no third ARG; no draft; USS Boxer ARG still INDOPACOM; Ford home). Noted, not endorsed. || JUNE 1-2 — Still unresolved. The MOU stayed unsigned at the 'turn of the week,' with the gaps over Hormuz language, HEU disposition, and now an immediate $12B frozen-assets payment either bridgeable end-stage details or deal-killing divergences — open sources cannot tell which until the text is publicly signed by both Trump and Mojtaba Khamenei or the talks visibly collapse. The ground rung is unchanged (no new troop movements; no third ARG; no draft; USS Boxer ARG still INDOPACOM; Ford home), and the named deal-failure alternative remains an air/strike package. Noted, not endorsed. || STILL UNRESOLVABLE as of May 31, with the ground rung unchanged and the deal-close track stalled a second consecutive day one signature short of completion. After Friday May 29's no-decision Situation Room meeting, the White House reiterated that Trump will only sign a deal that 'satisfies his redlines,' the administration floated a possible Sunday May 31 announcement of the 60-day MOU that was not delivered, and Lara Trump voiced 'couple of hours' optimism — while Iran's Mohsen Rezaei publicly charged Trump with stalling via 'excessive demands' and 'betraying diplomacy.' This neither validates the genuine-preparation reading (the administration's named deal-failure branch remains an air/strike package — Hegseth at Shangri-La: the US is 'more than capable' of resuming strikes, 'our stockpiles are more than suited for that' — not a ground order) nor closes it (no MOU has been signed by both Trump and Mojtaba Khamenei, and the blockade is being tightened, not relaxed, with CENTCOM's tally up to ~115-116 vessels redirected and 5 disabled). The question stays live and unresolvable until either the MOU is publicly signed by both leaders or the talks visibly collapse. || STILL UNRESOLVABLE as of May 30, with the ground rung unchanged and the deal-close track stalled one signature short of completion. The May 29-30 cycle delivered the cleanest test yet of the deal-imminent reading and it came up short: Trump convened the 'final determination' Situation Room meeting he had promised and ended it after ~2 hours without a decision, a senior administration official telling the NYT the agreement was 'still close' but needed further debate over issues including the unfreezing of Iranian funds, while Iran's state outlets (Fars/Tasnim) said Trump's restated demands 'contradict the provisions of the agreement's text' and that the 'final text... has not yet been completed or approved.' This neither validates the genuine-preparation reading (the failure branch remains an air/strike package, not a ground order: the May 29 skirmishing was US drone shoot-downs + a Bandar Abbas ground-control-station strike, Iran's aircraft-downing claim near Bushehr was CENTCOM-denied; no new troop movements; USS Boxer ARG still INDOPACOM; USS Gerald R. Ford home; no third ARG; no draft) nor the deal-imminent reading (the leaders demonstrably remain apart on terms negotiators had reportedly closed). Oil's continued slide to its worst month since March 2020 (Brent -19%) is deal-near pricing, not war-resumption or ground-commitment pricing. Whether the no-decision reflects routine final-mile sequencing or a substantive leader-level gap cannot be determined from open sources until the MOU is publicly signed by both Trump and Mojtaba Khamenei or the talks visibly collapse. || PRIOR STILL UNRESOLVABLE as of May 29 but tilted marginally further toward the leverage-signaling / deal-close reading, with the ground rung unchanged. The May 28-29 cycle produced the most concrete deal-close artifact to date — a negotiator-level 60-day MOU (Axios, May 28) — yet it remains double-pending: Trump 'wants a couple of days to think about it,' Iran has not confirmed acceptance, and Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei has not signed off (ABC: 'negotiators believe they have a deal, but leaders haven't signed off'). Toward leverage-signaling / deal-imminent: the MOU text reportedly agreed at negotiator level; its negotiated Hormuz reopening (no tolls + Iranian mine-clearing within 30 days) as the explicit alternative to a Kharg/Hormuz ground seizure; oil's measured ~2% rebound (Brent ~$96.29 / WTI >$90) rather than a war-resumption spike. Toward stalled / not-actually-closed: the May 27 White House branding of an Iranian-state-media MOU draft as a 'complete fabrication' ('FACTS MATTER'); Trump's public line that Iran will not get sanctions relief in exchange for surrendering its HEU and 'we're not satisfied with it, but we will be'; the Iranian FM's insistence that nuclear issues are not part of current negotiations; persistent HEU-disposition, enrichment, and Lebanon-coverage sticking points; and continued pro-Israel hawk criticism. The whole picture stays AIR/STRIKE-PACKAGE at the failure branch, not a ground order: no new troop movements; USS Boxer ARG still under INDOPACOM; USS Gerald R. Ford home at Norfolk; USS Abraham Lincoln + USS George H.W. Bush CSGs + USS Tripoli ARG in the Arabian Sea; no third ARG; no BCT-scale Guard activation; no new Selective Service/draft action. Whether the negotiator-level MOU is one signature from completion or the leaders remain materially apart cannot be determined from open sources until the MOU is publicly signed by both Trump and Mojtaba Khamenei or the talks visibly collapse. || PRIOR GENUINELY MIXED as of May 27 and still unresolvable, with the ground rung unchanged after the first kinetic US-Iran exchange of the Project Freedom pause stayed in the AIR/MARITIME 'self-defense' register and did not break the ceasefire. The May 26-27 cycle pulls in two directions from the same documented record. Retaliation/stall-risk-up: the IRGC's 'flagrant violation' / 'legitimate and certain' right-of-response framing; Shekarchi's 'far more severe' / 'beyond the region' threat; Mojtaba Khamenei's 'US bases no longer safe' warning; Shamkhani's 'fantasy' dismissal of Trump's nuclear demands; Austria DSN report alleging an advanced Iranian nuclear-weapons program; analysts (CNN, Al Jazeera, Euronews) warning the strikes could derail talks. Toward leverage-signaling / deal-imminent: CENTCOM's 'ceasefire remains in place'; Trump's 'just a love tap' framing; the Iranian delegation (Araghchi + Qalibaf + Hemmati) remaining in Doha with Qatari mediation continuing; Fox News' '95% there' reporting from senior US officials; Trump's May 27 'close to finalizing' / 'strong inspections' framing; US officials' claim Mojtaba Khamenei has 'endorsed the broad template of the deal'; Iran's partial lifting of the 87-day internet blackout. The US response register stays AIR/MARITIME — two IRGC mine-laying boats destroyed + one SAM site near Bandar Abbas struck — NOT a ground order: no new troop movements; USS Boxer ARG still under INDOPACOM; USS Gerald R. Ford home at Norfolk; USS Abraham Lincoln + USS George H.W. Bush CSGs + USS Tripoli ARG in the Arabian Sea; no third ARG; no BCT-scale Guard activation; no Selective Service/draft language; Murkowski draft AUMF would BAN ground troops; the >15,000-troop CENTCOM blockade tally is maritime enforcement. Status held at 'no_ground_troops'; ground-invasion probability EASED at the ground rung with the interim-deal-vs-air-strike outcome still the live binary and retaliation/stall risk RE-ELEVATED. Noted, not endorsed. Resolves fully if the Doha track signs an MOU (validates leverage-signaling / deal track) or if Iran's threatened 'far more severe / beyond the region' retaliation triggers a US re-strike spiral that crosses into the ground rung (validates genuine-preparation). || PRIOR GENUINELY MIXED as of May 26 and still unresolvable, with the ground rung unchanged as the deal-track decelerates rather than closes. The May 25-26 cycle pulls in two directions from the same documented record. Toward leverage-signaling / deal-track: (a) Trump's 'time is on our side' / 'not to rush' framing and 'proceeding nicely' characterization; (b) Rubio's 'a deal could materialize today'; (c) the disclosed 60-day-MOU architecture (negotiated Hormuz reopening without tolls + Iranian mine-clearing, 'relief for performance,' US forces withdrawing only on a final deal); (d) the Munir/PM-China Beijing mediation crossover. Toward decelerating / impasse-persists: (i) Trump's NEW 'mandatory' Abraham Accords condition (Saudi/Qatar/Pakistan-Israel normalization) stacked onto an already-unsigned interim deal; (ii) sharp pro-Israel criticism of the MOU (Cruz 'a disastrous mistake'; Wicker 'a disaster'; Pompeo 'not remotely America First'); (iii) Iran's 'an agreement is not imminent' / 'shifting positions' framing; (iv) the unresolved Hormuz-management and ~$25B frozen-asset-sequencing disputes, with the blockade held 'in full force... until... signed.' The threatened operation remains AIR/STRIKE-PACKAGE / 'alternatives' register, NOT a ground-invasion order: NPR reported 'no major military posture changes' for May 25; USS Boxer ARG still under INDOPACOM; USS Gerald R. Ford home at Norfolk; no new troop movements; no third ARG; no BCT-scale Guard activation; no Selective Service/draft language; Murkowski draft AUMF would BAN ground troops. Status held at 'no_ground_troops'; ground-invasion probability EASED at the ground rung with the interim-deal-vs-air-strike outcome still the live binary. Noted, not endorsed. Resolves fully if the 60-day MOU is signed (validates leverage-signaling / deal track) or if the strike backstop is executed over the Hormuz/asset/Abraham-Accords conditions (validates genuine-preparation). || PRIOR GENUINELY MIXED as of May 25 and still unresolvable, with the ground rung further eased as Decision Sunday resolved toward a deliberate slow-walk rather than either an immediate signature or a re-strike. The May 24-25 cycle pulls in two directions from the same documented record. Toward leverage-signaling / deal-imminent: (a) Trump told his reps 'not to rush into a deal' and 'both sides must take their time and get it right,' framing talks as 'orderly and constructive' / 'time is on our side'; (b) the White House expects the 60-day MOU 'signed within days' pending Khamenei approval; (c) the disclosed deal architecture (Axios) — negotiated Hormuz reopening without tolls + Iranian mine-clearing, 'relief for performance,' US forces withdrawing only on a final deal — is structurally a settlement, not an invasion. Toward genuine-but-deferred-preparation / impasse-persists: (i) Trump holding the naval blockade 'in full force and effect until an agreement is reached, certified, and signed'; (ii) the senior US official's caveat that 'there are ways in which the deal can be undermined'; (iii) Iran's Tasnim insisting 'one or two clauses' persist and that no first-step asset release means 'no agreement,' plus IRGC-linked outlets insisting Hormuz stays 'under Iranian supervision.' The threatened operation remains AIR/STRIKE-PACKAGE register, NOT a ground-invasion order: USS Boxer ARG still under INDOPACOM, USS Gerald R. Ford home at Norfolk, no new troop movements, no third ARG, no BCT-scale Guard activation, no Selective Service/draft language; Murkowski draft AUMF would BAN ground troops. Status held at 'no_ground_troops'; ground-invasion probability EASED at the ground rung with the interim-deal-vs-air-strike outcome still the live binary. Noted, not endorsed. Resolves fully if the 60-day MOU is signed (validates leverage-signaling / deal track) or if the strike backstop is executed over the Hormuz/asset red lines (validates genuine-preparation). || PRIOR GENUINELY MIXED as of May 24 and still unresolvable, with the ground rung eased on a decision weekend whose live binary is an interim-deal signature versus an AIR strike. The May 23-24 cycle pulls in two directions from the same documented record. Toward leverage-signaling / deal-imminent: (a) Trump announced a deal is 'largely negotiated' with a framework to be 'announced shortly,' including reopening the Strait of Hormuz; (b) mediators (Pakistan + Qatar, with Egypt/Saudi/Turkey) converged on a 60-day ceasefire-extension framework whose first phase is a 14-clause MOU (Iran FM spox Baqaei), with the nuclear file DEFERRED to a separate 30-60-day track, a gradual Hormuz reopening without tolls, Iran committing only to DISCUSS diluting/transferring its ~440 kg HEU, and limited sanctions relief + phased asset release; (c) Rubio cited 'some progress' and said the US 'may have something to say' in the coming days; (d) Munir's meeting with Pezeshkian was 'highly productive'/'encouraging.' Toward genuine-but-deferred-preparation / impasse-persists: (i) Trump's 'solid 50/50' framing with 'hit them harder than they have ever been hit' as the alternative; (ii) Iran's Fars news agency dismissing the announcement as 'incomplete and inconsistent with reality' and insisting Hormuz stays 'under Iranian management'; (iii) the still-unresolved HEU/Hormuz red lines the interim MOU only punts. The threatened operation remains AIR/STRIKE-PACKAGE register, NOT a ground-invasion order: USS Boxer ARG still under INDOPACOM, USS Gerald R. Ford home at Norfolk, no third ARG, no BCT-scale Guard activation, no Selective Service/draft language; Murkowski draft AUMF would BAN ground troops; the CENTCOM 100-vessel milestone (>15,000 US troops) is maritime-blockade enforcement. Status held at 'no_ground_troops'; ground-invasion probability EASED at the ground rung with the air-strike-vs-interim-deal outcome the live binary. Noted, not endorsed. Resolves fully if the 60-day extension MOU is signed (validates leverage-signaling / deal track) or if the strike backstop is executed over the HEU/Hormuz red lines (validates genuine-preparation). || PRIOR GENUINELY MIXED as of May 23 and still unresolvable, with the ground rung eased but the negotiation's nuclear risk rising. The May 22-23 cycle pulls in two directions from the same documented record. Toward leverage-signaling / deal-imminent: (a) Pakistan Field Marshal Asim Munir arrived in Tehran (received by Interior Min Momeni) and a Qatari delegation joined the mediation toward a 'letter of intent' to end the war + a 30-day broader-deal framework; (b) Saudi network Al-Arabiya leaked an UNCONFIRMED 9-point 'final draft' interim deal — immediate ceasefire on all fronts + guaranteed Hormuz freedom of navigation + gradual US sanctions relief, with the nuclear question PUNTED to a 7-day/30-day track — possibly announceable 'within hours'; (c) Rubio cited 'good signs' / 'slight progress'; (d) oil posted a weekly loss (Brent ~$103.82, down ~5% on the week), pricing a deal-close move. Toward genuine-but-deferred-preparation / impasse-persists: (i) Iran's Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei ordered the enriched-uranium stockpile to remain in Iran, rejecting Trump's central HEU